<!-- ============================================================ 1 -->
<h1 id="what">1. What polyir is</h1>

<p><strong>polyir</strong> is a small C-like programming language. A program written in it is compiled to
<strong>LLVM IR</strong>, the low-level, typed instruction format that the Clang C compiler and many other compilers
produce before they emit machine code. LLVM IR gives full control (explicit blocks, no hidden memory allocator) but
writing it by hand is tedious: every value needs a numbered register and every jump needs a labelled block. polyir keeps
that control and removes the tedium. It is both a practical way to write fast low-level code and a readable way to see
what a compiler produces.</p>

<p>polyir is written in <strong>Nina</strong>, the language of the surrounding repository, and builds on Nina's standard
<code>Llvm</code> builder library. The compiler needs no C++ of its own.</p>

<h2>Three things polyir can produce from one program</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Output</th><th>Command</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
  <tr><td>A native program or library</td><td><code>polyir app.poly --out app</code></td><td>Ordinary CPU programs. <code>--shared</code> and <code>--static-lib</code> make libraries.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>LLVM IR text</td><td><code>polyir app.poly --emit-llvm</code></td><td>Reading or testing what the compiler generated. <code>--check</code> only verifies.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Metal source (Apple GPUs)</td><td><code>polyir app.poly --emit-msl</code></td><td>The <code>kernel func</code>s of the program, as Metal Shading Language.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vulkan shaders (any Vulkan GPU)</td><td><code>polyir app.poly --emit-glsl</code></td><td>The same kernels as GLSL compute shaders, compiled to SPIR-V by <code>glslangValidator</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Run immediately (JIT)</td><td><code>polyir app.poly --jit</code></td><td>Quick experiments. Programs that use the GPU host runtime refuse this (see <a href="#native">section 28</a>).</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The GPU outputs are the unusual part. Most of this guide is about how a function written once can run as a CPU
function, a Metal kernel and a Vulkan kernel, and how the results were checked to agree.</p>

<h2>A first program</h2>
<pre><code>struct Point {
    x: double
    y: double
}

func distanceSquared(a: Point, b: Point) -> double {
    let dx: double = a.x - b.x
    let dy: double = a.y - b.y
    return dx * dx + dy * dy
}</code></pre>

<p>Every <code>let</code>, <code>var</code>, parameter, field and return type is written with its type. That is a
deliberate choice: the compiler only has to <em>infer</em> the types of literals, operators and calls, which keeps the
type checker small (section 4).</p>

<h2 id="why">Why polyir: the argument</h2>
<p>polyir is the half of a compiler that every language needs and no language wants to write: the part after the front
end has understood the program. A language is a front end plus N back ends plus a runtime plus debugging support plus
interop, and only the front end is the language. Everything else is the same work for every language, done badly N
times because nobody has the budget to do it well once. The usual escapes are to target one platform (and be a JVM
language, or a JavaScript language), to transpile to C (and lose the type system, the debugger and the platform's
libraries), or to target LLVM directly (and re-invent strings, collections, closures, exceptions and coroutines in IR).</p>
<p>polyir's claim is that the layer between <em>what the program means</em> and <em>what the target wants</em> can be
shared, provided it is typed enough to keep the high-level structure and portable enough that the structure can be
spelled in any target. A front end hands polyir a program once &mdash; as <code>.poly</code> text, as HIR JSON, or by building
the typed HIR in-process with libpolyir &mdash; and gets from that one description: native code, a JIT'd function and
WebAssembly through LLVM; Java, Kotlin, JavaScript, TypeScript, Swift, C++, Python, Rust, Objective-C and C as source
the target's own tools build; Metal, Vulkan GLSL and SPIR-V for the kernel subset; and a debugger that stops in the
<em>front end's</em> file and line. The Nina compiler is the proof: when its six legacy generators were deleted, nothing
was lost &mdash; every target now goes through one lowering and the polyir back ends, the compiler bootstraps through
them, and the same corpus produces the same output on every language. Where the lowering was wrong, it was wrong once.</p>
<p>Two implementations check each other: the reference compiler is written in Nina, the C++ library is its port and must
print byte-identical HIR for every program, and a harness compiles every test program through all ten source back ends
and compares their output with the LLVM route. The IR's meaning is what two implementations and eleven back ends agree
on, not what one implementation happens to do.</p>

<h2>What it lets a language do</h2>
<p>The typed HIR is not three-address code with types bolted on. It carries the constructs that high-level languages are
made of and that are painful to re-derive per target, so a front end does not have to choose between expressive features
and many targets. Nina's experience, feature by feature:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Nina feature</th><th>In polyir</th><th>What the back ends then do</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> with <code>fail</code>, <code>else</code>, auto-propagation</td><td><code>either&lt;T, E&gt;</code></td><td>a tagged struct in C, a sealed class in Kotlin, a tuple in Python &mdash; the same checks</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>Optional&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>if (opt)</code> narrowing</td><td><code>variant</code> with a none case</td><td>nullable where the target has it, a box where it does not</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>match</code> on payload enums, list patterns</td><td><code>match</code> and <code>switch</code>: a <code>Select</code> of keys, or an <code>if</code> chain over tags, unwraps and lengths</td><td><code>switch</code>, <code>when</code> or <code>match</code> where the target has it, <code>if</code> chains elsewhere</td></tr>
  <tr><td>lambdas, bound methods, <code>f of g</code></td><td>closures with captures</td><td>real closures everywhere; boxing decided per target</td></tr>
  <tr><td>generators, <code>Observable</code>, <code>Task</code>/<code>await</code></td><td>coroutines, a struct-based observable model</td><td><code>llvm.coro.*</code> natively; virtual threads, generators, async in the source languages</td></tr>
  <tr><td>interfaces, generic dispatch</td><td><code>interface</code> + vtables, monomorphized <code>struct&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>interfaces natively; erased where needed</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>with { &hellip; }</code> copies, <code>===</code> identity</td><td><code>with</code>, structural <code>==</code>, hashing</td><td>one definition of equality per type, not per language</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>import java "&hellip;"</code></td><td>host types, members and bridges</td><td><code>java.util.ArrayList</code> where it is Java, a bridge elsewhere</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The power is in composition: a Nina program that uses coroutines inside a closure that returns a <code>Result</code> of an
interface value debugs in Nina lines on macOS natively, runs as JavaScript in a browser, and compiles as C for a
microcontroller, with one lowering and no per-target runtime written by the language author.</p>

<h2 id="java-native">Mapping down and up: Java to native, with polyir in the middle</h2>
<p><strong>Down</strong> (high to low) is a contract, not a translation: the front end expresses its constructs in HIR terms
(sums, closures, containers, host calls) and declares what it needs from the host; polyir owes a spelling on each
target, written down construct by construct in the per-target mapping documents. A target that cannot do something
says so at the point of use (<code>runtime function X is not supported yet in rust</code>), never silently. Unstructured
control flow is restructured for targets without <code>goto</code> (a relooper), manual <code>free</code> is a hint
garbage-collected targets ignore, raw pointers and SIMD are declined where the target has none.</p>
<p>Imagine wanting ordinary Java to compile to a native executable &mdash; no JVM, no JIT warm-up, a binary a C program
could link. With polyir the job splits into a front end that only has to understand Java, and a back end that exists:</p>
<pre><code>Foo.java ──Java front end──▶ polyir HIR ──polyir──▶ LLVM IR ──clang──▶ native binary (or wasm, or Swift/Rust/C source)
           classes, generics,     structs + interfaces + vtables,          DWARF says Foo.java:12
           exceptions, lambdas    either/variant, closures, try/catch,
           String, ArrayList      string, array/mutarray, extern (JNI)</code></pre>
<p>What the front end maps: a class with fields becomes a reference <code>struct</code> and its methods struct methods;
<code>interface</code>/<code>implements</code> become polyir <code>interface</code> and <code>: I</code>, so an interface call is
the vtable call the LLVM back end already writes; <code>extends</code> flattens into the struct (the superclass's fields
first, overridden methods rebound in the table); generics are monomorphized per use like <code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;</code>;
<code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>/<code>finally</code> are <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>/<code>cleanup</code>; lambdas are closures;
<code>String</code> is <code>string</code>, <code>ArrayList</code>/<code>HashMap</code> are <code>mutarray</code>/<code>mutmap</code> (or stay
the JDK's own classes as host types when the output is Java again); <code>native</code> methods are <code>extern func</code>;
<code>synchronized</code> and <code>volatile</code> are atomics and a lock the front end declares. Positions go in as spans,
so the binary debugs in <code>Foo.java</code> lines and a crash prints Java method names.</p>
<p>What polyir contributes that the front end does not write: the object layout and every operation on it on each
target, structural equality and hashing, the release analysis (a Java object that never escapes is freed without a
collector &mdash; the escape analysis a JIT does at run time, done once at compile time), exceptions on LLVM, interfaces and
dispatch, closures, DWARF, wasm, and &mdash; because the HIR is the same &mdash; the same Java program written back as C, Rust,
Swift or Python, which no Java tool chain offers.</p>
<p><strong>The same exercise for other languages.</strong> Each would map most of itself onto what the HIR has, and each would ask for something specific:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Front end</th><th>Maps naturally to polyir</th><th>Would ask the IR for</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Kotlin</td><td>classes and <code>data class</code> → reference structs with structural <code>==</code>/hash and <code>copy</code> (<code>with</code>); sealed classes and <code>when</code> → <code>variant</code> + tags; <code>suspend</code> functions and <code>Flow</code> → coroutines; nullable <code>T?</code> → a <code>variant</code> with none; extension functions → ordinary functions; lambdas with receivers → closures</td><td>coroutine dispatchers and structured concurrency (the IR has coroutines, not a scheduler); <code>inline</code>/<code>reified</code> need the front end to monomorphize; delegation (<code>by</code>) is sugar the front end expands; the same GC and JDK questions as Java</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Swift</td><td>value structs and enums with payloads → value structs and <code>variant</code>; protocols → <code>interface</code>; <code>throws</code> → <code>either</code>/exceptions; optionals → <code>variant</code>; closures → closures; <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> → coroutines</td><td>ARC semantics (copy-on-write arrays, <code>weak</code>/<code>unowned</code>) against polyir&#x27;s release analysis; actors and the executor model; generic protocols with associated types need monomorphization by the front end</td></tr>
  <tr><td>TypeScript / JavaScript</td><td>classes → reference structs, interfaces → <code>interface</code>, union types → <code>variant</code>, <code>async</code> → coroutines, closures → closures, <code>Map</code>/arrays → <code>mutmap</code>/<code>mutarray</code>, numbers → <code>double</code></td><td>a dynamic object model (<code>any</code>, prototypes, property bags, <code>typeof</code>) has no HIR type &mdash; the front end needs a boxed dynamic value and a runtime; the number model (doubles, 53-bit integers) must be emulated on integer targets; the event loop and promises are a scheduler the IR does not have</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Python</td><td>functions, classes (as reference structs with a fixed field set), lists/dicts/sets → containers, generators → coroutines, exceptions → <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code></td><td>everything dynamic: duck typing, <code>getattr</code>, dynamic dispatch by name, big integers, the global interpreter model &mdash; a Python front end is a static subset (what Cython or mypyc compile) plus a boxed value for the rest; polyir has no big integer or dynamic value type yet</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rust</td><td>structs, enums → <code>variant</code>, traits → <code>interface</code> (static dispatch monomorphized by the front end), <code>Result</code>/<code>Option</code> → <code>either</code>/<code>variant</code>, closures → closures, <code>Box</code>/<code>Vec</code>/<code>HashMap</code> → references/containers</td><td>ownership and borrowing are checked by the front end and then DROPPED: polyir&#x27;s release analysis frees what does not escape, but <code>Drop</code> order, moves and lifetimes are not expressible, so destructors with side effects need explicit calls; no <code>unsafe</code> pointer model beyond <code>ptr&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>C</td><td>functions, structs, pointers → <code>ptr&lt;T&gt;</code>, arrays → <code>T[N]</code>, <code>goto</code> → labels; this is the closest fit and the direction the lifter already goes (compiled C → LLVM IR → lifted)</td><td>unions, bit fields, variadic functions, <code>setjmp</code>, inline assembly and the preprocessor are outside the IR; integer promotion rules and undefined behaviour must be chosen by the front end since polyir does not define them either</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The pattern is the same every time: types, control flow, sums, closures, coroutines, exceptions and collections are already there; what each language brings that the IR lacks is its <em>runtime model</em> (a collector, a scheduler, a dynamic value) and its <em>undefined corners</em> (numeric rules, string encoding, destructor order). Those are the items in the gap list below, which is why it is ordered as it is.</p>
<p><strong>Up</strong> (low to high): <code>polyir-lift</code> reads LLVM IR text or a WebAssembly binary and recovers a program
&mdash; functions, locals, loops and conditionals, structs, calls &mdash; as an IR the same back ends write as Java, C++, Kotlin,
Python, JavaScript or TypeScript. A module polyir itself compiled carries its <code>polyir.meta</code> description (names,
parameter names, struct fields, interfaces, positions), exactly what machine code loses, so a round trip keeps the
names and shapes a decompiler would have to guess. Down lets a new language start with eleven targets; up lets
existing compiled code enter the same world. Together they make polyir a place code moves <em>through</em>, not only a
place it goes to: Nina to C, C to Java, Java's own classes into a program that then compiles to Swift.</p>
<p><strong>The mirror case: C, compiled to LLVM IR, lifted into Java.</strong> Take a C library &mdash; a checksum and a small parser &mdash;
compiled by clang to LLVM IR. <code>polyir-lift</code> reads the <code>.ll</code> text (no libLLVM, no clang at lift time) and the Java back end writes a class:</p>
<pre><code>crc.c ──clang -O0 -emit-llvm──▶ crc.ll ──polyir-lift──▶ IR ──Java back end──▶ Crc.java   (or Kotlin, C++, Python, JS, TS)
         functions, locals,          allocas → locals, GEP patterns → fields,     static methods on long/double,
         loops, structs, calls       dominators + natural loops → while/if,        structs as classes, arrays as arrays,
                                     extern calls → declared methods               names from polyir.meta when present</code></pre>
<p>What the lift recovers: every <code>alloca</code> becomes a local; the control-flow graph is restructured into <code>while</code>,
<code>if</code>, <code>break</code>/<code>continue</code> by dominator and natural-loop analysis, so the Java reads as a loop, not as a state
machine over basic blocks; <code>getelementptr</code> chains over a known struct type become field access; calls stay calls; a
function the IR only declares becomes a method the Java side must provide. When the IR was produced by polyir itself, the
<code>polyir.meta</code> section gives back the function and parameter names, the struct fields and their types, <code>var</code>
fields and interfaces &mdash; the lifted Java has the original names instead of <code>%3</code> and <code>field_2</code>.</p>
<p>What Java gains: C code it could only reach through JNI becomes ordinary Java it can read, step through, unit-test and
refactor; the same lift writes Kotlin for an Android project or TypeScript for a browser; and the lifted program,
re-entering polyir as a front end's input, compiles back down to native or wasm &mdash; a round trip that keeps the
program's shape. The gap for this direction: the lifter is reliable on <code>-O0</code>-shaped IR (one <code>alloca</code> per
local); optimized IR writes loops with phi nodes that come back as guard flags; raw memory has no Java spelling &mdash; a
<code>char*</code> string, <code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code> and pointer arithmetic become a byte array plus an index, correct but not
idiomatic; function pointers become an interface the lifter invents; unions, bit fields, <code>setjmp</code> and inline assembly
stop it; and without <code>polyir.meta</code> every name is a guess. The lifted subset and a canonical round-trip form are the
two definitions the lifter still owes.</p>


<h2 id="gap">The gap, candidly</h2>
<p>polyir is a working system and an incomplete IR. What is missing, in the order it hurts:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The target contract is in code, not written down.</strong> What a back end must provide (memory, strings, pointers, exceptions, coroutines, atomics, control flow) is implicit in the lowering; <code>docs/portability.md</code> proposes a capability table the compiler reads so a program is rejected up front by capability name.</li>
  <li><strong>The runtime object model lives in the LLVM lowering.</strong> The source back ends bind collection operations to their own libraries; the byte layout of list and map headers the LLVM route uses is not part of the IR's specification.</li>
  <li><strong>Undefined numeric and string semantics.</strong> Integer overflow, division by zero, out-of-range shifts and indexing are whatever the target does; strings are UTF-8 bytes natively and the host's strings elsewhere, so non-ASCII equality, hashing and invalid bytes differ per target.</li>
  <li><strong>A flat namespace.</strong> <code>import</code> merges files; front ends with modules mangle names themselves; separate compilation and linking of polyir modules do not exist yet.</li>
  <li><strong>The lifter recovers <code>-O0</code>-shaped code.</strong> Loops written with phis, closures, strings and coroutines come back as pointers and calls; the lifted subset and a canonical round-trip form are not defined.</li>
  <li><strong>Debug information is one-way for most front ends.</strong> The text route and the HIR carry positions, but the C++ library has no <code>meta</code> field and no builder-side <code>at(span)</code>, and Nina does not yet hand statement spans over, so a Nina program does not debug in <code>.nina</code> lines.</li>
  <li><strong>For the Java case specifically:</strong> Java assumes a collector and polyir's release analysis frees only what provably does not escape (cycles and shared long-lived graphs need a collector the front end brings, or reference counting polyir lacks); threads, monitors and the memory model are not in the IR; reflection, class loading and <code>instanceof</code> on arbitrary hierarchies need runtime type information polyir keeps only as interface tables; and the JDK itself must be bound as host types or re-implemented over polyir's collections and strings.</li>
  <li><strong>Exceptions and coroutines are native-first.</strong> Both compile on LLVM; the source back ends carry them where the language has them and decline elsewhere; wasm has no exception handling yet.</li>
</ol>
<p>None of these is a flaw in the idea; each is a piece of specification or a bounded piece of work. The idea &mdash; one
typed, high-level, portable IR that front ends target and back ends read, with debugging and host interop as first-class
parts of it &mdash; is what the rest of the Nina project is now built on.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ 2 -->
<h1 id="tour">2. A tour of the language</h1>

<p>This is a map, not a manual. The README in the repository documents every feature and its limits; here is enough to
read the examples in the rest of the guide.</p>

<h2>Values and variables</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>let x: i64 = 5</code> &mdash; a name bound to a value. It can never be reassigned, only shadowed.</li>
  <li><code>var total: i64 = 0</code> &mdash; a real mutable local. Reassign it with <code>total = total + 1</code> from anywhere in scope.</li>
  <li>Types: <code>i1 i8 i16 i32 i64</code> (integers by bit width), <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>string</code>, named <code>struct</code>s, fixed arrays <code>T[N]</code>, pointers <code>ptr&lt;T&gt;</code>, and SIMD vectors <code>vec&lt;float, 4&gt;</code>.</li>
  <li>Integer literals are 64-bit by default. This matters later: on a GPU, mixing 64-bit and 32-bit integers needs care (section 8).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Control flow</h2>
<pre><code>func sumEven(n: i32) -> i32 {
    var acc: i32 = 0
    for (i = 0; i &lt; n; i = i + 1) {      // i is carried from one round to the next
        if (i % 2 == 0) { acc = acc + i }
    }
    return acc
}

func gcd(a0: i64, b0: i64) -> i64 {      // labels are blocks that take arguments
    goto loop(a0, b0)
label loop(a: i64, b: i64):
    branch b == 0 ? done(a) : body(a, b)
label body(a: i64, b: i64):
    let r: i64 = a % b
    goto loop(b, r)
label done(result: i64):
    return result
}</code></pre>
<p>A <code>label</code> with parameters is how polyir expresses what LLVM calls a <em>phi node</em>: each jump to the
label supplies the values the label's parameters take. <code>if</code> and <code>for</code> are shorthand for the same
mechanism. <code>switch</code> maps to LLVM's own <code>switch</code> instruction.</p>
<p><code>if (c) a else b</code> is also a value: <code>a</code> when <code>c</code> holds, else <code>b</code>. Both branches
have one type (where a type is expected, as in <code>let s: Shape = if (big) Square(side: 3) else Rect(w: 1, h: 2)</code>,
each branch is that type). Only the branch taken is worked out. What follows <code>else</code> reaches as far as an
expression goes, so <code>if (c) 1 else 2 + 3</code> is <code>2 + 3</code> when <code>c</code> fails, and
<code>else if</code> chains: <code>return if (n &lt; 0) "negative" else if (n == 0) "zero" else "positive"</code>. The checker
works the value out just before its statement, so a loop's test or step cannot hold one.</p>

<h3>Matching</h3>
<p><code>match</code> picks a value by what a value is. Each arm is <code>pattern =&gt; value</code>, tried in order, and
<code>else =&gt;</code> takes what no arm does. A pattern is a literal (alternatives joined by <code>|</code>),
<code>_</code>, a name, <code>Some(n)</code> or <code>None</code>, <code>Ok(v)</code> or <code>Err(e)</code>,
<code>variant(N, x)</code>, or a list: <code>[]</code>, <code>[x]</code>, <code>[first, ...rest]</code>. A name binds the
part it matches, and <code>if</code> after a pattern is a <em>guard</em> on what it bound. Any other expression in an
arm is a <em>condition</em>, such as <code>path.endsWith(".poly")</code> or <code>n &lt; 0</code>. A <code>match</code> with no
subject takes only conditions. A value <code>match</code> needs an <code>else</code> unless its arms cover every case: both flags,
<code>Some</code> and <code>None</code>, <code>Ok</code> and <code>Err</code>, every case of a variant, or lists of every
length. The same arms work as a statement, each giving a block, and <code>switch</code> with block arms is that statement.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:examples/match.poly:sizeOf+gradeOf+kindOf+shown+said+report}}</code></pre>
<p>The checker lowers a <code>match</code> to a <em>switch</em> (the HIR's <code>Select</code>) when every arm is exact keys
or a sum's case, and to an <code>if</code> chain otherwise. A guard reads the part it tests (<code>found.unwrap() &gt; 100</code>),
and an arm binds a name only when its block reads it. A subject that is not a plain name is worked out once, into a hidden
<code>let</code>. Written back (<code>--emit-poly</code>) or translated, the program still reads like the match. The VM,
LLVM and wasm run a <code>Select</code> as its <code>if</code> chain. A page writes it as the target's own
<code>switch</code>, <code>when</code> or <code>match</code> where the keys allow, and as the <code>if</code> chain where they do
not. <code>...rest</code> is the built-in Lists library's <code>drop</code>, beside <code>first</code> and <code>last</code>.</p>

<h2>Data structures and abstractions</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>In one sentence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Structs</td><td>Passed by value; fields are read with one <code>extractvalue</code>. Methods are declared inside the struct and called as <code>p.dist()</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Containers</td><td><code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;</code> (growable), <code>array</code>, <code>grid</code>, <code>tensor</code>, <code>sparse</code>, <code>mutmap&lt;K,V&gt;</code>. A <code>mutarray</code>, <code>sparse</code>, <code>mutmap</code>, <code>grid</code> or <code>tensor</code> is a <em>reference</em>; <a href="#memory">Memory</a> lists where every type lives.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interfaces</td><td><code>interface Shape { ... }</code> and <code>struct Square : Shape</code>: one function works on any struct that implements the interface, and a call such as <code>s.area()</code> picks the right method while the program runs, through a table of functions (a <em>vtable</em>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Generics</td><td><code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>func pick&lt;T&gt;(a: T, b: T) -&gt; T</code> are <em>monomorphized</em>: one real struct or function per set of types used, like C++ templates. A call takes its types from its arguments: <code>pick(1, 2)</code> calls <code>pick_i64</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Closures</td><td><code>closure(x: i64) -&gt; i64 { ... }</code> capturing variables by reference; stored as a function pointer plus an environment.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sum types</td><td><code>variant&lt;A,B&gt;</code> and <code>either&lt;Ok,Err&gt;</code>, one universal tagged shape.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Exceptions</td><td><code>try { } catch (e) { }</code> and <code>throw</code>, built on <code>setjmp</code>/<code>longjmp</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Coroutines</td><td><code>coro func</code> with <code>yield</code>, built on LLVM's real <code>llvm.coro.*</code> intrinsics (native builds only).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Atomics</td><td>Load, store, read-modify-write and compare-exchange on a <code>ptr&lt;T&gt;</code>, plus <code>fence</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>C interop</td><td><code>extern func puts(s: string) -&gt; i32</code> declares a C function; the native build links it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Modules</td><td><code>module Geometry { ... }</code> groups declarations, named from outside as <code>Geometry.Point</code>; <code>internal</code> keeps one for the code around it (<a href="#modules">Modules</a>). <code>import "other.poly"</code> merges a file (like C's <code>#include</code>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>GPU kernels</strong></td><td><code>kernel func</code>: the subject of sections 5 to 9.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="modules">Modules and <code>internal</code></h2>
<p>A module groups declarations under a name. Inside it they are named as written; outside, through the module. Modules
nest, and a name is looked for in the code's own module first, then in each module around it, then at the top level.</p>
<pre><code>module Geometry {
    struct Point(x: i64, y: i64) {
        func manhattan() -&gt; i64 { return this.absolute(this.x) + this.absolute(this.y) }
        internal func absolute(n: i64) -&gt; i64 { return if (n &lt; 0) 0 - n else n }
    }

    internal func clamp(n: i64, low: i64, high: i64) -&gt; i64 {
        return if (n &lt; low) low else if (n &gt; high) high else n
    }

    func distance(a: Point, b: Point) -&gt; i64 {
        let step: Point = Point(x: a.x - b.x, y: a.y - b.y)
        return clamp(step.manhattan(), 0, 100)
    }
}

func main() -&gt; void {
    let a: Geometry.Point = Geometry.Point(x: 1, y: 2)
    print(str(Geometry.distance(a, Geometry.Point(x: 4, y: -2))))    // 7
}</code></pre>
<p>Everything is public unless it is marked <code>internal</code>. An internal function, struct or interface is for the code
inside its module (and the modules inside that one); at the top level, for the code in its own file. An internal method is
for its struct's own methods. Any other use is refused with who may use it:
<code>Geometry.clamp is internal: only code inside module Geometry can use it</code>. A module is not a value:
<code>let g = Geometry</code> is refused, and the message says to name one of its declarations.</p>
<p>In the HIR a declaration is named by its path, <code>Geometry.Point</code>, and records its module and its
<code>internal</code> mark, so a program built from HIR JSON or <code>.pbc</code> prints back as the same <code>.poly</code>
(<code>polyir-check F.poly --emit-poly</code>). <code>examples/modules.poly</code> shows modules, <code>internal</code>,
<code>if</code> values and generic functions together.</p>

<h2 id="builder">Structs inside structs, defaults, annotations and <code>@Builder</code></h2>
<p>A struct can hold structs and <code>static func</code>s of its own. They are named through it, <code>Shape.Size</code> and
<code>Shape.square()</code>, and the struct's own code names them bare. The HIR keeps that nesting (a nested struct records its
<code>outer</code> struct), so a translation can write it as the target's own nested classes.</p>
<pre><code>@Builder
struct Shape(side: i64, name: string = "shape", tags: mutarray&lt;string&gt;) {
    static func square(side: i64) -&gt; Shape {
        return Shape.builder().side(side).name("square").build()
    }

    func size() -&gt; Size { return Size(area: this.side * this.side) }

    struct Size(area: i64)
}

func main() -&gt; void {
    let s: Shape = Shape.builder().side(2).build()     // name is "shape", tags is empty
    print(str(s.size().area))                          // 4
}</code></pre>
<p>A field may have a default, <code>name: string = "shape"</code>: a literal of the field's type, which a struct literal that leaves
the field out takes. An annotation, <code>@Name</code> or <code>@Name(key: literal, ...)</code>, goes before a struct, an interface, a
function or a method. The HIR, its JSON and <code>.pbc</code> keep it for tools and back ends to read.</p>
<p><code>@Builder</code> also gives the struct a nested <code>Builder</code> with one setter per field, a <code>build()</code>, and the static
<code>builder()</code> that starts one. A field is required unless it has a default, holds a container (which starts empty) or is an
<code>optional</code> (which starts as <code>None</code>). A chain that visibly skips a required field is refused when the program is
checked: <code>Shape.builder().build()</code> gives <code>Shape.build: side is not set</code>. A builder passed around and filled in
elsewhere is checked when <code>build()</code> runs. A missing field then stops the program through <code>fail(message)</code>, which
prints <code>trap: </code> and the message and ends with status 1. <code>examples/builder.poly</code> shows all of it.</p>

<h2>Containers are references</h2>
<p>A <code>mutarray</code> (growable list), a <code>sparse</code> and a <code>mutmap</code> are <em>references</em>. The name holds a pointer to a small header on the heap (a mutarray's header is its data pointer, its length and its capacity), not the container itself. Copying the name, passing it to a function, putting it in a struct or capturing it in a closure copies the <em>pointer</em>, so all of those are names for the <em>same</em> container, and a <code>push</code> or a write through any one of them is seen through all of them: the way a list behaves in Python or Java. <code>a + b</code> is how to make a new mutarray holding both.</p>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/containers.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @containers. Three names for one mutarray. A <code>push</code> through <code>b</code> changes the one header (its length, and its data pointer when the elements have to move), so <code>a</code> and <code>bag.items</code> see it too.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>That is what lets a function fill a list for its caller:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:examples/mutarray.poly:fillSquares+squaresThroughAHelper}}</code></pre>
<p><code>squaresThroughAHelper(5)</code> returns <code>5016</code>: five elements, and the last one is 16. Because a push changes the mutarray and not the name, the name can be a <code>let</code>, and the lint says so for a <code>var</code> that is only pushed to. A <code>sparse</code> or <code>mutmap</code> written inside a function works the same way (<code>hm[key] = value</code> in a helper fills the caller's map).</p>
<div class="note"><strong>History.</strong> The first versions of polyir copied the header whenever a mutarray was passed to another function. A push inside that function was lost to the caller, and a push that had to move the elements left the caller pointing at memory that had been freed. The compiler hid part of the problem by re-binding the variable after a bare <code>push</code> statement; that trick is gone. Twenty-two checks in <code>core/tests/programs/ContainerTests.poly</code> cover the sharing (a callee's push, a hundred pushes that move the data, two names, a struct field, a closure, a returned mutarray, a list of lists, a mutarray inside an <code>either</code>, a <code>sparse</code> and a <code>mutmap</code> written by a helper, a <code>mutmap</code> of lists).</div>

<h2 id="collections">Collections: <code>array</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>set</code> and their changeable forms</h2>
<p>Lists, maps and sets each come in two kinds. The plain kind cannot be changed once it is made; the <code>mut</code> kind changes in place.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Immutable</th><th>Changeable</th><th>Made with</th><th>Changed with</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>array&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>array&lt;T&gt;()</code>, <code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;()</code></td><td><code>a.with(x)</code> gives a new array; <code>push(m, x)</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>map&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td><td><code>mutmap&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td><td><code>map&lt;K,V&gt;()</code>, <code>mutmap&lt;K,V&gt;(capacity)</code></td><td><code>m.with(k, v)</code> gives a new map; <code>m[k] = v</code>, <code>m.remove(k)</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>set&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>mutset&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>set&lt;T&gt;()</code>, <code>mutset&lt;T&gt;(capacity)</code></td><td><code>s.with(x)</code> gives a new set; <code>s.add(x)</code>, <code>s.remove(x)</code></td></tr>
</table>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reading</strong> is the same for both kinds: <code>len(x)</code>, <code>x[i]</code> and <code>m[k]</code> (a missing key reads as zero, so ask <code>m.has(k)</code> or <code>s.has(x)</code> to tell absent from zero), <code>==</code>, <code>x.copy()</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Looping.</strong> <code>for (x in xs)</code> walks a list in order. <code>for (k in m)</code> walks a map's keys and <code>for (x in s)</code> a set's members, in the table's own order, which can differ from one target to another (the keys are the same on all of them); read a value with <code>m[k]</code>. The front end writes each as the counting loop it means: over a list's indices, or over a table's slots, taking the key of each slot that holds an entry.</li>
<li><strong>Removing.</strong> <code>m.remove(k)</code> takes the entry for <code>k</code> out of a <code>mutmap</code>, and <code>s.remove(x)</code> takes <code>x</code> out of a <code>mutset</code>. Each gives a <code>bool</code>: whether it was there. On a <code>map</code> or <code>set</code> it is an error that says to <code>thaw()</code> it first.</li>
<li><strong>A list literal takes its element type from where it goes.</strong> In <code>let shapes: array&lt;Shape&gt; = [Square(side: 2.0), Circle(r: 1.0)]</code> each element is checked as a <code>Shape</code>, so both structs are boxed into the interface. An argument of a declared function and a <code>return</code> do the same, with the parameter's and the result's type.</li>
<li><strong>Changing an unchangeable collection is an error</strong> that says what to do: <code>push</code> or an index assignment on an <code>array</code> or <code>map</code> says to use <code>with(...)</code> or <code>thaw()</code>; <code>with</code> on a <code>mutarray</code> says to use <code>push</code>.</li>
<li><strong><code>freeze()</code> and <code>thaw()</code></strong> turn one kind into the other. Both copy, so a frozen collection is never changed by a later <code>push</code> to the one it came from.</li>
<li><strong><code>with</code> copies.</strong> Building n elements with <code>with</code> costs O(n&sup2;); build with a <code>mutarray</code>, <code>mutmap</code> or <code>mutset</code> and <code>freeze()</code> when that matters. A <code>var</code> that is reassigned (<code>a = a.with(i)</code>) frees each older version as it goes, so such a loop runs in constant memory.</li>
<li><strong>Keys.</strong> A map key or set member must keep the same hash while it is in the table, so it is a number, a string, or an immutable value made only of those (an immutable struct, an <code>array</code>, <code>map</code> or <code>set</code> of keys, an <code>either</code> or <code>variant</code> of keys). A float, closure, pointer, mutable collection or struct with a <code>var</code> field is rejected. Keys are compared with <code>==</code> and hashed field by field; a struct may define <code>func hash() -&gt; i64</code> and <code>func equals(other: T) -&gt; i1</code> to decide for itself.</li>
<li>A <code>mutmap</code> or <code>mutset</code> starts at the capacity given at construction and doubles when three quarters full; a <code>map</code> or <code>set</code> grows by <code>with</code>.</li>
<li>The fixed-size sibling of <code>T[N]</code> is now <code>buffer&lt;T,S&gt;</code>. <code>darray</code> and <code>hashmap</code> are gone (use <code>mutarray</code> and <code>mutmap</code>), and <code>streq</code> is <code>==</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:examples/collections.poly}}</code></pre>

<h2 id="debug">Debug and meta information</h2>
<p><code>polyir --meta</code> and <code>polyir --debug</code> put information about the program in the compiled module: where each function and statement came from, the structs, and <code>meta</code> notes the author wrote. <code>--debug</code> adds DWARF so a debugger can break on a function or a line. There is <em>one</em> description of the program, independent of any target; each output carries what it can.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Output</th><th><code>--meta</code></th><th><code>--debug</code> adds</th></tr>
  <tr><td>LLVM IR, native code</td><td>named metadata <code>!polyir.meta</code>, <code>!polyir.function</code>; the JSON as the constant <code>@polyir.debug</code></td><td>DWARF: a compile unit, a file per source, a subprogram per function, a location for every statement</td></tr>
  <tr><td>wasm</td><td>a custom section named <code>polyir.meta</code> holding the JSON</td><td>the DWARF custom sections clang writes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>any other target</td><td colspan="2"><code>--debug-json FILE</code> writes the JSON</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two things make it useful to a front end built on polyir. <code>meta "license" = "MIT"</code> (about the module) and <code>meta area "doc" = "..."</code> (about the function or struct <code>area</code>) carry notes through to the module. And <code>--source-map FILE</code> maps every position to the front end's own source, so the debugger shows <code>app.nina:40</code> rather than a line of generated polyir text. The map is plain text: <code>source app.nina</code> lines, then <code>map &lt;polyir line&gt; &lt;source number&gt; &lt;line&gt; &lt;column&gt;</code> lines. The full description is in <code>docs/debug-and-meta.md</code>; every capability is listed in <code>CAPABILITIES.md</code>; and <code>docs/portability.md</code> assesses what to change so polyir maps cleanly to more targets, and back.</p>
<p>Positions reach polyir by one of three routes, and everything after is the same whichever was taken: a <strong>source map</strong>
next to generated <code>.poly</code> text; <code>"at": [line, column]</code> and <code>"origin": "file"</code> in <strong>HIR JSON</strong>;
or <code>Span</code> on every expression, statement and function and <code>origin</code> on each function of the HIR a front end
builds <strong>in-process</strong> with libpolyir (<code>CompileOptions::debugInfo</code> then writes DWARF from the spans). A
function's <code>origin</code> is also how the source back ends split a program into units: one C++ file per origin. What a
front end should hand over: a span on every statement and on every expression that can be the subject of an error or a
breakpoint (1-based, in its own source); an origin on every function; the source files; names that survive into a
backtrace; and <code>meta</code> for what a later tool needs without re-parsing the source. The Nina front end sets origins and
closure spans today and does not yet hand statement spans over.</p>
<pre><code>$ polyir app.poly --debug --source-map app.map --out app
$ polyir app.poly --meta --emit-wasm --out app.wasm       # custom section "polyir.meta"
$ polyir app.poly --debug-json app.json --check           # the description alone</code></pre>

<h2 id="equality">Equality: <code>==</code> and <code>===</code></h2>
<p><code>a == b</code> asks whether two values hold the same thing; <code>a === b</code> asks whether they are the same object, as in Kotlin.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Type</th><th><code>==</code> / <code>!=</code></th><th><code>===</code> / <code>!==</code></th></tr>
  <tr><td>number, <code>bool</code>, <code>char</code></td><td>the value (floats follow IEEE: <code>NaN != NaN</code>)</td><td>not allowed: a number has no identity</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>the same characters</td><td>the same string object</td></tr>
  <tr><td>struct</td><td>every field equal, recursively, as the fields are now</td><td>the same object</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>array</code>, <code>mutarray</code>, <code>buffer&lt;T,S&gt;</code></td><td>the same length and equal elements</td><td>the same object</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>variant</code>, <code>either</code></td><td>the same case and an equal payload</td><td>not allowed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>closure, <code>coro</code>, <code>ptr</code></td><td>not allowed: use <code>===</code></td><td>the same pointers</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>map</code>, <code>mutmap</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>mutset</code></td><td>the same entries (or members), in any order</td><td>the same object</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sparse</code>, <code>grid</code>, <code>tensor</code>, interface value</td><td>not supported yet</td><td>the same object</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A struct that defines <code>func equals(other: T) -&gt; i1</code> is compared with it, by <code>==</code>, <code>!=</code> and inside lists and other structs; without one the compiler writes the field-by-field comparison. A struct with no <code>var</code> field is an immutable value and has no identity until the program asks: a program that writes <code>a === b</code> on it makes it an object like one with a <code>var</code> field, so <code>let b = a</code> gives <code>b === a</code> and two separately made equal values are different objects. Only the structs compared by identity pay for it. <code>streq(a, b)</code> is gone; it is <code>a == b</code>.</p>
<pre><code>struct Point(x: i64, y: i64);
let p: Point = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
let q: Point = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
let same: Point = p
p == q       // true: the same fields
p === q      // false: two objects
p === same   // true: one object, two names</code></pre>

<h2 id="memory">Memory: what lives where</h2>
<p>polyir has no garbage collector and no <code>free</code> in the source. The rule is Java's: <strong>a number is a value; every object (a struct that can change, a container, a closure) is a reference, and copying the name shares the object.</strong> A struct that nothing can change is an immutable value, which behaves the same either way, so the compiler keeps it in registers.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Kind</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>A copy is&hellip;</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>i1</code>..<code>i64</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>vec&lt;T,N&gt;</code></td><td>a register</td><td>a copy</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>struct</code> with no <code>var</code> field (generic or not)</td><td>an immutable <em>value</em>: an SSA aggregate (<code>insertvalue</code>/<code>extractvalue</code>), never memory by itself</td><td>a copy (indistinguishable from sharing, since nothing can change it)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>struct</code> with a <code>var</code> field</td><td><strong>heap</strong>: one object holding the fields; the value is a pointer</td><td><strong>the same object</strong></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>var x</code></td><td>one stack slot (<code>alloca</code>) in the function's entry block</td><td>(a name, not a value)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>T[N]</code> local</td><td>the stack; as a parameter it decays to a <code>ptr</code></td><td>the same storage</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><strong>heap</strong>: a 24-byte header <code>{data, length, capacity}</code> from <code>calloc</code>, and the elements from <code>realloc</code> (grown by <code>push</code>)</td><td><strong>the same mutarray</strong></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sparse&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><strong>heap</strong>: a 72-byte header holding three mutarray headers (rows, columns, values)</td><td>the same sparse</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>mutmap&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td><td><strong>heap</strong>: a 40-byte header and three tables (keys, values, states), sized once when the map is made</td><td>the same mutmap</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>grid</code>, <code>tensor</code></td><td><strong>heap</strong>: one zeroed (<code>calloc</code>) row-major block of <code>R*C</code> (or <code>d1*&hellip;*dn</code>) elements; the value is the pointer</td><td><strong>the same grid</strong></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>array&lt;T,S&gt;</code></td><td><strong>heap</strong>: <code>S</code> elements from <code>realloc</code></td><td>the same array</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>string</code></td><td>a pointer to NUL-terminated bytes: a literal is a read-only global; <code>a + b</code> builds a new buffer</td><td>the same bytes (strings are never changed in place)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>closure</code></td><td><code>{fnPtr, envPtr}</code> by value; <code>envPtr</code> is a heap array of pointers to the captured names' boxes. A function that contains a closure literal puts <em>all</em> its parameters and locals in heap boxes so the closure and its creator share them</td><td>the same function and the same boxes</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>variant</code>, <code>either</code></td><td><code>{tag, payload}</code> by value; the payload is one heap cell</td><td>the same cell (it is never changed)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>interface value</td><td><code>{data, vtable}</code> by value; <code>data</code> is a heap copy of the struct, <code>vtable</code> a constant global</td><td>the same heap copy</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>coro</code></td><td><code>{handle, yieldSlot}</code> by value; the frame and the yield slot are heap</td><td>the same coroutine</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>try</code></td><td>a 256-byte <code>jmp_buf</code> on the stack; one internal global points at the innermost</td><td>(not a value)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>global</code></td><td>static storage (scalars and structs; a container-typed global is an error)</td><td>a copy</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>References.</strong> A <code>mutarray</code>, <code>sparse</code>, <code>mutmap</code>, <code>grid</code>, <code>tensor</code> or <code>array</code> is a pointer, and so is a struct with a <code>var</code> field: <code>let b = a</code> is a second name for the same object, a callee that writes through a parameter changes the caller's object, and a struct field of such a type shares it.</p>
<h3>Which fields can change</h3>
<p>A field is fixed once the struct is made unless it is declared <code>var</code>. A struct with at least one <code>var</code> field is a reference type; a struct with none is an immutable value.</p>
<p>The fields can also be written in parentheses after the name, like a constructor, with an optional body for methods: <code>struct Point(x: double, y: double);</code> is the same as <code>struct Point { x: double  y: double }</code>, and <code>struct Counter(var n: i64, id: i64) { func bump() -&gt; i64 { ... } }</code> has one field that can change. Generic structs take it too (<code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;(value: T);</code>), and so does an interface list after the parentheses (<code>struct Tag(code: i64) : Named</code>).</p>
<pre><code>struct Counter {
    var n: i64      // can change: c.n = c.n + 1
    id: i64         // fixed
    func bump() -&gt; i64 { this.n = this.n + 1
        return this.n }
}
let a: Counter = Counter { n: 1, id: 7 }
let b: Counter = a      // the same object
b.n = 5                 // a.n is now 5 too
a.id = 3                // error</code></pre>
<p>Assigning a field that is not <code>var</code> is a compile error that lists both groups: <code>cannot assign field id of Counter: it is not a var field. Counter can change: n; fixed: id</code>. A fixed field can still hold a container whose contents change (<code>push(h.items, 1)</code> is fine). The HIR dump prints <code>var</code> in front of the fields that can change.</p>
<h3>let, var and val</h3>
<p>The three declarations say what the <em>name</em> may do, separately from what the object may do.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>let a: T = e</code> fixes the name: it always means the same object, and assigning to it (or to a parameter) is a compile error. The object can still change through its <code>var</code> fields, or <code>push</code> and index writes for a container.</li>
<li><code>var a: T = e</code> lets the name move: <code>a = other</code> points it at a different object.</li>
<li><code>val b: T = e</code> is <code>let</code> over a complete copy: <code>b</code> is a fixed name for a new, independent object (<code>let b: T = val e</code>).</li>
</ul>
<p><code>val</code> is also a modifier. On a parameter, <code>func f(c: val Counter)</code> makes the callee work on its own copy (its body starts with <code>let c = val c</code>), so the caller's object is never touched. In front of an expression, <code>val a</code> means <code>a.copy()</code>. On a number or an immutable struct, <code>val</code> changes nothing.</p>
<p><strong>Copying.</strong> <code>x.copy()</code> (or <code>copy(x)</code>, or <code>val x</code>) works on all six reference containers and on structs, and returns a <em>new</em> container with the same elements; a later <code>push</code> or write to one is not seen by the other. The copy is shallow: a container inside a container is shared, as with <code>let y = outer[0]</code>. <code>a + b</code> on two darrays also builds a new mutarray.</p>
<pre><code>let a: mutarray&lt;i64&gt; = mutarray&lt;i64&gt;()
push(a, 1)
let b: mutarray&lt;i64&gt; = a.copy()
push(b, 2)          // a still has one element, b has two</code></pre>
<p><strong>Release.</strong> A container that nothing else can see is freed automatically. After type checking, <code>hir/Release.nina</code> looks at each container or reference struct a function makes (<code>Counter { ... }</code>, <code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;()</code>, <code>sparse</code>, <code>mutmap</code>, <code>array</code>, a <code>grid</code> or <code>tensor</code> declaration, <code>x.copy()</code>, a mutarray <code>+</code>) and binds to its own name. If that name is only indexed, measured, pushed to, copied, or passed to a function that does the same with that parameter (worked out for the whole program, so <code>fillGrid(g)</code> only <em>lends</em> the grid), it cannot outlive its block. The compiler then adds <code>free</code> where the block ends and before every <code>return</code> that leaves it, after the returned value has been computed. A loop that makes a 100&times;100 grid and a mutarray per iteration runs in constant memory: 20,000 iterations used 1.4&nbsp;MB, against about 1.6&nbsp;GB without the pass.</p>
<p>Two more things are released by the same analysis. A string made by <code>a + b</code> and bound to its own name is freed when nothing else can see it; a join that is only an operand of another join (<code>a + b</code> inside <code>a + b + c</code>) is freed as soon as the outer join has copied it. And a closure: a name that a closure literal mentions lives in a heap box so the closure and its creator share it. When every closure that mentions the name is bound to a name that is only ever called, the box, the closure's environment array and the closure itself are freed where the name goes out of scope. Only the names closures mention are boxed (they used to be all of a function's names). A loop that builds a string and a counting closure 1,000,000 times runs in 1.4&nbsp;MB.</p>
<h3>Ownership handed over by a call</h3>
<p>Only heap objects have owners. A number, a <code>vec</code> or an immutable struct is copied, so nothing is handed over. A function hands its caller a <em>fresh</em> object when every <code>return</code> is an object the function made itself and kept nowhere else: a literal, <code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;()</code>, a join, <code>x.copy()</code>, a closure literal, the result of another such call, or a local that holds one and goes nowhere but the <code>return</code>. The caller then owns the result of <code>let a = f()</code> as if it had made it, and frees it when nothing else can see it; a call whose result is thrown away is freed at once.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>the function returns&hellip;</th><th>handed over?</th></tr>
  <tr><td>a list it made and kept nowhere else</td><td>yes: the caller owns it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>the result of another call that hands one over</td><td>yes (worked out for the whole program)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>one of its arguments, an element, a field, a global</td><td>no: another name for something still shared</td></tr>
  <tr><td>an object it also pushed into a list or stored in a struct</td><td>no: the other holder still has it</td></tr>
  <tr><td>its argument on one path and a new list on another</td><td>no: the caller cannot tell which</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A loop that makes a struct, a string and a list from calls 1,000,000 times runs in 1.4&nbsp;MB.</p>
<h3>Variants, eithers and interface values</h3>
<p>The payload cell of <code>eitherOk(x)</code> or <code>variant&lt;...&gt;(i, x)</code>, and the private copy an interface value makes of a plain struct, are freed when the name that holds the value is only read (<code>isOk</code>, <code>unwrapOk</code>, <code>tag</code>, <code>s.area()</code>) and goes nowhere. An interface value over a struct with a <code>var</code> field points at the object itself, so it owns nothing.</p>
<h3>Objects held inside objects</h3>
<p>Freeing a struct frees the struct. Whether it also frees what its fields hold is decided for each <em>slot</em> of the whole program at once: <code>Pocket.items</code> (a struct field), or the elements of every <code>mutarray&lt;mutarray&lt;i64&gt;&gt;</code>. A slot is <strong>owned</strong> when</p>
<ul>
<li>every object put into it is fresh (a struct-literal field, a <code>push</code> or an assignment is a literal, <code>mutarray&lt;T&gt;()</code>, a join, a copy, or a call that hands over a fresh object);</li>
<li>every read of it is used in place (indexed, measured, pushed to, lent to a callee that only borrows it), never kept in a name, returned, stored or captured;</li>
<li>the outer object is never copied (<code>val</code>, <code>.copy()</code>, or <code>a + b</code> on lists would share the inside).</li>
</ul>
<p>Freeing the outer object then frees the owned slots first (for a list, one loop over its elements), and assigning a new object to an owned slot frees the old one. One stray <code>let items = p.items</code> anywhere in the program turns that slot back into a shared one, and its contents are simply not freed. A loop that makes a struct with its own list 1,000,000 times runs in 1.4&nbsp;MB (97&nbsp;MB before).</p>
<h3>Coroutines and maps</h3>
<p>A coroutine made by calling a <code>coro func</code> and only resumed (<code>resume</code>, <code>done</code>) is destroyed with its name (<code>llvm.coro.destroy</code>; the cleanup path frees the frame and the yield slot), and each <code>resume()</code> frees the cell it boxed when the coroutine turned out to be finished. A coroutine keeps its arguments in its frame past the call, so none of them is ever merely lent. A <code>mutmap</code> or <code>sparse</code> releases the <em>values</em> it holds under the same slot rule as lists (a loop over the slots that hold an entry, or over the entries); writing a new value over an old one in a mutmap releases the old one (a sparse only appends, so it does not). String keys are not released.</p>
<h3>Temporaries</h3>
<p>A fresh object used on the spot (<code>len(makeList())</code>, <code>lookup(Pocket { ... })</code>, <code>makeBag()[0]</code>, <code>streq(a + b, "x")</code>) is given a name by <code>hir/ReleaseTemporaries.nina</code>: a <code>let</code> just before the statement, so the analysis above frees it. Only a place that merely uses its operand is rewritten (an argument the callee only borrows, the operand of <code>len</code> or <code>streq</code>, the base of an index or field read), and only when nothing before it in the statement has effects, so the order of events never changes. The right side of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> is left alone.</p>
<p>An object is <em>not</em> released, and lives to the end of the run, when it is <em>shared</em> beyond what the program can prove: returned, stored in a closure or another variable, wrapped in an interface value, assigned to another name, passed to a function that keeps it (one that pushes it onto a list, or an <code>extern</code> function), held in a slot that is not owned, or made by a function that does not hand over a fresh object. Freeing those needs reference counting or a collector, and polyir has neither. Also never released: string literals (constants), mutmap string keys, and everything inside a function that uses labels and jumps (<code>goto</code>, <code>branch</code>, <code>switch</code>) or is itself a coroutine body. On a native target the memory comes from the C library's <code>realloc</code>, <code>calloc</code> and <code>free</code>; <code>--emit-wasm</code> links a small allocator (with <code>free</code>, <code>memcpy</code>, <code>strlen</code> and <code>strcmp</code>) that keeps a free list for each block size, so wasm loops run in constant memory too. GPU kernels never allocate: their buffers belong to the host. Coroutines run on native builds only (the JIT cannot compile them).</p>
<p>The allocations are made in <code>src/Containers.nina</code> (headers and their field offsets), <code>hir/lower/LowerNew.nina</code> (new containers, grids, tensors, arrays), <code>LowerConcat.nina</code> (joins), <code>LowerBox.nina</code> and <code>LowerClosure.nina</code> (boxes and environments), <code>LowerCopy.nina</code> (copies and <code>free</code>), <code>LowerSum.nina</code> (variant payloads), <code>LowerIface.nina</code> (interface copies and tables), <code>LowerCoro.nina</code> (frames) and <code>LowerTry.nina</code> (the <code>jmp_buf</code>).</p>

<h2>Method call syntax</h2>
<p><code>receiver.method(args)</code> is rewritten to <code>method(receiver, args)</code>. That is a <em>static</em> call: the compiler
knows which function it is. (When the program has to decide while it runs, use an interface, in the next section.)
<code>x.toI32()</code>, <code>x.toFloat()</code> and friends are explicit numeric casts, and <code>.toI64()</code> /
<code>.toPtr()</code> convert between an address and an integer (the GPU host runtimes rely on this).</p>

<h2 id="interfaces">Interfaces</h2>
<p>Sometimes one function has to work on values of <em>different</em> structs, and which struct it gets is only known while the program
runs: a drawing function that is handed a circle or a rectangle, a list that holds both. An <strong>interface</strong> names the methods
those structs have in common. A struct says which interfaces it implements after a colon, and the compiler checks the promise.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:examples/interfaces.poly:7:10}}

{{def:examples/interfaces.poly:Square+Rect}}</code></pre>
<p><code>Square</code> and <code>Rect</code> both have <code>area</code> and <code>scaled</code> with the same parameter and return types as <code>Shape</code>
declares (the parameter <em>names</em> may differ), so both may be used wherever a <code>Shape</code> is expected. A value written with the
interface's name holds any of them:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:examples/interfaces.poly:totalArea+pick+chosen+sumAll}}</code></pre>
<p><code>chosen()</code> returns <code>36034</code>: <code>a</code> is a square of side 6 (area 36, written <code>36000</code> after the multiplication), and
<code>b</code> is a 2 by 7 rectangle, whose area is 14 and whose <code>scaled(10)</code> is 20. The line <code>a.area()</code> and the line
<code>b.area()</code> look the same, but they call different functions. A struct is converted to the interface where the interface is expected: in a
<code>let</code> or <code>var</code>, an assignment, a call argument, a <code>return</code>, a struct literal's field, a <code>push</code> onto a
<code>mutarray&lt;Shape&gt;</code>, or the payload of an <code>either</code>.</p>

<h3>What an interface value is</h3>
<p>An interface value is two pointers, <code>{ data, vtable }</code>; in LLVM, <code>%Shape = type { ptr, ptr }</code>.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>data</code> points at a <em>copy</em> of the struct on the heap. The copy is made at the conversion, with <code>realloc</code>, sized by
      LLVM itself (a <code>getelementptr</code> one step past a null pointer, so padding is counted), and, like every other box in polyir, it is
      never freed. Structs in polyir are immutable values, so the copy can never be out of date.</li>
  <li><code>vtable</code> points at a constant table, one per pair of (struct, interface): <code>@polyir_vt_Square_Shape = global [2 x ptr]</code>, with one
      function pointer per interface method, in the order the interface declares them.</li>
</ul>
<p>The table entries are not the struct's methods themselves, because a method such as <code>Square_area</code> takes the struct <em>by value</em>
and the caller of an interface value only has a pointer. Each entry is a small generated function, a <em>thunk</em>, that loads the struct and calls
the real method:</p>
<pre><code>define i64 @polyir_thunk_Square_Shape_area(ptr %p0) {
entry:
  %v1 = load %Square, ptr %p0
  %v2 = call i64 @Square_area(%Square %v1)
  ret i64 %v2
}</code></pre>
<p>A call <code>s.area()</code> then reads the interface's slot number (known at compile time) out of the table and calls it with the data pointer:</p>
<pre><code>define i64 @totalArea(%Shape %p0) {
entry:
  %v1 = extractvalue %Shape %p0, 0          ; data
  %v2 = extractvalue %Shape %p0, 1          ; vtable
  %v3 = getelementptr ptr, ptr %v2, i64 0   ; slot of area
  %v4 = load ptr, ptr %v3
  %v5 = call i64 %v4(ptr %v1)
  ret i64 %v5
}</code></pre>

<h3>The rules, and the messages</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What the compiler says</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>struct C : Nope</code></td><td><code>1:12: unknown interface Nope</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>the struct lacks a method</td><td><code>4:12: C does not implement Shape: it needs func area() -&gt; double</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>a method has other types</td><td><code>6:8: C.area does not match Shape.area: expected func area() -&gt; double, found func area() -&gt; i64</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>a struct that never wrote <code>: Shape</code> used as one</td><td><code>9:18: R does not implement Shape (declare it: struct R : Shape)</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>a method the interface lacks</td><td><code>10:12: Shape has no method perimeter</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>reading a field of an interface value</td><td><code>10:12: Shape is an interface: it has methods, not fields (call a method instead of reading r)</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>a method declared twice</td><td><code>3:8: interface Shape declares area twice</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>a generic struct, once instantiated, whose method has other types</td><td><code>6:8: Box_i64.area does not match Shape.area: expected func area() -&gt; double, found func area() -&gt; i64</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>an interface value in a <code>kernel func</code></td><td><code>4:13: the type Shape cannot be used in a kernel</code> (a GPU has no tables of function pointers)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The checks that look only at declarations (everything but the conversion and the call) are in <code>src/Interfaces.nina</code> and run before the program is
checked; the conversion and the call are checked with the rest of the program in <code>hir/check/CheckIface.nina</code>; the tables and thunks are written by
<code>hir/lower/LowerIface.nina</code>. In the typed HIR an interface is a declaration (<code>HIfaceDef</code>: the method signatures and the structs that
implement it) beside an ordinary struct of the same name that holds the two pointers, so everything that moves structs around (arguments, fields,
<code>mutarray</code> elements) moves interface values with no extra code. The two new operations are <code>HBuiltin.IfaceBox</code> and
<code>HBuiltin.IfaceCall</code>.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>WebAssembly.</strong> The tables and the indirect calls need nothing special on wasm32, but the copy made at the conversion needs
<code>realloc</code>, and a wasm module has no libc. <code>polyir --emit-wasm</code> therefore links a 30-line bump allocator (<code>realloc</code> and
<code>calloc</code>, growing linear memory a page at a time) whenever the program declares either one. Running
<code>examples/interfaces.poly</code> under wasmtime gives the same numbers as the JIT.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ 3 -->
<h1 id="pipeline">3. The compiler pipeline</h1>

<p>A compiler is a chain of stages, each turning one representation of the program into the next. polyir's chain is
written down in one file, <code>src/Pipeline.nina</code>, so you can read it top to bottom and see when each step
happens. There are seven phases. Phases 1 to 5 are shared by <em>every</em> output; only the end differs.</p>

<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 280" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="compiler pipeline diagram">
  <defs><marker id="a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="#586069"/></marker></defs>
  <rect x="10" y="10" width="95" height="44" rx="6" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><text x="57" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">1 parse</text><text x="57" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">text → tree</text>
  <rect x="120" y="10" width="95" height="44" rx="6" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><text x="167" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">2 imports</text><text x="167" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">merge files</text>
  <rect x="230" y="10" width="95" height="44" rx="6" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><text x="277" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">3 restrictions</text><text x="277" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">coroutine rules</text>
  <rect x="340" y="10" width="95" height="44" rx="6" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><text x="387" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">4 monomorphize</text><text x="387" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">generics → structs</text>
  <rect x="450" y="10" width="110" height="44" rx="6" fill="#0b5cad"/><text x="505" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">5 check</text><text x="505" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#fff">tree → typed HIR</text>
  <rect x="575" y="10" width="115" height="44" rx="6" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><text x="632" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">5b diagnose</text><text x="632" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">file:line:col errors</text>
  <path d="M105 32H120M215 32H230M325 32H340M435 32H450M560 32H575" stroke="#586069" marker-end="url(#a)"/>
  <path d="M632 54V90H505" stroke="#586069" fill="none" marker-end="url(#a)"/>
  <rect x="405" y="90" width="200" height="40" rx="6" fill="#fff" stroke="#0b5cad" stroke-width="2"/><text x="505" y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">typed HIR</text><text x="505" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">every expression carries its type and position</text>
  <path d="M430 130L125 190M505 130V190M580 130L585 190" stroke="#586069" fill="none" marker-end="url(#a)"/>
  <rect x="10" y="190" width="230" height="76" rx="6" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="125" y="210" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">6 declare + 7 lower</text><text x="125" y="227" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">LLVM types, signatures, then instructions</text><text x="125" y="248" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700">LLVM IR → clang → CPU program</text>
  <rect x="260" y="190" width="215" height="76" rx="6" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="367" y="210" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">kernel verify + MSL emitter</text><text x="367" y="227" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">reads only the kernel functions</text><text x="367" y="248" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700">Metal source (--emit-msl)</text>
  <rect x="495" y="190" width="195" height="76" rx="6" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="592" y="210" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700">kernel verify + GLSL emitter</text><text x="592" y="227" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9">reads only the kernel functions</text><text x="592" y="248" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700">GLSL (--emit-glsl) → SPIR-V</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 1. Phases 1 to 5 are shared. The three outputs (CPU, Metal, Vulkan) all start from the typed HIR.</figcaption>
</figure>

<table>
  <tr><th>#</th><th>Phase</th><th>Where</th><th>Input → output</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>parse</td><td><code>Polyir.parse</code> (from <code>Polyir.intent</code>)</td><td>source text → parse tree</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>imports</td><td><code>Imports.nina</code></td><td>merge imported files, reject duplicate names, mark which file each declaration came from</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>restrictions</td><td><code>Coroutines.nina</code>, <code>Interfaces.nina</code></td><td>syntax rules that need the raw tree (for example where <code>yield</code> is allowed, and that a struct really implements the interfaces it names)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4</td><td>monomorphize</td><td><code>Generics.nina</code></td><td><code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code> → one concrete struct per use</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>check</td><td><code>hir/check/*</code></td><td>parse tree → typed HIR</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5b</td><td>diagnose</td><td><code>CheckDiagnose.nina</code></td><td>unknown names, wrong argument counts, type mistakes → <code>file:line:col: message</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>6</td><td>declare</td><td><code>Declare.nina</code>, <code>Types.nina</code></td><td>an LLVM type, signature or global for every top-level item</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>lower</td><td><code>hir/lower/*</code></td><td>typed HIR → LLVM instructions, function by function</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Why the split matters: from phase 5 on, nothing reads the parse tree again. The HIR is the single source of
truth, so a CPU program and a GPU kernel mean exactly the same thing: the type checker decided it once.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>Reading tip.</strong> <code>PolyirPipeline.compile</code> is the CPU route. For the GPU the
entry points are <code>kernelsToMsl</code> and <code>kernelsToGlsl</code>; both call <code>analyze</code> (phases 1 to
5) and then hand the HIR to <code>PolyirKernels</code>. The command-line tool (<code>cli/src/Polyircli.intent</code>)
parses the file once, runs the unused-variable lint on the tree, and then calls into these functions.</div>

<h2 id="generics">Generics: one generic, many copies</h2>
<p>A generic struct or function (<code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;(value: T)</code>, <code>func pick&lt;T&gt;(a: T, b: T) -&gt; T</code>) is
compiled as one copy per set of types the program uses: <code>Box_i64</code>, <code>Box_double</code>, <code>pick_i64</code>. LLVM and
the VM run those copies. The HIR also keeps the generic itself, read once with <code>T</code> standing for any type, and every copy names
the generic it came from and its types. A back end that writes generics, such as a Java or TypeScript one, can then write
<code>Box&lt;Long&gt;</code> and one <code>class Box&lt;T&gt;</code>, not <code>Box_i64</code>.</p>
<p>That one reading also says whether the generic <em>works for every type</em>. A body that only stores, returns and passes on its
<code>T</code> values does. One that adds them (<code>this.value + this.value</code>) or casts them (<code>this.a.toI64()</code>) needs
<code>T</code> to be a number, so it does not. The program still compiles and runs, because each copy has real types. The generic keeps
the reason, <code>T can be any type, and `+` does not work on every type</code>, and is written as its copies.</p>
<pre><code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;(value: T) {          // works for every type: --emit-poly writes it once
  func get() -&gt; T {
    return this.value
  }
}
struct Doubler&lt;T&gt;(value: T) {      // does not: --emit-poly writes Doubler_i64
  func doubled() -&gt; T {
    return this.value + this.value
  }
}</code></pre>
<p><code>polyir-check F.poly --emit-poly</code> writes <code>Box</code> once, with its uses as <code>Box&lt;i64&gt;</code> and its
literals and calls by the generic's name (<code>Box(value: 1)</code>, <code>pick(a, b)</code>). HIR JSON and <code>.pbc</code> carry the
generics too (see <code>docs/language/hir-json.md</code>).</p>

<!-- ============================================================ 4 -->
<h1 id="hir">4. The typed HIR</h1>

<p><strong>HIR</strong> stands for <em>high-level intermediate representation</em>: the program after every name is
resolved and every type is known, but before it becomes any final output. Before the HIR existed, polyir's compiler
walked the parse tree and emitted LLVM instructions immediately. It never wrote down what type an expression had; it
re-discovered types by looking at the text of LLVM types (for example <code>"%Point"</code>). A second output format
would have had to repeat all of that. The HIR fixes this once.</p>

<h3>Generic structs implement interfaces too</h3>
<p>A generic struct may claim an interface. Each instantiation is its own struct (<code>Cell&lt;i64&gt;</code> becomes <code>Cell_i64</code>, <code>Cell&lt;double&gt;</code> becomes <code>Cell_double</code>) with its own method table, so the interface's signatures are compared with the struct's methods <em>after</em> the type argument has been put in: <code>func get() -&gt; T</code> matches an interface method <code>-&gt; i64</code> for <code>Cell&lt;i64&gt;</code> and is reported for <code>Cell&lt;double&gt;</code>. A name after the <code>:</code> that is not an interface is reported even when the generic struct is never used.</p>
<pre><code>interface Weighted { func weight() -&gt; i64 }
struct Cell&lt;T&gt; : Weighted {
    value: T
    func weight() -&gt; i64 { return 7 }
}
func total(a: Weighted, b: Weighted) -&gt; i64 { return a.weight() + b.weight() }
// total(Cell { value: 5 }, Cell { value: 2.5 }) is 14: one table for Cell&lt;i64&gt;, another for Cell&lt;double&gt;</code></pre>

<h2>What is in it</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Types</strong> (<code>HType</code>): <code>HInt(bits)</code>, <code>HFloat(bits)</code>, <code>HPtr</code>, <code>HVec</code>, <code>HStruct</code>, <code>HDarray</code>, <code>HClosure</code>, ... one explicit variant for each.</li>
  <li><strong>Expressions</strong> (<code>HExpr</code>): constants, locals, binary and unary operations, casts, calls, field and index access. Every node records its type and its source position (<code>Span</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Statements</strong> (<code>HStmt</code>): <code>HLet</code>, <code>HVarDecl</code>, <code>HAssign</code>, <code>HIf</code>, <code>HFor</code>, <code>HReturn</code>, labels, goto, ...</li>
  <li><strong>Declarations</strong> (<code>HDecl</code>): structs and functions with resolved parameter and return types.</li>
</ul>
<p>Operations that were implicit before are now explicit nodes. For example, whether <code>a + b</code> is an integer or a
floating-point add is decided once (from the operand types), not re-guessed by each back end.</p>

<h2>Errors come from the checker</h2>
<p>When the checker meets something wrong it does not stop at the first problem: it records an <code>HUnsupported(message, type, span)</code>
node. After checking, phase 5b walks the HIR and reports each as <code>line:col: message</code> (with the file in front when the program is made of several, see below). What it catches:</p>
<pre><code>{{file:snippets/errors.poly}}</code></pre>
<p>The compiler answers:</p>
<pre><code>$ polyir --check errors.poly
{{file:snippets/errors.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>Type checking is by <em>kind</em> (integer, float, string, struct); everything else is left unchecked, which keeps the
checker small and avoids rejecting valid programs.</p>

<h2>Errors say which file</h2>
<p>A program made of several files (<code>import "lib/helper.poly"</code>) names the file in every error, in the
<code>file:line:col: message</code> form that editors and <code>make</code> already understand. Here <code>main.poly</code> imports
<code>lib/helper.poly</code>; the helper has a mistake in a function and another in a struct method, and <code>main</code> has its own:</p>
<p><code>lib/helper.poly</code>:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:tests/programs/fixtures/diagnostics/lib/helper.poly}}</code></pre>
<p><code>main.poly</code>:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:tests/programs/fixtures/diagnostics/main.poly}}</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ cd tests &amp;&amp; polyir --check fixtures/diagnostics/main.poly
{{file:snippets/errors-files.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>The same form is used for a parse error (which also gains its line and column), for a kernel rule broken in an imported file, for a
missing import (reported at the <code>import</code> line that names it) and for the lint's warnings. <strong>How it works:</strong> the
import step keeps each <code>import</code> line in the list of declarations as a <em>marker</em> meaning "what follows is from that file"
(<code>src/Origins.nina</code>), starting each file's declarations with a marker for the file itself and repeating it after each import. A
diagnostic looks up the function it was found in (a struct's method as <code>Struct_method</code>, as the checker names it) and writes that
function's file in front. A program checked from memory has no markers, so its errors read <code>line:col: message</code> as before.</p>

<h2>Why this is the foundation for GPUs</h2>
<p>GPU languages require <em>structured</em> control flow (clear <code>if</code> and loops). LLVM IR allows arbitrary
jumps, so Metal or SPIR-V cannot be recovered from it reliably. The HIR still has the original structure: an
<code>HIf</code> is still an <code>HIf</code>. The GPU emitters read the HIR directly and never look at LLVM IR.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ 5 -->
<h1 id="gpu-primer">5. GPU computing foundations</h1>

<p>This section is the mental model. If it clicks, writing a kernel (section 6) is easy; if it does not, kernels look
like strange functions with odd rules. Read it slowly once.</p>

<h2>5.1 CPU versus GPU: two answers to &ldquo;go fast&rdquo;</h2>
<p>A computer can get faster in two ways: finish each task sooner, or finish many tasks at once. A <strong>CPU</strong> is
built for the first. It has a handful of big, clever cores with large caches, branch prediction and out-of-order
execution, all aimed at making <em>one</em> sequence of instructions run as fast as possible. A <strong>GPU</strong> (graphics
processing unit) is built for the second. It has hundreds to thousands of small, simple arithmetic units that are
cheap, slow individually, and fed in bulk. Think of a CPU as a few expert chefs and a GPU as a thousand line cooks who
each know only one recipe step. The experts win on a complicated dish; the line cooks win when you need a million
identical sandwiches.</p>

<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 200" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="CPU versus GPU chip layout">
  <text x="170" y="16" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700">CPU: few big cores</text>
  <rect x="20" y="26" width="300" height="150" rx="8" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/>
  <g fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"><rect x="34" y="40" width="64" height="56"/><rect x="106" y="40" width="64" height="56"/><rect x="178" y="40" width="64" height="56"/><rect x="250" y="40" width="56" height="56"/></g>
  <g font-size="9" text-anchor="middle"><text x="66" y="72">core</text><text x="138" y="72">core</text><text x="210" y="72">core</text><text x="278" y="72">core</text></g>
  <rect x="34" y="106" width="272" height="26" fill="#fff8e6" stroke="#9a6700"/><text x="170" y="123" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">big caches</text>
  <rect x="34" y="140" width="272" height="26" fill="#fff" stroke="#586069"/><text x="170" y="157" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">control logic: prediction, reordering</text>
  <text x="530" y="16" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700">GPU: many small cores</text>
  <rect x="380" y="26" width="300" height="150" rx="8" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/>
  <g fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad" id="cells"></g>
  <g fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad">
    <rect x="392" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="418" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="444" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="470" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="496" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="522" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="548" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="574" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="600" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="626" y="40" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="652" y="40" width="18" height="22"/>
    <rect x="392" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="418" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="444" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="470" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="496" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="522" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="548" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="574" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="600" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="626" y="66" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="652" y="66" width="18" height="22"/>
    <rect x="392" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="418" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="444" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="470" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="496" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="522" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="548" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="574" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="600" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="626" y="92" width="22" height="22"/><rect x="652" y="92" width="18" height="22"/>
  </g>
  <rect x="392" y="122" width="278" height="20" fill="#fff8e6" stroke="#9a6700"/><text x="531" y="136" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">small shared memory + very high memory bandwidth</text>
  <rect x="392" y="148" width="278" height="20" fill="#fff" stroke="#586069"/><text x="531" y="162" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">little control logic: all cores do the same step</text>
  <text x="350" y="194" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#586069">Same silicon budget, different spending: the CPU spends it on cleverness, the GPU on arithmetic units.</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 2. Schematic only: real chips have far more units than are drawn.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>5.2 The execution model: one kernel, a grid of threads</h2>
<p>You write a small function, the <strong>kernel</strong>, for <em>one element</em> (&ldquo;double this number&rdquo;). The GPU runs it
once per element, in parallel. Each running copy is a <strong>thread</strong>. All the threads of one launch form a
<strong>grid</strong>, and each thread learns which element it owns from a built-in number, its <strong>thread index</strong>
(called <code>__gid</code> in the generated Metal source and <code>index i</code> in a polyir kernel).</p>

<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 190" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="one kernel, many threads">
  <defs><marker id="a2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="#586069"/></marker></defs>
  <text x="350" y="16" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700">kernel scale(x, k) = x * k + 1</text>
  <g font-size="11" text-anchor="middle">
    <rect x="30" y="40" width="70" height="30" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="65" y="60">1.0</text>
    <rect x="100" y="40" width="70" height="30" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="135" y="60">2.5</text>
    <rect x="170" y="40" width="70" height="30" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="205" y="60">0.0</text>
    <rect x="240" y="40" width="70" height="30" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/><text x="275" y="60">8.0</text>
