<text x="62" y="38" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x0</text>
<text x="568" y="38" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X0</text>
<text x="62" y="62" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x1</text>
<text x="568" y="62" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X1</text>
<text x="62" y="86" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x2</text>
<text x="568" y="86" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X2</text>
<text x="62" y="110" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x3</text>
<text x="568" y="110" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X3</text>
<text x="62" y="134" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x4</text>
<text x="568" y="134" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X4</text>
<text x="62" y="158" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x5</text>
<text x="568" y="158" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X5</text>
<text x="62" y="182" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x6</text>
<text x="568" y="182" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X6</text>
<text x="62" y="206" font-size="11" fill="#586069" text-anchor="end">x7</text>
<text x="568" y="206" font-size="11" fill="#586069">X7</text>
<text x="350" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#586069">8 numbers: 3 stages of 4 butterflies = 12 butterflies (the plain transform needs 64 multiplications).</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 59. Eight numbers need three stages of four butterflies each. Each butterfly (an X) reads two lines and writes a sum and a rotated difference. The pairs are 4 apart, then 2 apart, then 1 apart. (Schematic: the actual Stockham arrangement in the program numbers its lines differently but does the same work.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>The linear-algebra view.</strong> The Fourier matrix <em>F</em> of section 24.5 is a dense <em>N</em> &times; <em>N</em> matrix: multiplying by it costs <em>N</em>&sup2;. The FFT is a <em>factorization</em> of <em>F</em> into log<sub>2</sub> <em>N</em> matrices that are each almost empty (every row has just two non-zero entries, one butterfly). Multiplying by a sparse matrix is cheap, so applying the factors one after another costs <em>N</em> log <em>N</em>. The same matrix, the same answer, found through a cleverly chosen product.</p>
<h3>On the GPU</h3>
<p>Each stage is one GPU launch with one thread per butterfly, reading one array and writing another (so the two arrays swap roles, &ldquo;ping-pong&rdquo;, as in the pair-adding reduction of section 13.4). The program uses the <em>Stockham</em> arrangement, which writes the results in natural order and needs no final reshuffle. Complex numbers are stored as pairs (real, imaginary) in one array, and <em>batches</em> of many transforms run at once by giving each its own stretch of the array:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/audio/Fft.poly:20:52}}</code></pre>
<p>The program <code>ideas/gpu/examples/fft.poly</code> checks it three ways: every one of 2,048 frequencies agrees with the plain transform; the inverse transform (the same stages with the rotation reversed, then divided by <em>N</em>) brings back the original signal; and a batch result agrees with the plain transform of the same slice. Then it times it:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Work</th><th>Plain transform (GPU)</th><th>FFT, one CPU core</th><th>FFT (GPU)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1 transform of 1,024</td><td>about 3 ms</td><td>under 1 ms</td><td>about 3 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 transform of 16,384</td><td>about 65 ms</td><td>about 2 ms</td><td>about 4 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 transform of 65,536</td><td>(not tried: 4.3 billion terms)</td><td>about 4 ms</td><td>about 4 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 transform of 262,144</td><td>(not tried)</td><td>about 18 ms</td><td>about 5 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>2,048 transforms of 4,096 at once</strong></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>about 365 ms</td><td>about 25 ms</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Read this table honestly. The algorithm matters far more than the hardware: the FFT on <em>one CPU core</em> beats the plain transform on the GPU by a huge margin. For a single modest transform the GPU gains nothing, because each of the 10 to 18 stages is a separate launch, and launches cost about as much as the work. The GPU's advantage appears when there is a <strong>lot</strong> to do: one very large transform (262,144 numbers: about 4 times faster), and above all a <strong>batch</strong>, where thousands of transforms keep thousands of threads busy (about 15 times faster here). Batches are exactly what a spectrogram needs.</p>
<h3>The spectrogram: seeing sound</h3>
<p>A single transform tells you which frequencies a sound contains <em>overall</em>, but not <em>when</em>. A <strong>spectrogram</strong> answers both: cut the sound into short, overlapping slices (here 512 samples, advancing 128 each time), take the FFT of each slice, and draw the strength of each frequency as brightness, with time running left to right and frequency running upward. Before transforming, each slice is multiplied by a smooth <strong>Hann window</strong> that fades to zero at both ends, because a slice cut out sharply would show false frequencies from its abrupt edges. The test sound is a glide, a scale and a chord:</p>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/spectrogram.pgm:3}}" alt="a spectrogram showing a rising line, a staircase of notes with harmonics, and a stack of parallel lines for a chord" style="width:90%; border:1px solid var(--rule); display:block; margin:auto;">
<figcaption>Figure 60. A spectrogram computed entirely on the GPU: 278 overlapping slices transformed as one batch, then one thread per pixel. Time runs right (4.5 seconds), frequency runs up (0 to 2,000 Hz). Left: a sine gliding from 200 Hz to 1,800 Hz, a straight rising line. Middle: the C major scale, a staircase; each note is three lines (the note and its two harmonics). Right: a held chord, a stack of parallel lines.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Reading a spectrogram becomes a skill: a steady tone is a horizontal line, a rising pitch a rising line, a harmonic-rich note a comb of parallel lines (the spacing is the fundamental frequency), a drum hit a vertical smear (a click contains every frequency), speech a pattern of moving bands. Spectrograms are how speech-recognition and music-identification systems &ldquo;see&rdquo; audio, and they are an image, so the picture tools of the earlier sections (convolutional filters, section 17.8) apply to sound too.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/audio/Fft.poly:63:99}}</code></pre>
<div class="note"><strong>Why the pixel code carries a &ldquo;4&rdquo;.</strong> A sine of height 1 shows up with magnitude <em>N</em>/2 in the transform, but the Hann window halves its height, so the pixel code multiplies by 4 / <em>N</em> to make a full-height sine read as 1 (0 dB). The picture maps &minus;70 dB (black) to 0 dB (white): decibels are twenty times the base-10 logarithm of the ratio, the scale on which loudness is perceived.</div>

<h2>24.12 How television worked: from a grey picture to colour</h2>
<p>A television signal is, in the end, <strong>one wave that carries a picture</strong>. A picture is two-dimensional; a wire carries one number at a time. The whole of television is the answer to a single question: <em>how do you send a 2D image down a 1D wire so a receiver can rebuild it?</em> The answer starts from section 24.2 (a picture is a list of numbers) and adds one trick: <strong>the position of a value on the screen is carried by <em>when</em> it arrives</strong>. No pixel is ever addressed. The receiver just has to sweep its beam across the screen in step with the sender. This section explains the machinery, first for the black-and-white sets of the 1940s and then for the way colour was added without breaking every set already in people&rsquo;s homes.</p>
<h3>The black-and-white set: a beam, a screen and a voltage</h3>
<p>The picture tube (a <em>cathode-ray tube</em>, CRT) is a glass bottle with the air pumped out. At the narrow end an <strong>electron gun</strong> fires a beam of electrons; at the wide end the inside of the glass is coated with a <strong>phosphor</strong> that glows where electrons hit it. Three things make a picture:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Brightness is a voltage.</strong> A control grid in the gun lets more or fewer electrons through as the incoming voltage rises and falls. A high voltage gives a bright dot; a low one a dark dot. The video signal simply <em>is</em> that voltage, moment by moment.</li>
<li><strong>Position is a sweep.</strong> Magnetic coils (the <em>deflection yoke</em>) steer the beam. One makes it sweep left to right at a steady speed, then snap back; the other moves it slowly down. The beam therefore draws <strong>lines</strong>, one under another, and a picture is a stack of 525 of them (about 480 visible). There are no pixels along a line: the brightness varies continuously, which is why analogue video is a <em>waveform</em> and not a grid of numbers. Only the vertical direction is sampled, one sample per line.</li>
<li><strong>Persistence.</strong> The phosphor keeps glowing for a few milliseconds, and the eye blends a rapid sequence of images, so a dot of light racing over the screen looks like a whole steady picture.</li>
</ul>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/raster.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @raster. How the beam draws a picture. Each field is a set of lines; the second field fills the gaps of the first. The American standard of 1941 chose 525 lines and 60 fields (30 pictures) a second, tied to the 60 Hz of the mains so that hum from the power supply would not roll across the picture.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><strong>Interlacing.</strong> Sending 60 whole pictures a second would have needed twice the bandwidth, and sending only 30 flickers visibly. The compromise is to send the odd lines (a <em>field</em>), then the even lines in the gaps: the screen is refreshed 60 times a second, but only 30 pictures&rsquo; worth of lines are sent.</p>
<h3>Keeping sender and receiver in step: sync pulses</h3>
<p>If the receiver&rsquo;s beam is a hair too fast or slow the picture tears or rolls, so the sender includes timing marks in the signal itself. Between lines, the voltage drops to a level <em>lower than black</em> for about 4.7 microseconds: a <strong>horizontal sync pulse</strong>, meaning &ldquo;start the next line now&rdquo;. Between fields there is a longer run of such pulses, the <strong>vertical sync</strong>: &ldquo;go back to the top&rdquo;. Because the sync lies &ldquo;blacker than black&rdquo;, it cannot be mistaken for picture, and the beam is switched off while it passes, so the retrace is invisible. The stretches around the sync pulses are the <em>blanking</em> intervals. A line lasts 63.5 microseconds, of which about 52.6 carry picture.</p>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/scanline.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @scanline. One line of the signal. Top: black and white. The line starts at the blanking level, drops to the sync tip, comes back, and then the voltage between black (7.5 on a scale where white is 100) and white (100) is the brightness along the line. This example climbs from dark to bright in eight steps. Bottom: a colour line, with the burst on the back porch and the colour wave added on top of the same brightness steps. (The wave is drawn with far fewer cycles than the real one.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Over the air, the picture was sent by amplitude modulation of a radio carrier (strong signal = bright) and the sound by frequency modulation of a second carrier 4.5 MHz above it, all in a 6 MHz-wide channel. The set&rsquo;s tuner, amplifier and detector turn it back into the voltage, and the sync pulses are split off by a circuit that simply looks for the part of the signal below black. That is the whole black-and-white set: <strong>a detector, a sync separator, two sweep generators and a gun</strong>.</p>
<h3>Adding colour without breaking every television</h3>
<p>By the late 1940s there were millions of black-and-white sets, and a broadcaster who switched to a new colour system would have silenced them all. So the regulator faced a hard constraint: the colour signal had to be <strong>compatible</strong>. A black-and-white set receiving it must show a good grey picture, and a colour set receiving an old black-and-white broadcast must show grey too.</p>
<p>The first colour system approved in the United States (1950) was <em>field-sequential</em>: a spinning wheel of red, green and blue filters turned a monochrome picture into colour. It was not compatible, since ordinary sets could not show it, and it was withdrawn in 1951. The system that won, designed by the second National Television System Committee (<strong>NTSC</strong>) and approved in 1953, took the opposite approach: leave the black-and-white signal exactly as it was, and <strong>add the colour on top as something an old set would hardly notice</strong>. Four ideas made that possible, and each is something from this chapter:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Split the picture into brightness and colour.</strong> Red, green and blue are mixed to make the old brightness signal, <em>Y</em> (the &ldquo;luma&rdquo;), which is sent exactly as before; an old set uses only that. The extra colour information is two <em>difference</em> signals (<em>I</em> and <em>Q</em>, section 24.13), which are zero for any grey. Grey and white therefore need no colour information at all: on a colour set a grey picture <em>is</em> the old signal, with the colour part switched off by a &ldquo;colour killer&rdquo; circuit.</li>
<li><strong>Put the colour in the gaps of the spectrum.</strong> A picture signal repeats line after line, 15,734 times a second, so its energy bunches into narrow bands around multiples of that rate, with empty spaces between them. Choose a colour carrier of exactly 227.5 times the line rate (about 3.58 MHz) and the colour energy falls into those empty spaces, interleaved with the brightness. Both share the same stretch of the channel. An old set sees the colour as a very fine dot pattern, too small to notice on a small screen (the <em>dot crawl</em> of section 24.13 is its trace).</li>
<li><strong>Carry two signals on one carrier.</strong> The sine and cosine of the carrier are independent (quadrature), so <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> ride the same wave. Hue is the wave&rsquo;s timing shift, and saturation is its size (section 24.13).</li>
<li><strong>Tell the receiver where the timing starts.</strong> A short run of the pure carrier, the <strong>colour burst</strong> (about nine cycles), is sent on the back porch of every line, after the sync. The set locks a local oscillator to it, and every colour is measured against that reference. If the lock is a little off, every hue turns by the same angle, which is exactly what the <strong>tint</strong> knob corrects.</li>
</ol>
<p>One adjustment is worth knowing. The colour carrier and the 4.5 MHz sound carrier can beat together and print a faint pattern on the picture. To avoid it, the line rate was moved from 15,750 to 15,734.26 Hz, which slowed the picture from 30 to <strong>29.97 pictures a second</strong>. That odd frame rate, still found in video today, exists because of the colour carrier.</p>
<h3>The colour tube: three guns and a mask</h3>
<p>A colour screen has three kinds of phosphor, one glowing red, one green, one blue, laid out as thousands of tiny triads of dots (or stripes). Behind the glass sits a thin metal sheet punched with holes, the <strong>shadow mask</strong>, and the tube has <strong>three guns</strong>, one for each colour. The geometry is arranged so that the red gun&rsquo;s electrons can only pass through a hole at the angle that lands them on a red dot, and likewise for green and blue: each gun&rsquo;s beam is shadowed from the other colours&rsquo; phosphors. The set therefore needs three voltages at every instant, which is why the receiver turns <em>Y</em>, <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> back into red, green and blue (the 3 &times; 3 matrix inverted at the end of section 24.13) before they reach the guns.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>What you see</th><th>What the three guns do</th></tr>
  <tr><td>black</td><td>all three off: nothing glows</td></tr>
  <tr><td>grey</td><td>all three on equally, at a middling level; the eye blends the dots into grey</td></tr>
  <tr><td>white</td><td>all three on, at full strength</td></tr>
  <tr><td>pure red</td><td>only the red gun on; the others are off</td></tr>
  <tr><td>yellow</td><td>red and green on, blue off (the colours mix in the eye, not on the screen)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So grey is simply <em>colour with the three guns equal</em>, which is exactly the signal in which <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> are zero. That is why one signal can serve both kinds of set: the black-and-white tube&rsquo;s single gun is driven by <em>Y</em>, and the colour tube&rsquo;s three guns are driven by <em>Y</em> plus difference signals that are zero for grey.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>The summary.</strong> A television set is a machine that converts <strong>time into position</strong>. The signal is one waveform; sync pulses keep the clocks of sender and receiver together; the voltage at each instant is the brightness at one point of one line; and colour is a second, hidden wave riding on the first, whose size and timing are the saturation and hue. The next section is that receiver, written as a GPU program.</div>

<h2>24.13 A whole signal chain: how an old NTSC television carried colour</h2>
<p>Everything so far has been one idea at a time. A television set from the 1950s to the 2000s is a complete <em>chain</em> of them: a colour picture is squeezed into <strong>one</strong> wave per scan line (the <em>composite signal</em>), sent down a single wire or through the air, and pulled apart again at the other end. Every step is something from this chapter, and the program <code>ideas/gpu/examples/ntsc.poly</code> runs the chain on the GPU, so you can see which artifacts are real consequences of the maths. (The earlier question &ldquo;what are saturation and hue on an old TV?&rdquo; is answered by the angle and the length in step 2.)</p>
<h3>Step 1: change the colour numbers (linear algebra, section 24.5)</h3>
<p>Red, green and blue are a poor way to send a picture that black-and-white sets must also show. So the signal is first re-expressed as <strong>YIQ</strong>: <em>Y</em> (luma, the brightness: all a black-and-white set reads) and two colour-difference numbers <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em>. It is a plain 3 &times; 3 matrix multiply, so it is <em>linear</em> and exactly reversible:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>Y =  0.299 R + 0.587 G + 0.114 B       (green counts most: the eye is most sensitive to it)
I =  0.596 R - 0.274 G - 0.322 B       (the orange-to-cyan axis)
Q =  0.211 R - 0.523 G + 0.312 B       (the magenta-to-green axis)</code></pre>
<p>For any grey (R = G = B) both <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> are 0: grey has brightness and no colour.</p>
<h3>Step 2: hide the colour in a wave (modulation)</h3>
<p>Treat (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>) as a point on a flat plane. Its <strong>angle</strong> around the centre is the <strong>hue</strong> and its <strong>distance</strong> from the centre is the <strong>saturation</strong>. The set sends that point as a single wave, the <em>colour subcarrier</em>:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>composite = Y + I cos(phase) + Q sin(phase)</code></pre>
<p>Two numbers share one carrier because cosine and sine are a quarter-turn apart (&ldquo;quadrature&rdquo;): they do not disturb each other, as in the dot-product view of section 24.4. Add the two terms and you get one wave <em>R</em> cos(phase &minus; hue), whose <strong>size <em>R</em> = &radic;(<em>I</em>&sup2; + <em>Q</em>&sup2;) is the saturation</strong> and whose <strong>timing shift, its phase, is the hue</strong>. Brightness is the level the wave rides on. That is the whole answer to what hue and saturation <em>are</em> on an NTSC set.</p>
<p>The real subcarrier runs at 3.579545 MHz, which is 227.5 cycles per scan line. The program models it with four samples per cycle (so the phase advances a quarter turn per pixel) and, because 227.5 has a half in it, half a turn per line. In polyir that is one tiny kernel and one more for the encoder:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/ntsc.poly:phase+encode}}</code></pre>
<p>The encoder is the <strong>map</strong> pattern of section 5.5: one thread per pixel, no neighbours, the cheapest shape a GPU has.</p>
<h3>Step 3: take the wave apart (filtering and mixing)</h3>
<p>The receiver sees only <code>composite</code>. It must recover <em>Y</em>, <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> from it, and it does so with two tools from this chapter.</p>
<p><strong>Tool 1: a moving average is a low-pass filter with a notch at the carrier.</strong> Average four neighbouring samples, exactly one subcarrier period. A cosine sampled four times per cycle takes the values <em>c</em>, &minus;<em>s</em>, &minus;<em>c</em>, <em>s</em> (where <em>c</em> and <em>s</em> are the cosine and sine of the starting phase), which add to zero; the sine likewise. So the carrier cancels <em>exactly</em> and only the brightness is left. In the frequency picture (section 24.5, point 6) a 4-sample average multiplies each frequency by <code>sin(4&pi;f / f<sub>s</sub>) / (4 sin(&pi;f / f<sub>s</sub>))</code>, which is 1 at zero frequency and <strong>0 at f<sub>s</sub>/4, the carrier</strong>. The filter was chosen so that its notch lands on the carrier.</p>
<p><strong>Tool 2: multiply, then low-pass (synchronous demodulation).</strong> To pull <em>I</em> out, multiply the composite by <code>cos(phase)</code> and average. Using the identity cos&sup2; = (1 + cos 2&middot;phase) / 2, the <em>I</em> term becomes <em>I</em>/2 (a slow, steady value) plus a wave at twice the carrier frequency (which the same 4-sample average also removes: its other zero is at f<sub>s</sub>/2). The <em>Q</em> term becomes sin &middot; cos = &frac12; sin 2&middot;phase, which averages to nothing. Multiplying by sine does the reverse and recovers <em>Q</em>. Multiplication by a carrier shifts frequencies; the filter then keeps only the part that landed at zero. The two factors of 2 in the code (<code>* 0.5</code> over four terms) undo the halving. This is exactly the lock-in amplifier of electronics, and it is the same trick as the Fourier transform of section 24.4: a dot product with a wave picks out how much of that wave is present.</p>
<h3>Step 4: the two knobs are a rotation and a scale (chapter 12)</h3>
<p>Now (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>) is back, and the front-panel knobs are plain geometry. <strong>Tint</strong> (hue) turns the point around the colour wheel: it is exactly the 2D rotation matrix of section 12.7 (<em>I</em>&prime; = <em>I</em> cos &theta; &minus; <em>Q</em> sin &theta;, <em>Q</em>&prime; = <em>I</em> sin &theta; + <em>Q</em> cos &theta;). <strong>Color</strong> (saturation) multiplies both by one number, a scaling; zero leaves only <em>Y</em>, a black-and-white picture. Finally the inverse matrix turns YIQ back into red, green and blue for the screen. The whole receiver, in polyir:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/ntsc.poly:nearColumn+decode}}</code></pre>
<p>The decoder is a <strong>stencil</strong> (section 6.3): each thread reads four neighbouring samples of its own scan line and writes one pixel. It is a convolution with a 4-tap filter, the same operation as the blur of section 17.8 and the reverb of section 25, so the GPU techniques carry over unchanged. Because every scan line is independent, a line is one row of threads.</p>
<h3>What the picture shows</h3>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/ntsc-1-source.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">source: eight colour bars, then fine black-and-white stripes</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/ntsc-2-composite.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">the composite signal as a black-and-white set would show it: colour is a fine checkerboard (dot crawl)</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/ntsc-3-decoded.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">decoded: bars intact, fringes at the edges, rainbow in the stripes</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/ntsc-4-tint.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">tint turned 40 degrees: every hue moves round the wheel by the same angle</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure @ntsc. The NTSC chain on the GPU (160 &times; 120 pixels). Top right: the checkerboard in the coloured bars is the subcarrier itself; a plain grey area (the white bar) has none, because grey has no colour wave. Bottom left: the middle of each bar decodes to its exact source colour; the fringes are the 4-sample window straddling an edge; and stripes only three pixels apart, a frequency of f<sub>s</sub>/3, sit so close to the carrier at f<sub>s</sub>/4 that the decoder mistakes brightness for colour and paints a rainbow.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>These are the famous flaws of analogue television, and they are not bugs in the program but consequences of the design. <strong>Cross-colour</strong> (rainbows on fine patterns, such as a presenter's striped tie) and <strong>dot crawl</strong> (the checkerboard that crawls along edges) happen because brightness and colour share <em>one</em> signal and a filter cannot perfectly tell them apart where their frequencies overlap. <strong>Colour smear</strong> happens because the colour part is low-passed. Real NTSC deliberately sent <em>Y</em> with the full bandwidth (about 4.2 MHz) and gave <em>I</em> about 1.5 MHz and <em>Q</em> only about 0.5 MHz, because the eye resolves colour detail much more coarsely than brightness detail (the same reasoning behind JPEG's chroma subsampling today). The program uses one 4-tap filter for all three, a simplification. It also does not model the &ldquo;burst&rdquo; (a few cycles of reference carrier at the start of each line that the real receiver locks to) nor interlacing; the tint knob exists because that lock could drift, so the hue was only as good as the phase reference. PAL television flipped the phase every other line so that such errors cancelled, trading a more complex receiver for stable colour.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>How it was checked.</strong> The GPU composite matches the same kernel run on the CPU at every pixel (within 0.001); the decoded picture matches the CPU decode at every third pixel (within 0.01, since the GPU's <code>sin</code> and <code>cos</code> round differently); and the middle of each of the eight bars decodes to its source colour (within 0.02), which tests the maths rather than the CPU/GPU agreement. As a sabotage check, changing the luma weight from 0.25 to 0.30 makes the program exit with the code 13 (&ldquo;the GPU and CPU disagree&rdquo; is how the first check reports it). The program prints no numbers; it only saves the pictures and returns an exit code.</div>
<h3>Where the same chain turns up</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Idea in the chain</th><th>Where else you meet it</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Change to luma plus two colour differences</td><td>JPEG, MPEG and H.264 video (YCbCr); the colour spaces of section 22</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Throw away colour detail the eye cannot see</td><td>chroma subsampling in every video file (4:2:0); the 1.5 MHz and 0.5 MHz of <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Carry two signals on one wave using sine and cosine</td><td>Wi-Fi, 4G and 5G, cable modems and Bluetooth (QAM and phase modulation)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Multiply by a carrier, then low-pass</td><td>software-defined radio, lock-in amplifiers, radar, ultrasound imaging</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A short moving-average filter with a zero at one frequency</td><td>comb and notch filters: removing mains hum, de-interlacing, and the 4-tap blur of section 17.8</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rotate (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>) by an angle</td><td>the hue slider in photo editors; phase-shifting in audio effects</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One thread per pixel, a stencil on a scan line</td><td>CRT and retro-game shaders (&ldquo;NTSC filters&rdquo; in emulators run exactly this on the GPU), image sensors, video scalers</td></tr>
</table>
<p>For a GPU, this chain is attractive for the reason the whole chapter keeps returning to: both stages are <em>independent per pixel</em> with a tiny, fixed neighbourhood, so a 1080p frame is two million threads that never talk to each other, and a software television can run at many times real time. Exercise: average eight samples (two carrier periods) instead of four: in <code>decode</code> make the loop run to 8 and halve the two weights (0.25 to 0.125, 0.5 to 0.25), and in <code>nearColumn</code> change <code>k - 2</code> to <code>k - 4</code>. Predict first what happens to the colour smear and to the stripes, then run it.</p>

<h2>24.14 Colour as a signal: viewing and manipulating it with DSP</h2>
<p>Section 24.13 split a colour into brightness and two colour numbers because a television had to. That split turns out to be the most useful idea in all of colour processing, for editing as much as for broadcasting. This section shows how to <em>look at</em> colour as signals and how to <em>change</em> it, using only tools already in this chapter. The kernels are in <code>ideas/gpu/examples/shared/colour.poly</code>, run through Metal by <code>colour.poly</code> and through Vulkan by <code>colour-vulkan.poly</code>; every picture below was produced by them on the GPU.</p>
<h3>Colour is three signals, not one</h3>
<p>A colour picture is three grids of numbers (red, green, blue), so each channel is a 2D signal and everything in this chapter applies to each: they have spectra, they can be blurred, sampled, aliased. But <strong>RGB is a poor space to edit in</strong>, because the things a person asks for do not line up with its axes. &ldquo;Make it brighter&rdquo; moves all three numbers; &ldquo;make it less green&rdquo; changes the apparent brightness too; &ldquo;shift the hue&rdquo; changes all three in a way you cannot write down without trigonometry. The remedy is the linear-algebra move of section 24.5: <strong>change the basis</strong> to one whose axes match the questions. YIQ is one such basis (the same 3 &times; 3 matrix as the television&rsquo;s); the hue/saturation/value family and Lab are others. Then each edit becomes a simple operation on one or two numbers.</p>
<h3>Viewing colour: three ways to look at the same picture</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>View</th><th>What it shows</th><th>How</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Luma only</strong></td><td>The black-and-white picture: all the structure the eye uses to find edges and shapes</td><td>show <em>Y</em> in all three channels</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Chroma only</strong></td><td>The colour with the brightness thrown away</td><td>fix <em>Y</em> at one middle value and show <em>I</em>, <em>Q</em></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Vectorscope</strong></td><td>Which colours are present and how strongly, ignoring where they are</td><td>plot every pixel as a point at (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The vectorscope is the colourist&rsquo;s main instrument, and it is the colour wheel of section 24.13 made into a histogram. Angle around the centre is hue; distance from the centre is saturation; grey pixels pile up at the very middle. Because editing a colour is a rotation or a scaling of this plane, you can <em>see</em> an edit as a geometric movement of the cloud: a hue change turns it, a saturation change shrinks or stretches it. A scope also shows <em>what a picture cannot show you</em>: whether it has a colour cast (the cloud sits off-centre), whether it is washed out (a small cloud), and whether anything is out of gamut.</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-1-source.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">source: hue sweeps across, saturation grows downward; stripes and a disc add brightness detail</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-2-luma.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">luma only: the shapes survive, the colour is gone</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-3-hue-only.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">chroma only, at constant brightness: the stripes and the disc almost vanish, because they were brightness</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure @colourviews. The same picture as brightness and as colour. Shapes live in the luma; the chroma is a smooth wash.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-scopes.pgm:2}}" alt="three vectorscope panels" style="width:70%; border:1px solid var(--rule); display:block; margin:auto;">
<figcaption>Figure @scopes. Vectorscopes of the test picture (I across, Q up; the vertical line marks I = 0). Left: the source, a ring (every hue, saturation growing downward in the picture, so a thick ring). Middle: after a 60&deg; hue rotation, the same ring turned. Right: after scaling the colour to 0, every point has collapsed to the centre: a grey picture.</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3>Editing colour: pointwise operations are a matrix</h3>
<p>A hue change is a <strong>rotation of (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>)</strong> and a saturation change is a <strong>scaling of (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>)</strong>; brightness is added to or multiplied into <em>Y</em>. These are the rotation and scaling matrices of chapter 12, applied to a 2D plane:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>[ I' ]   [ cos &theta;  -sin &theta; ] [ I ]          hue: turn the point by the angle &theta;
[ Q' ] = [ sin &theta;   cos &theta; ] [ Q ]

[ I' ]   [ s  0 ] [ I ]                   saturation: stretch the point away from the centre by s
[ Q' ] = [ 0  s ] [ Q ]                   (s = 0: grey, s = 1: unchanged, s &gt; 1: more vivid)</code></pre>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/colour.poly:rotateHue+scaleColour}}</code></pre>
<p>Both edits touch each pixel on its own, with no neighbours, so this is the <strong>map</strong> pattern again, the cheapest thing a GPU does. And because every step (convert to YIQ, rotate, scale, convert back) is linear, the whole edit collapses into <em>one</em> 3 &times; 3 matrix acting on (red, green, blue). Photo editors compute that single matrix once and apply it to every pixel; a video effect that adjusts saturation in real time is just that. Rotating by a full turn must change nothing, and scaling by zero must give a grey picture: the program checks both, and they are a quick test of any colour code you write.</p>
<h3>Editing colour: neighbourhood operations are filters</h3>
<p>Blurring and sharpening are the convolutions of section 24.7. The new question colour adds is <em>which channel</em> to filter, and the answer is where the eye&rsquo;s lopsidedness pays off. The eye resolves brightness detail about as finely as the picture allows but resolves colour detail much more coarsely. So two filters of the same strength look completely different:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/colour.poly:chromaBlur+lumaBlur}}</code></pre>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-1-source.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">source</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-6-chroma-blur.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">only the colour blurred (9 &times; 9 average): hardly any change</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-7-luma-blur.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">only the brightness blurred, same filter: the stripes are smeared and the disc&rsquo;s edge softened</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure @colourblur. The same 9 &times; 9 average applied to the colour channels versus the brightness channel.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>This asymmetry is used everywhere. <strong>Chroma subsampling</strong> (the &ldquo;4:2:0&rdquo; of JPEG, DVD, Blu-ray, streaming video) stores the two colour channels at a quarter of the resolution of the brightness, throwing away half the data of the picture with little visible loss. <strong>Chroma denoising</strong> blurs colour noise strongly while leaving the brightness alone, because colour noise is ugly and cheap to remove. And the reverse is a warning: never blur the luma of something you want to keep sharp. The program checks the key property: a chroma blur must not change any pixel&rsquo;s brightness, at every sampled pixel.</p>
<p><strong>Sharpening</strong> is a high-boost filter. The <em>unsharp mask</em> computes <code>Y + k (Y &minus; blur(Y))</code>: the difference between the picture and its blurred copy is the high-frequency part, and adding more of it raises the response at fine detail to 1 + <em>k</em>(1 &minus; <em>H</em>), where <em>H</em> is the blur&rsquo;s response (section 24.5, point 6). Doing this to <em>Y</em> only has a practical reason. Sharpening red, green and blue separately overshoots each channel on its own at a colour edge, and the overshoots do not line up: you get bright coloured halos. Sharpening the luma alone changes the brightness profile of the edge and leaves the colour exactly where it was:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/colour.poly:sharpenLuma}}</code></pre>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-4-rotate-60.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">hue turned 60&deg;: the whole sweep moves round the wheel</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/colour-8-sharpen-luma.ppm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);image-rendering:pixelated;"><div class="k">luma sharpened (k = 1.5): crisper stripes and disc edge, colours unchanged</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure @colouredit. Two more edits from the program: a pointwise one (hue) and a neighbourhood one (sharpening).</figcaption>
</figure>
<h3>Colour in the frequency domain</h3>
<p>Take the Fourier transform of each of the three channels of a natural photograph and a pattern appears: <em>Y</em> has plenty of energy at high frequencies (edges, texture, text), while <em>I</em> and <em>Q</em> are overwhelmingly low-frequency (smooth washes of colour that change slowly across an object). That is the reason the television could give <em>Q</em> only about 0.5 MHz and the reason 4:2:0 works: you can cut away the high frequencies of a channel when it has few. It is also why a colour <strong>edit that looks local can be done in the frequency domain</strong> as well: scaling a channel&rsquo;s spectrum by a function of frequency <em>is</em> filtering it (section 24.8), whether you call that a blur, a sharpen or a band-pass. (The test picture here is built to make the point, not to be a photograph: its stripes and disc are pure brightness by construction.)</p>
<h3>Three cautions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gamma.</strong> Pictures are stored gamma-encoded (section 22). The television pipeline did these edits on the encoded values, and so do most quick tools, but a physically correct blur or hue shift converts to linear light first and encodes afterwards. The two differ visibly on strong edges and saturated colours. The program edits encoded values for simplicity, as TV did.</li>
<li><strong>Gamut.</strong> Rotating or boosting colour can push red, green or blue outside 0 to 1. The program keeps the raw numbers and the picture writer clamps them; a careful tool would choose how to bring them back (clip, compress).</li>
<li><strong>YIQ is one choice.</strong> It is not perceptually uniform: equal steps in <em>I</em> or <em>Q</em> are not equal steps to the eye. For hue shifts it is good enough; for judging &ldquo;how different are two colours&rdquo; use a perceptual space such as Lab.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The same ideas in tools you may have used</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Operation</th><th>DSP idea</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Hue / saturation sliders</td><td>rotate and scale the (<em>I</em>, <em>Q</em>)-like plane (a pointwise matrix)</td><td>every photo editor; the &ldquo;colour&rdquo; and &ldquo;tint&rdquo; knobs of section 24.13</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vectorscope, waveform monitor</td><td>histograms of the colour plane and of the luma</td><td>video colour grading, broadcast engineering</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chroma subsampling, chroma denoise</td><td>low-pass only the channels the eye cannot resolve</td><td>JPEG, H.264, camera sensors</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unsharp mask on luma</td><td>high-boost filter, on one channel</td><td>image sharpening in phones and RAW converters</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chroma key (&ldquo;green screen&rdquo;)</td><td>threshold on distance in the colour plane, then soften the mask</td><td>weather forecasts, film compositing</td></tr>
  <tr><td>White balance</td><td>scale the three channels by three numbers (best done in linear light)</td><td>cameras and editors</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><strong>How it was checked.</strong> Each of the seven kernels runs on the GPU and is compared with the same kernel on the CPU at every 7th pixel (within 0.01); this was done for both the Metal and the Vulkan program (the Vulkan one also with the shaders written directly as SPIR-V, without glslang). Three properties of the maths are checked as well: a chroma blur leaves every sampled pixel&rsquo;s brightness unchanged, a colour scale of 0 gives pixels with equal red, green and blue, and a full-turn hue rotation returns the source. Making the chroma blur add 0.05 to the brightness makes the program exit with code 14, so the property check can fail. The vectorscopes are drawn on the CPU, since plotting points into a histogram is a scatter (section 13.6) and the point here is the colour maths. The claims about the pictures were measured on the saved files: the stripes&rsquo; brightness contrast is 0.32 in the source, 0.32 after the chroma blur and 0.12 after the luma blur; in the chroma-only view it falls to 0.04 (the remainder is where the clamp to 0 to 1 cut off a colour); and in the vectorscopes the source and rotated panels both form a ring of radius about 14 to 49 points, while the grey panel has every point at the centre.</div>

<h2>24.15 Try it yourself</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Experiment</th><th>What to change in <code>dsp.poly</code></th></tr>
  <tr><td>Add a third wave</td><td>in <code>twoSines</code>, add <code>0.3 * sinf(6.2831853 * 40.0 * t)</code> and look for a new spike at 40</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Watch aliasing move</td><td>change 50 to 40, then 60 in <code>tooFast</code>: the false frequency becomes 24, then 4 (always 64 minus the true one)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A longer blur</td><td>spread <code>blurFilter</code> over 5 samples (0.2 each): the low-pass gets narrower</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Different stripes</td><td>change the <code>across</code> and <code>down</code> arguments in <code>spectra2D</code> and predict where the dots will land before running</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Edit colour (<code>colour.poly</code>)</td><td>blur luma by 1 and chroma by 6 and compare with the opposite; or change <code>sharpenLuma</code> to sharpen R, G and B separately and look for coloured halos at the disc edge</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Change the television (<code>ntsc.poly</code>)</td><td>average 8 samples instead of 4 (the loop in <code>decode</code>, its two weights, and the offset in <code>nearColumn</code>), or set the tint to 3.14159 and watch the hues turn to their opposites</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Check the DFT is lossless</td><td>(harder) apply the same sum with the signs of the sine terms reversed to the spectrum and recover the signal</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ 23 -->
<h1 id="sound">25. Sound: signals you can hear</h1>

<p>Section 24 treated pictures as signals. <strong>Sound is the most natural signal there is</strong>: a list of numbers that the speaker turns into motion of the air. This section explains, from the beginning, what those numbers are, why the <em>sine function</em> is the heart of every tone, what the <em>sign function</em> is and how it differs, and what happens when waves are added together, which is the whole secret of chords, instruments and synthesizers. The program <code>ideas/gpu/examples/audio.poly</code> builds sounds on the GPU, checks their frequencies with the Fourier transform of section 24.4, writes them as WAV files and, when you ask, plays them through the speakers with a small audio runtime written in polyir (<code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>).</p>

<h2>25.1 What sound is</h2>
<p>When a guitar string, a loudspeaker cone or your vocal cords move, they push the air next to them, which pushes the air next to that, and a wave of slightly higher and lower air pressure travels outward. Your ear (or a microphone) feels the pressure at one spot rise and fall. <strong>Plot that pressure against time and you have the sound's waveform.</strong> A microphone turns the pressure into a voltage; a computer measures that voltage at evenly spaced instants and stores the numbers. That is digital audio:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Idea</th><th>In plain terms</th><th>Typical value</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>sample</strong></td><td>one measurement of the pressure curve's height</td><td>a number from &minus;1 to +1 (or a 16-bit whole number)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>sample rate</strong></td><td>how many samples are taken each second</td><td>44,100 per second for CDs, 48,000 for video, 8,000 for old telephones</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>time of sample <em>i</em></strong></td><td><code>t = i / rate</code> seconds</td><td>sample 441 at 44,100 Hz is 0.01 s</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>channel</strong></td><td>one independent signal; stereo has two (left and right)</td><td>1 for mono, 2 for stereo</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>bit depth</strong></td><td>how finely each height is stored</td><td>16 bits: 65,536 levels, about 96 decibels between the quietest and loudest</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Why 44,100? The <strong>Nyquist limit</strong> of section 24.6 says a signal sampled <em>S</em> times a second can only hold frequencies below <em>S</em>/2. People hear up to about 20,000 Hz, so a rate a little above 40,000 captures everything audible: 44,100 gives a limit of 22,050 Hz.</p>

<h2>25.2 Pitch, loudness and timbre: the three things you hear</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>You hear</th><th>It is the wave's</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>pitch</strong> (how high a note is)</td><td><strong>frequency</strong>: how many cycles per second, in hertz (Hz)</td><td>A above middle C is 440 Hz; middle C is 261.63 Hz; doubling the frequency raises the note one <em>octave</em></td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>loudness</strong></td><td><strong>amplitude</strong>: how tall the wave is</td><td>perceived on a logarithmic scale: doubling the amplitude adds about 6 decibels</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>timbre</strong> (why a violin and a flute differ on the same note)</td><td>the wave's <strong>shape</strong>, equivalently its <strong>spectrum</strong>: which frequencies are mixed in</td><td>a flute is close to a pure sine; a violin has many strong harmonics</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A piano keyboard divides the octave into 12 equal steps (<em>semitones</em>), each one multiplying the frequency by the same factor, <code>2<sup>1/12</sup> = 1.05946</code>, so twelve steps double it. For the key with MIDI number <code>m</code> (middle C is 60, A above it 69): <code>f = 440 &middot; 2<sup>(m &minus; 69)/12</sup></code>. Check: <code>m = 60</code> gives <code>440 &middot; 2<sup>&minus;0.75</sup> = 261.63</code> Hz. &#10003; Every note in the program's melody comes from that rule.</p>

<h2>25.3 The sine function, from scratch</h2>
<p>The <strong>sine</strong> is the most important curve in all of signal processing, and it has a lovely physical meaning. Picture a point moving around a circle of radius 1 at a perfectly steady speed, like a dot on a spinning wheel. Look at the circle from the side, so only the dot's <em>height</em> counts. As the dot goes round, its height rises from 0 to 1, falls through 0 to &minus;1, and comes back to 0. <strong>That height, plotted against how far round the dot has turned, is the sine.</strong> (Its sibling, the <em>cosine</em>, is the dot's distance from the middle sideways.)</p>
<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 215" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="circle unrolled into a sine wave">
  <defs><marker id="s1" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10z" fill="#586069"/></marker></defs>
  <circle cx="90" cy="105" r="65" fill="#f6f8fa" stroke="#586069"/>
  <line x1="20" y1="105" x2="170" y2="105" stroke="#d0d7de"/><line x1="90" y1="30" x2="90" y2="180" stroke="#d0d7de"/>
  <line x1="90" y1="105" x2="136" y2="59" stroke="#0b5cad" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="136" cy="59" r="5" fill="#0b5cad"/>
  <line x1="136" y1="59" x2="136" y2="105" stroke="#9a6700" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/>
  <path d="M110 105 A20 20 0 0 0 104 91" stroke="#0b5cad" fill="none"/><text x="118" y="98" font-size="11" fill="#0b5cad">&theta;</text>
  <text x="150" y="84" font-size="11" fill="#9a6700">sin&theta;</text>
  <line x1="136" y1="59" x2="260" y2="59" stroke="#d0d7de" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
  <g stroke="#586069"><line x1="260" y1="105" x2="680" y2="105" marker-end="url(#s1)"/><line x1="260" y1="25" x2="260" y2="185"/></g>
  <path d="M260 105 C 290 45, 320 45, 365 105 S 440 165, 470 165 S 520 105, 575 105" fill="none" stroke="none"/>
  <path d="M260 105 Q 295 35 330 105 T 400 105 T 470 105 T 540 105 T 610 105 T 680 105" fill="none" stroke="#0b5cad" stroke-width="2.5"/>
  <circle cx="260" cy="59" r="0"/><g font-size="11" fill="#586069"><text x="236" y="109">0</text><text x="240" y="62">1</text><text x="233" y="170">&minus;1</text><text x="640" y="125">angle (or time) &rarr;</text></g>
  <text x="350" y="200" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">Unroll the circle: the height of the turning point, plotted as it goes round, is the sine wave. One full turn = one cycle.</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 48. The sine: the height of a point circling at a steady speed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A few values (angles are measured in <strong>radians</strong> in programs, where a full turn is 2&pi; = 6.2832 radians; in degrees a full turn is 360&deg;):</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Angle</th><th>0&deg;</th><th>30&deg;</th><th>45&deg;</th><th>90&deg;</th><th>180&deg;</th><th>270&deg;</th><th>360&deg;</th></tr>
  <tr><th>Radians</th><td>0</td><td>&pi;/6</td><td>&pi;/4</td><td>&pi;/2</td><td>&pi;</td><td>3&pi;/2</td><td>2&pi;</td></tr>
  <tr><th>sine</th><td>0</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.707</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>&minus;1</td><td>0</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A pure tone is one sine wave with three settings:</p>
<pre><code>x(t)  =  A &middot; sin( 2&pi; &middot; f &middot; t  +  &phi; )

A  amplitude   how tall (loudness)           f  frequency   cycles per second (pitch)           &phi;  phase   where in its cycle it starts</code></pre>
<p>The factor <code>2&pi; &middot; f &middot; t</code> is the angle the dot has turned after <code>t</code> seconds if it makes <code>f</code> full turns per second. In the program, sample number <code>i</code> is computed as exactly this formula with <code>t = i / rate</code>. The Wave library measures the angle in <em>whole cycles</em> (so a full turn is 1, not 2&pi;); <code>fract</code> keeps only the part after the decimal point, because a wave repeats, and <code>sine</code> multiplies by 2&pi; and takes the sine:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:stdlib/Math.poly:fract}}</code></pre>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Wave.poly:sine}}</code></pre>
<p>and the additive synthesizer that sums several of them with an envelope:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/audio.poly:17:35}}</code></pre>

<h2>25.4 Why sine? Four reasons</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>It is what vibrating things do.</strong> Pull a mass on a spring away from rest and let go: the spring pulls back with a force proportional to how far it was pulled (&ldquo;the more you stretch it, the harder it pulls&rdquo;). That single rule makes the mass swing back and forth following exactly a sine wave. A tuning fork, a guitar string (to a good approximation), a pendulum swinging through small angles and the air in a bottle all follow the same rule, and so they all make sines. (In calculus words: the sine is the function whose acceleration is always minus itself times a constant, <code>y&Prime; = &minus;&omega;&sup2;y</code>.)</li>
  <li><strong>It is the projection of steady circular motion</strong> (Figure 48): the simplest motion that repeats forever.</li>
  <li><strong>A sine is the one wave that passes through any linear system unchanged in shape.</strong> Put a sine into a loudspeaker, a room, a guitar-amplifier circuit, or the ear canal and what comes out is <em>a sine of the same frequency</em>, just taller or shorter and shifted in time. No other shape has this property. In linear-algebra language (section 24.5), <strong>sines are the eigenvectors of every filter</strong>, which is why the frequency view makes filters easy to reason about.</li>
  <li><strong>Your ear works in sines.</strong> Inside the cochlea of the ear, different positions respond to different frequencies, so the ear is a built-in Fourier analyser: a tone sounds like one pure pitch because it excites one spot, and a chord sounds like several notes because it excites several.</li>
</ol>
<p>And because different sines are <em>perpendicular</em> to each other (their dot product is zero, section 24.5, point 2), a sound made of many sines can be taken apart again without confusion. That is exactly what the program's frequency check does.</p>

<h2>25.5 &ldquo;Sine&rdquo; or &ldquo;sign&rdquo;? Two different functions</h2>
<p>The words sound alike and both come up in this section, so here are both. <strong>Sine</strong>, <code>sin(x)</code>, is the smooth wave above. The <strong>sign function</strong> (also written <em>signum</em>), <code>sign(x)</code>, answers a much simpler question: <em>is the number negative, zero or positive?</em></p>
<pre><code>sign(x) =  -1   if x &lt; 0
            0   if x = 0
           +1   if x &gt; 0</code></pre>
<p>It throws away how big the number is and keeps only which side of zero it is on (the same &ldquo;which side&rdquo; test as the edge function of section 20.2). Combine the two and something instructive appears: <strong><code>sign(sin(x))</code> is a square wave</strong>, a wave that jumps between &minus;1 and +1 every half cycle. It sounds buzzy and hollow, like an old video game. Because a square wave is not a sine, it must be a <em>mixture</em> of sines, and the Fourier transform says exactly which: only the <strong>odd</strong> multiples of the base frequency (1&times;, 3&times;, 5&times; ...), with heights <code>4/&pi;</code>, <code>(4/&pi;)/3</code>, <code>(4/&pi;)/5</code> ... that is 1.273, 0.424, 0.255, 0.182 ... The program makes the square wave in one line (<code>sign(turns(...))</code>) and has the GPU find the harmonics:</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-4-square.pgm:2}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">sign(sin(2&pi; &middot; 100 &middot; t)): a square wave</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-4-square-spectrum.pgm:2}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">its spectrum: spikes at 100, 300, 500, 700 Hz, heights 1.27, 0.42, 0.25, 0.18</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 49. The sign of a sine, and the harmonics the GPU found in it (the program checks the first two against 4/&pi; and 4/(3&pi;)).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Where the sign function turns up elsewhere: <em>clipping</em> a sound too loud for the speaker flattens its peaks toward a square wave (the harsh distortion of an overdriven guitar is exactly this); <em>one-bit audio</em> converters store only the sign of a rapidly sampled signal; counting how often a signal's sign flips (<em>zero crossings</em>) is a quick way to estimate its pitch; and in graphics, the sign of a determinant decides whether a triangle faces the camera.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:stdlib/Math.poly:sign}}</code></pre>

<h2>25.6 Combining waveforms: just add the numbers</h2>
<p>Here is the most useful fact about sound: <strong>when two sounds happen at once, the air pressure is the <em>sum</em> of the two pressures.</strong> This is called <em>superposition</em>, and it means mixing sounds is nothing but adding their samples, number by number. Multiplying by a constant changes the volume. Those two operations, adding and scaling, are exactly the vector operations of section 12.1: a sound is a vector, and mixing is vector addition. Everything below is some way of adding or multiplying waves.</p>

<h3>Chords and harmony</h3>
<p>Play three notes at once and their waves add. Whether the result sounds pleasant depends on the <strong>ratios</strong> of the frequencies. Notes whose frequencies are in a simple ratio have harmonics that line up and reinforce each other:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Interval</th><th>Frequency ratio</th><th>Example from C (261.63 Hz)</th><th>Equal-tempered value</th></tr>
  <tr><td>octave</td><td>2 : 1</td><td>C &rarr; C&prime; 523.25 Hz</td><td>exactly 2</td></tr>
  <tr><td>perfect fifth</td><td>3 : 2</td><td>C &rarr; G 392.44 Hz (piano: 392.00)</td><td>2<sup>7/12</sup> = 1.4983</td></tr>
  <tr><td>perfect fourth</td><td>4 : 3</td><td>C &rarr; F 348.8 Hz (piano: 349.23)</td><td>2<sup>5/12</sup> = 1.3348</td></tr>
  <tr><td>major third</td><td>5 : 4</td><td>C &rarr; E 327.0 Hz (piano: 329.63)</td><td>2<sup>4/12</sup> = 1.2599</td></tr>
  <tr><td>minor third</td><td>6 : 5</td><td>C &rarr; E&flat; 313.9 Hz (piano: 311.13)</td><td>2<sup>3/12</sup> = 1.1892</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A major chord is a root, a major third and a fifth, roughly in the ratios <strong>4 : 5 : 6</strong>. The analysis in the program uses exactly that: 400, 500 and 600 Hz with heights 1, 0.8 and 0.6. The waveform looks like an unrecognisable tangle, but its spectrum is three clean spikes, which the GPU's transform recovers:</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-2-chord.pgm:2}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">three tones added: a complicated-looking wave</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-2-chord-spectrum.pgm:2}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">its spectrum: three spikes at 400, 500, 600 Hz</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 50. Adding waves hides the notes in the waveform, but the spectrum shows them plainly.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>(A piano is tuned to <em>equal temperament</em> rather than the pure ratios: a slight compromise so that every key sounds acceptable, at the cost of making every interval except the octave very slightly impure.)</p>

<h3>Beats: two nearly equal tones</h3>
<p>Add two sines of almost the same frequency, say 400 Hz and 404 Hz. Sometimes their peaks line up (loud), sometimes a peak meets a trough (they cancel, quiet), and the pattern repeats at the <em>difference</em>, 4 times a second. The sum-to-product identity of trigonometry says why:</p>
<pre><code>sin(a) + sin(b)  =  2 &middot; sin( (a + b) / 2 )  &middot;  cos( (a &minus; b) / 2 )
                         the average pitch         a slow wave that sets the loudness</code></pre>
<p>The result is a tone at the <em>average</em> frequency (402 Hz) whose volume is multiplied by a slow cosine that swells and fades at the difference frequency. You hear it as a throbbing: <strong>beats</strong>. Piano tuners listen for beats between two strings; when the throbbing slows to nothing, the strings agree.</p>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-3-beats.pgm:2}}" style="width:65%;border:1px solid var(--rule); display:block; margin:auto;">
<figcaption>Figure 51. 400 Hz plus 404 Hz over a quarter second: the wave swells, nearly vanishes in the middle and swells again, once per 1/4 second: 4 beats per second.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Building any repeating shape: additive synthesis</h3>
<p>Fourier's discovery, in action: <strong>any repeating wave can be built by adding sines whose frequencies are whole-number multiples (<em>harmonics</em>) of one base frequency.</strong> Different recipes make different shapes, and different shapes sound different (timbre):</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Wave</th><th>Recipe (x = the angle)</th><th>Sounds like</th></tr>
  <tr><td>square</td><td><code>(4/&pi;) &middot; [ sin x + sin 3x / 3 + sin 5x / 5 + ... ]</code> (odd harmonics, each divided by its number)</td><td>hollow, buzzy (clarinet-like, video games)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>sawtooth</td><td><code>(2/&pi;) &middot; [ sin x &minus; sin 2x / 2 + sin 3x / 3 &minus; ... ]</code> (all harmonics, signs alternating)</td><td>bright, brassy, string-like</td></tr>
  <tr><td>triangle</td><td><code>(8/&pi;&sup2;) &middot; [ sin x &minus; sin 3x / 9 + sin 5x / 25 &minus; ... ]</code> (odd harmonics, divided by the number <em>squared</em>)</td><td>soft, flute-like</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The more harmonics you add, the sharper the corners become. Here is the square wave built from 1, 3 and 9 sine waves (the program computes each with a GPU kernel, <code>partialSum</code>):</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:8px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-5-square-1.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">1 harmonic: a plain sine</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-5-square-3.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">first 3 odd harmonics: starting to square off</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-5-square-9.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">9 harmonics: nearly square, with overshoot at the corners</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 52. Square wave by adding sines. The little ears at each jump are the <em>Gibbs overshoot</em>: about 9% of the jump height, however many harmonics are added.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:8px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-6-sawtooth-9.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">sawtooth from 9 harmonics</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-6-triangle-9.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">triangle from 9 harmonics (already almost perfect: its terms fall off as 1/number&sup2;)</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 53. The other classic shapes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/audio.poly:42:69}}</code></pre>
<p>This is <em>additive synthesis</em>, the principle of the pipe organ (every stop adds a harmonic) and of early electronic instruments. A real instrument's character is its particular mix of harmonics, and how they rise and fall over the length of a note.</p>

<h3>Shaping a note: the envelope</h3>
<p>A note that starts and stops instantly makes a click (the wave jumps from 0 to full size). Real notes have an <strong>envelope</strong>: a slow curve multiplied on to the wave, rising during the <em>attack</em>, falling during the <em>release</em> (full ADSR envelopes add a <em>decay</em> and a <em>sustain</em> level). A pluck has a fast attack and long release; a bowed string, the opposite. Multiplication, not addition, is the operation here: the envelope scales the volume moment by moment.</p>

<h3>Modulation: multiplying waves</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Technique</th><th>What is multiplied or added</th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>tremolo</strong></td><td>the amplitude times a slow sine (about 5 Hz)</td><td>a shimmering volume</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>vibrato</strong> (frequency modulation)</td><td>a slow sine added to the wave's <em>phase</em>: <code>sin(2&pi; f t + m &middot; sin(2&pi; v t))</code></td><td>the pitch wobbles like a singer's. The <code>synth</code> kernel has this built in (settings 5 and 6)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>FM synthesis</strong></td><td>the same, but with a fast modulator (audio rate) and a large depth</td><td>rich, bell-like tones from just two sines (the basis of famous 1980s keyboards)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>AM and FM radio</strong></td><td>a sound wave modulates a very fast carrier wave</td><td>sound sent through the air at radio frequencies</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Cancelling: adding a wave to its opposite</h3>
<p>Add a wave to a copy turned upside down and the sum is exactly zero: <code>sin(x) + sin(x + &pi;) = 0</code>. <strong>Noise-cancelling headphones</strong> do this: a microphone listens to the noise, the electronics make its mirror image, and the speaker plays it, so the noise and its inverse add up to near silence at your ear. Cancelling is the extreme case of the same idea behind beats: waves meeting peak-to-trough.</p>

<h3>Filtering and reverb</h3>
<p>Because a sound is a sum of sines and a filter treats each sine separately (section 24.5, point 6), you can reshape a sound by scaling frequencies: turn the bass up (an equalizer), cut the hiss (a low-pass filter). Echo and room reverb are the same filter idea in another form: the sound is <em>convolved</em> with a recording of the room's response to a single click, so every moment of the sound is repeated, quieter and delayed, thousands of times over. (Section 24.5: filtering is a matrix; convolution is its sliding-sum form.)</p>

<h3>Mixing, and the danger of too much</h3>
<p>Adding waves adds their heights: two full-size tones sum to a wave twice as tall, and mixing ten instruments can easily exceed the range &minus;1 to +1 that the speaker can reproduce. The numbers then get <em>clipped</em> to the limit, flattening the peaks, which adds harsh harmonics (the sign-function effect of 25.5). The cures: scale each part down before adding (the program multiplies the chord by 0.25 for three tones), or use a <em>limiter</em> that gently turns the volume down when the sum gets large.</p>

<h2>25.6a The Wave library: the first piece of waveforms inside polyir</h2>
<p>The oscillators and helpers used above are not special to this one program: they are collected in a small library, <code>ideas/audio/Wave.poly</code>, the first step of the roadmap item &ldquo;waveforms as part of polyir&rdquo; (section 30). It is one of the libraries under review in <code>ideas/</code>, not part of the standard library: a program imports it (<code>import "../../ideas/audio/Wave.poly"</code>, the path relative to the importing file). Its four general helpers, <code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code> and <code>mix</code>, are the standard library&rsquo;s <code>core/stdlib/Math.poly</code>, which Wave.poly imports and which is <strong>built into the language</strong> (below). Every function is a <code>kernel func</code> on <code>float</code>s, so each one runs on the CPU, runs inside GPU kernels, is checked by the kernel rules, and is covered by unit tests:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Function</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sine(phase)</code></td><td>smooth wave; <code>phase</code> is in whole cycles, so a note is <code>sine(f * t)</code></td><td><code>sine(0.25) = 1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>square(phase)</code></td><td>+1 for the first half of a cycle, &minus;1 for the second</td><td><code>square(0.75) = &minus;1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sawtooth(phase)</code></td><td>rises from &minus;1 to +1 across the cycle</td><td><code>sawtooth(0.75) = 0.5</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>triangle(phase)</code></td><td>rises over half a cycle, falls over the other half</td><td><code>triangle(0.5) = 1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>noise(seed)</code></td><td>a repeatable pseudo-random number from &minus;1 to +1</td><td>hiss, texture</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>sign(x)</code></td><td>&minus;1, 0 or +1</td><td><code>sign(&minus;3) = &minus;1</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>fract(x)</code></td><td>the part after the decimal point (always 0 up to 1)</td><td><code>fract(&minus;0.25) = 0.75</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>clamp(x, low, high)</code></td><td>hold a value between two limits</td><td><code>clamp(5, &minus;1, 2) = 2</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>mix(a, b, t)</code></td><td>slide from a to b (also called lerp)</td><td><code>mix(10, 20, 0.25) = 12.5</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>fade(t, attack, release, total)</code></td><td>an envelope: fade in, hold, fade out</td><td><code>fade(0.5, 1, 1, 4) = 0.5</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>noteFrequency(m)</code></td><td>frequency of MIDI key <code>m</code> in equal temperament</td><td><code>noteFrequency(69) = 440</code></td></tr>
</table>
<p>Because the functions are ordinary kernels they also work for things that are not sound: a <code>sawtooth</code> is a repeating ramp (animation, scrolling textures), a <code>triangle</code> is a bouncing value, <code>mix</code> and <code>clamp</code> are the everyday blending tools of graphics, and <code>noise</code> gives repeatable randomness for textures. A minimal tone in a kernel is now one line: <code>return 0.5 * sine(440.0 * i.toFloat() / 44100.0)</code>. The melody in the example builds its notes from <code>noteFrequency</code> of MIDI key numbers (60, 62, 64, ...), not from typed-in frequencies.</p>
<h3>Built into the language: no import needed</h3>
<p>A program may simply call <code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code> and <code>mix</code>, with no <code>import</code> and no definitions. The oscillators are not built in: <code>ideas/audio/examples/waves.poly</code> imports them:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:ideas/audio/examples/waves.poly}}</code></pre>
<p>How it works is deliberately small. When the compiler checks a program and finds that it calls a function that is not defined, it looks the name up in a short list (the four Math names, and those of the GPU and Text libraries). If the name is on the list, the compiler adds the library's definition of that function, <em>and of whatever it calls</em> (<code>fract</code> needs the C function <code>floorf</code>; the Text library's <code>lines</code> needs <code>split</code>), in front of the program, and checks again. Nothing else is added: the Metal text of a program that uses <code>fract</code> contains the kernel <code>fract</code> and not <code>mix</code>. The checking is repeated until nothing new turns up, because the arguments of an unknown call are only examined once the call is known: in <code>clamp(fract(t), ...)</code> the first round finds <code>clamp</code> and the second finds <code>fract</code>.</p>
<p>Three rules keep it predictable. A program that <strong>imports</strong> the library, or <strong>defines</strong> a function of the same name, never reaches this machinery, because nothing is unknown. If adding what a call needs would <strong>clash</strong> with something the program defines (its own <code>split</code> while it calls the library's <code>lines</code>), nothing is added and the call is reported unknown, as it always was: the program's own names always win and are never silently replaced or hijacked. And a real mistake (a misspelt function) is reported as before, without the library's names in the message. The library's text is <code>core/stdlib/Math.poly</code> itself, copied into both compilers by <code>core/stdlib/sync-prelude.py</code> (the compiler cannot read that folder when it runs), and a test fails if the copy ever differs from the file.</p>
<p>What is still ahead (section 30): running and fixing the Linux and Windows audio back ends on real machines, and audio input.</p>

<h2>25.6b The Sound type: build, mix and save audio in a few lines</h2>
<p>The second slice of the roadmap is a <strong>buffer type</strong>, <code>Sound</code> (<code>ideas/audio/Sound.poly</code>), so that a whole piece can be written without hand-rolled sample loops. A <code>Sound</code> is four numbers (where the samples live, how many <em>frames</em> there are, how fast they play, and how many <em>channels</em>: section 25.6e) together with operations that work in place. It uses the Wave library underneath, runs on the CPU, and the program that uses it needs no GPU:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>soundNew(seconds, rate)</code></td><td>a new, silent sound of that length</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.tone(shape, hz, amplitude, start, length)</code></td><td>add one note: shape 0 sine, 1 square, 2 sawtooth, 3 triangle, with a short fade in and out. Several at once make a chord</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.add(other, start, gain)</code></td><td><strong>mix</strong> another sound in, scaled, beginning <code>start</code> seconds in</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.scale(gain)</code></td><td>multiply every sample (volume)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.noise(amplitude, seed)</code></td><td>add repeatable hiss</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.peak()</code>, <code>s.normalize(level)</code></td><td>the loudest sample; scale so the loudest is exactly <code>level</code> (prevents clipping after mixing)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.seconds()</code></td><td>the length in seconds</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.saveWav(path)</code>, <code>soundLoadWav(path)</code></td><td>write a 16-bit WAV file (any number of channels); read one back (section 25.6f)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>soundPlay(s)</code></td><td>play it through the speakers, mono or stereo (from <code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>; native builds only; section 25.6i)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A short piece, <code>ideas/audio-output/examples/compose.poly</code>, uses all of it: a scale of sine notes, a sawtooth bass, a held chord, and then an <strong>echo</strong> made by mixing a quieter copy of the song back in 0.25 seconds later:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/audio-output/examples/compose.poly:28:54}}</code></pre>
<p>That echo is worth a second look. <code>add(song, 0.25, 0.4)</code> adds a delayed, scaled copy of the sound to itself, which is exactly a <em>convolution</em> of the sound with two spikes: one at time 0 of height 1 and one at 0.25 s of height 0.4. A concert hall's reverb is the same operation with thousands of spikes (section 24.5). So even this one-line mix is a filter.</p>
<h3>What is inside a WAV file</h3>
<p>The format is deliberately simple: 44 bytes that describe the sound, then the samples. All numbers are stored low byte first.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Contents</th><th>Value written</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0&ndash;3</td><td>the letters <code>RIFF</code> (the container type)</td><td>82 73 70 70</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4&ndash;7</td><td>the size of the rest of the file</td><td>total bytes &minus; 8</td></tr>
  <tr><td>8&ndash;11</td><td>the letters <code>WAVE</code></td><td>87 65 86 69</td></tr>
  <tr><td>12&ndash;15</td><td>the letters <code>fmt </code> (the description follows)</td><td>102 109 116 32</td></tr>
  <tr><td>16&ndash;19</td><td>size of the description</td><td>16</td></tr>
  <tr><td>20&ndash;21</td><td>format: 1 means plain (PCM) samples</td><td>1</td></tr>
  <tr><td>22&ndash;23</td><td>channels</td><td>1 (mono), 2 (stereo), &hellip;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>24&ndash;27</td><td>sample rate</td><td>44100</td></tr>
  <tr><td>28&ndash;31</td><td>bytes per second</td><td>rate &times; 2 &times; channels</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32&ndash;33</td><td>bytes per sample frame</td><td>2 &times; channels</td></tr>
  <tr><td>34&ndash;35</td><td>bits per sample</td><td>16</td></tr>
  <tr><td>36&ndash;39</td><td>the letters <code>data</code></td><td>100 97 116 97</td></tr>
  <tr><td>40&ndash;43</td><td>size of the sample data</td><td>frames &times; channels &times; 2</td></tr>
  <tr><td>44 &hellip;</td><td>the samples (interleaved), each a whole number from &minus;32768 to 32767</td><td>sample &times; 32767, negatives stored as 65536 + n</td></tr>
</table>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:saveWav+soundNumber32+soundNumber16}}</code></pre>
<p>The tests build sounds with these operations and check the results: a 0.5-amplitude tone has peak 0.5, halving it gives 0.25, mixing two equal tones doubles the peak to 1.0, <code>normalize(0.9)</code> sets the peak to 0.9, a note mixed in 0.5 s late leaves the earlier samples silent. The saved piece was also checked by a separate program: its scale notes are at 261.6, 293.7 and 329.6 Hz and the chord at 261.6, 329.6 and 392.0 Hz, with nothing at 300 Hz.</p>

<h2>25.6c Filters: reshaping a sound's spectrum</h2>
<p>Once a sound is a list of numbers, a <strong>filter</strong> is any rule that makes each output sample from nearby input samples. The Sound library (<code>ideas/audio/Sound.poly</code>) has two simple ones, an echo, a reverb and a general convolution (the steeper filters and equalizers come in section 25.6h), and the program <code>ideas/gpu/examples/filters.poly</code> shows exactly what each does to each frequency, using the GPU's Fourier transform to look.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Sounds like</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.lowPass(cutoff)</code></td><td>lets slow waves through, reduces fast ones</td><td>muffled, dull, &ldquo;behind a wall&rdquo;</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.highPass(cutoff)</code></td><td>removes slow waves, keeps fast ones</td><td>thin, tinny; removes rumble and hum</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.echo(delay, gain)</code></td><td>adds the sound back, delayed and quieter, repeatedly (feedback)</td><td>a canyon or a long hall</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.reverb(wet)</code></td><td>four short echoes side by side, blended in: a dense wash</td><td>a room</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.convolve(response)</code></td><td>replaces every sample by a scaled copy of a whole sound and adds the copies</td><td>any of the above, depending on the response</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The one-pole low-pass: how a simple filter works</h3>
<p>The low-pass is only three lines. Each output sample moves a fraction <code>alpha</code> of the way from the previous output toward the new input:</p>
<pre><code>output[i]  =  output[i-1]  +  alpha * ( input[i] - output[i-1] )        alpha = dt / (RC + dt),   RC = 1 / (2&pi; &middot; cutoff),   dt = 1 / rate</code></pre>
<p>A slow wave changes little from one sample to the next, so the output keeps up with it. A fast wave flips direction before the output has moved far, so the output only wiggles a little: it has been smoothed. The cutoff sets how slow &ldquo;slow&rdquo; means. This is the same rule as an electronic resistor-capacitor (RC) circuit, and as the physical blurring of a sound by a wall. It is also a <em>moving average</em> with a long, fading memory, which is why it is the audio cousin of the blur filter of section 17.8. The high-pass is the mirror image: <code>output[i] = alpha * (output[i-1] + input[i] - input[i-1])</code> passes only the <em>changes</em> and lets a steady level decay away.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:lowPassArray+highPassArray}}</code></pre>

<h3>Seeing what a filter does: two tones through it</h3>
<p>The test sound has two tones of height 1: 200 Hz (bin 50) and 1000 Hz (bin 250). Both filters have their cutoff at 500 Hz, between them. The spectra, from the GPU:</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:8px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-1-original.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">original: two spikes of height 1</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-2-lowpass.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">low-pass 500 Hz: 200 Hz keeps 0.90, 1000 Hz drops to 0.40</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-3-highpass.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">high-pass 500 Hz: 200 Hz drops to 0.36, 1000 Hz keeps 0.78</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 55. The same two tones through each filter. The program checks all four gains against the expected values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>This is the convolution theorem of section 24.5 in action: the waves pass through the filter <em>separately</em>, each scaled by a number that depends only on its frequency. The filter does nothing else.</p>

<h3>The frequency response, measured with a click</h3>
<p>How does one find the scale factor for <em>every</em> frequency at once? Feed the filter a single <strong>click</strong> (an <em>impulse</em>: one sample of height 1, then silence). A click contains every frequency in equal measure (its spectrum is flat), so the spectrum of whatever comes out <em>is</em> the filter's response, the list of its scale factors. (Linear-algebra view: the output for an impulse is one column of the filter's matrix, and its Fourier transform lists the matrix's eigenvalues, section 24.5.)</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap:10px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-4-lowpass-response.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">low-pass response, 0 to 2000 Hz: 1 at 0 Hz, falling as frequency rises</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-5-highpass-response.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">high-pass response: 0 at 0 Hz, rising toward 1</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 56. Filter responses from the GPU's transform of each filter's answer to a click.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>An ideal analog one-pole low-pass has the response <code>1 / &radic;(1 + (f / cutoff)&sup2;)</code>: at the cutoff it is 0.71 (&minus;3 decibels, &ldquo;half the power&rdquo;), at twice the cutoff 0.45, at ten times 0.10: it falls by half for every doubling of frequency, <strong>6 decibels per octave</strong>. The digital version used here is a little different (0.65 at the cutoff), which the program's check reflects. A steeper filter, like the 24 dB-per-octave ones in synthesizers, simply uses several of these in a row.</p>

<h3>Echo and reverb</h3>
<p>An <strong>echo</strong> is the simplest filter of all: <code>output[i] = input[i] + gain &middot; output[i &minus; delay]</code>. A short beep with a 0.25-second delay and gain 0.5 repeats at half volume each time:</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:8px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-6-beep.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">one beep</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-7-echo.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">with echo(0.25, 0.5): four repeats, each half as loud</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/filters-8-reverb.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">with reverb(0.8): a dense, quickly fading tail</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 57. Echo and reverb, drawn as waveforms (one second each).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>A real room does not echo once every quarter second; sound bounces between many surfaces and arrives from all directions at all delays, merging into a smooth wash. The library's <code>reverb</code> imitates this the classic way (a <em>Schroeder reverberator</em>): four echoes with different short delays (29.7, 37.1, 41.1 and 43.7 milliseconds, chosen so their repeats rarely line up), each feeding back on itself, added together. The pieces are small:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:echo+reverbArray+convolveArrays}}</code></pre>
<p><strong><code>convolve</code> is the general tool.</strong> Convolving a sound with a short list of spikes gives an echo; with a recording of a real room's response to a click it gives <em>that room's</em> reverb, so a studio recording can be made to sound as if it were played in a cathedral. The direct form does <code>count &times; response.count</code> multiplications: a second of CD sound (44,100 samples) with a two-second room response (88,200 samples) is nearly four billion multiply-adds, which is exactly the kind of work that belongs on the GPU (or in the frequency domain, where convolution becomes plain multiplication: section 24.5).</p>
<p>All of these are covered by tests: the filters' gains match a reference run of the same recurrence, an echo of an impulse puts 0.5 and 0.25 at the right places, <code>[1, 2]</code> convolved with <code>[1, 0, 3]</code> is <code>[1, 2, 3, 6]</code>, and the reverb of an impulse has the sum of the four feedbacks at its start and the first echo at 29.7 ms.</p>

<h2>25.6d Convolution on the GPU: a real room in milliseconds</h2>
<p>Convolution deserves a closer look, because it is the most useful operation in audio and one of the best fits for a GPU. The idea in plain terms: <strong>replace every sample of a sound by a whole copy of another sound, scaled by that sample, and add up all the copies.</strong> If the second sound is a recording of a room's reaction to a single hand-clap (the room's <em>impulse response</em>), the result is the first sound as it would have been heard in that room: each moment of it is smeared into the room's whole echo pattern.</p>
<pre><code>output[i]  =  sum over j of  input[i - j] &middot; response[j]          (input has nx samples, response nh: the output has nx + nh - 1)</code></pre>
<p>The cost is <code>nx &times; nh</code> multiply-adds. A second of CD sound (44,100 samples) with a one-second room response (44,100 samples) is <strong>1.9 billion</strong> of them. But look at the formula from the output's side: <em>each output sample is a sum that depends only on the inputs, never on other outputs</em>. That is a map with a loop in it: one GPU thread per output sample, each adding up its own products. (The version in <code>Sound.convolve</code> works from the input's side, spreading each input sample over the output; the kernel version <em>gathers</em> into each output sample instead, so no thread writes where another writes and no atomics are needed.)</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/audio/Convolve.poly:1:29}}</code></pre>
<p>A helper in <code>ideas/gpu/SoundGpu.poly</code> uploads the two sounds, launches one thread per output sample and hands back the result as a <code>Sound</code>, so on the program's side a GPU reverb is one line: <code>let wet: Sound = soundConvolveGpu(device, pipeline, dry, room)</code>. The example <code>ideas/gpu/examples/convolve.poly</code> makes a made-up room (a click followed by noise that fades exponentially to a thousandth over the room's &ldquo;reverberation time&rdquo;, the standard cheap trick), convolves a short 440 Hz beep with it both on the CPU and on the GPU, and compares:</p>
<figure>
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap:8px;">
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/convolve-1-room.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">the room's response: a click, then fading noise</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/convolve-2-dry.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">the dry beep (0.1 s)</div></div>
<div><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/convolve-3-wet.pgm:1}}" style="width:100%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><div class="k">the beep in the room: smeared out and ringing</div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Figure 58. Convolution as reverb. The short beep leaves a long, fading tail.</figcaption>
</figure>
<table>
  <tr><th>Beep</th><th>Room response</th><th>Output samples</th><th>CPU, one core</th><th>GPU</th><th>GPU faster by</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0.5 s</td><td>0.1 s</td><td>26,459</td><td>about 204 ms</td><td>about 5 ms</td><td>about 40&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>0.5 s</td><td>0.3 s</td><td>35,279</td><td>about 625 ms</td><td>about 11 ms</td><td>about 57&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>0.5 s</td><td>0.5 s</td><td>44,099</td><td>about 1,019 ms</td><td>about 13 ms</td><td>about 78&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 s</td><td>1 s</td><td>88,199</td><td>about 4,094 ms</td><td>about 23 ms</td><td>about 180&times;</td></tr>
</table>
<p>(Wall-clock times on this Apple Silicon Mac, one CPU core against the GPU; yours will differ.) The CPU time grows with the product of the lengths, as the formula says; the GPU time grows far more slowly because the thousands of output samples are computed at the same moment. The GPU's samples equal the CPU's at every sample checked, to within a tiny difference (the products are added in a different order, so the last digits of a floating-point sum can differ). The saved result was also checked independently: the loudness (RMS) of the result was measured in 0.1-second windows with a separate program: it starts high, then decays smoothly, falling by a factor of roughly 2.5 to 3 every tenth of a second (measured in 0.1-second windows), as an exponentially fading room should.</p>
<h3>Through the frequency domain, now on the GPU</h3>
<p>The frequency-domain method is implemented too (<code>soundConvolveFft</code> in <code>ideas/gpu/SoundGpu.poly</code>), using the FFT of section 24.11: transform both sounds (padded with zeros to a power of two long enough to hold the result), multiply the two spectra number by number with a small kernel (<code>complexMultiply</code>), and transform back. For a one-second beep and room responses of increasing length, on the GPU:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Room response</th><th>Direct sum</th><th>Through the FFT</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0.5 s</td><td>about 9 ms</td><td>about 25 ms (includes first-use set-up)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 s</td><td>about 13 ms</td><td>about 13 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2 s</td><td>about 23 ms</td><td>about 13 ms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4 s</td><td>about 44 ms</td><td>about 14 ms</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The break-even is around a one-second response here; beyond it the FFT method pulls ahead and keeps its time almost flat while the direct sum keeps growing with the product of the lengths. The program checks that the two methods agree (to within the rounding of single-precision numbers, measured against the loudest sample).</p>
<h3>The other way: through the frequency domain</h3>
<p>Section 24.5 explained that convolution in time is <em>multiplication</em> in frequency: transform both sounds, multiply their spectra number by number, transform back. With the fast Fourier transform this costs about <code>N log N</code> instead of <code>N &times; M</code>, which is why professional reverb plug-ins convolve in the frequency domain, in blocks, rather than by the direct sum. The direct sum on a GPU remains attractive when responses are short (filters of a few hundred samples), when simplicity matters, or as the reference to test faster methods against. The Fourier transform in this guide (<code>dft</code>) is also a GPU kernel, and the fast version is in section 24.11.</p>
<p><strong>Real-time caveat.</strong> A launch costs a millisecond or so of set-up and waiting (section 5.4), which is comparable to a small live-audio buffer (section 25.8). So GPU convolution is wonderful for <em>offline</em> work (mixing, mastering, rendering a whole song through a cathedral) and for long responses processed in large blocks, but a live instrument with a 64-sample buffer still processes on the CPU.</p>

<h2>25.6e Stereo: channels, frames and panning</h2>
<p>Everything so far was <strong>mono</strong>: one list of numbers for one speaker. Most music is <strong>stereo</strong>: two lists, one for the left speaker and one for the right, so that sounds can be <em>placed</em> (the guitar on the left, the drums in the middle, the piano on the right). A sound with several <strong>channels</strong> carries one number per channel at every instant. One instant's worth of numbers is a <strong>frame</strong>: a mono frame is a single sample, a stereo frame is a left sample and a right sample, a surround-sound frame has six.</p>
<p>The samples are stored <strong>interleaved</strong>, which is the order sound hardware and WAV files expect: the left and right samples of frame 0, then those of frame 1, and so on.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>position in memory</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th><th>&hellip;</th></tr>
  <tr><td>what is stored there</td><td>left, frame 0</td><td>right, frame 0</td><td>left, frame 1</td><td>right, frame 1</td><td>left, frame 2</td><td>right, frame 2</td><td>&hellip;</td></tr>
</table>
<p>So frame <code>f</code>, channel <code>c</code> lives at <code>samples[f * channels + c]</code>. The <code>Sound</code> type of section 25.6b therefore gained a fourth number, <code>channels</code>, and its <code>count</code> now counts <em>frames</em>: <code>count / rate</code> is still the length in seconds, whether the sound is mono or stereo. Every operation that does the same thing to every sample (<code>scale</code>, <code>peak</code>, <code>normalize</code>, <code>echo</code>, <code>tone</code>) just handles all channels; the filters run on one channel at a time so that the left and right never leak into each other.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>soundNewChannels(seconds, rate, 2)</code></td><td>a new, silent stereo sound (<code>soundNew</code> is the mono shorthand)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.channel(c)</code></td><td>channel <code>c</code> on its own, as a mono sound</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>soundStereo(left, right)</code></td><td>two mono sounds interleaved into one stereo sound</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.toMono()</code>, <code>s.toStereo()</code></td><td>average all channels into one; copy a mono sound into both channels</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>s.pan(position)</code></td><td>place a mono sound in a stereo picture, from &minus;1 (all left) to +1 (all right)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>a.add(b, start, gain)</code></td><td>mixing: channel <code>c</code> of <code>b</code> goes into channel <code>c</code> of <code>a</code>; a mono <code>b</code> goes into every channel</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Panning: why the middle is 0.707, not 0.5</h3>
<p>To put a mono sound at position <code>p</code> you need a volume for each speaker. The obvious rule is a straight line: right = (1 + p)/2 and left = (1 &minus; p)/2, so the middle is 0.5 and 0.5. But the <em>loudness</em> your ear perceives follows the sound's <em>power</em>, which is the square of the amplitude (loudness, section 25.2), and the two speakers' powers add. In the middle that rule gives 0.5&sup2; + 0.5&sup2; = 0.5: only <em>half</em> the power it has at the edges, so the sound seems to sag by 3 decibels as it passes through the centre.</p>
<p>The cure is to choose the two volumes so that <strong>left&sup2; + right&sup2; = 1</strong> everywhere. That equation is the circle, and the identity sin&sup2;&theta; + cos&sup2;&theta; = 1 solves it: <code>left = cos &theta;</code>, <code>right = sin &theta;</code>, with the angle &theta; = (p + 1)&pi;/4 sweeping a quarter turn from hard left (&theta; = 0: left 1, right 0) to hard right (&theta; = &pi;/2: left 0, right 1). In the middle each speaker gets cos 45&deg; = sin 45&deg; = 0.7071 (&minus;3 decibels, not &minus;6). This is the <em>constant-power pan law</em>, and it is the same unit-circle picture as the rotation matrices of section 12.</p>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/pan.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @pan. The panning law. Left: the volume sent to each speaker as a sound moves from hard left to hard right. Right: the total loudness (left&sup2; + right&sup2;). With cos and sin it is exactly 1 everywhere; with a straight line it sags to 0.5 in the middle.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:pan}}</code></pre>
<p>The program <code>ideas/audio-output/examples/studio.poly</code> builds a four-second stereo piece (a 440 Hz melody panned to &minus;0.6, a 100 Hz sawtooth bass in the middle, a 660 Hz chord note panned to +0.6), saves it as a stereo WAV file, and then <em>measures</em> where each tone ended up with <code>strength</code>, the one-frequency Fourier transform of section 24.4 (<code>s.strength(hz, start, length)</code> is the height of the sine wave of that frequency hiding in the sound):</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:snippets/studio.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>The first line reads: the stereo file was written, read back, and no sample differs by more than 0.046 thousandths (a 16-bit file keeps about four decimal places). The second line is the pan law at work: a 0.4-high tone panned to &minus;0.6 has &theta; = 0.1&pi;, so its left volume is 0.4 cos(0.1&pi;) = 0.380 and its right volume 0.4 sin(0.1&pi;) = 0.124, which is exactly what was measured. (The bass is at 100 Hz so that none of its harmonics land on 440 or 660 Hz and disturb the measurement; an earlier choice of 110 Hz put the bass's fourth harmonic <em>exactly</em> on the melody and visibly changed the measured numbers.)</p>

<h2>25.6f Reading WAV files</h2>
<p>Section 25.6b wrote a WAV file; <code>soundLoadWav(path)</code> reads one, so a recording, a sound effect or the output of another program can become a <code>Sound</code>. A WAV file is a <strong>RIFF container</strong>: the letters <code>RIFF</code>, a size, the letters <code>WAVE</code>, and then a sequence of <strong>chunks</strong>, each a four-letter name, a 32-bit size, and that many bytes (padded with one extra byte if the size is odd). Two chunks matter: <code>fmt </code> says how the samples are stored and <code>data</code> holds them. Every other chunk (<code>LIST</code> holds the title and artist, for example) is skipped. The reader walks the chunks:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:wavParse}}</code></pre>
<p>What the <code>fmt </code> chunk can say, and how each kind of sample becomes a float from &minus;1 to +1:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Stored as</th><th>Range of the stored number</th><th>Becomes</th></tr>
  <tr><td>8-bit whole numbers (<em>unsigned</em>: 128 is silence)</td><td>0 to 255</td><td>(n &minus; 128) / 128</td></tr>
  <tr><td>16-bit whole numbers</td><td>&minus;32768 to 32767</td><td>n / 32768</td></tr>
  <tr><td>24-bit whole numbers</td><td>&minus;8,388,608 to 8,388,607</td><td>n / 8,388,608</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32-bit whole numbers</td><td>&minus;2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647</td><td>n / 2,147,483,648</td></tr>
  <tr><td>32-bit or 64-bit floating point</td><td>already &minus;1 to +1</td><td>the same number</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Newer programs write an <em>extensible</em> header (format code 65534) whose real format code is hidden inside an extension; the reader looks inside it. A file whose <code>data</code> chunk claims more bytes than the file really has (a recording cut short) loads what is there. A file that is missing, or is not a WAV file the reader supports, gives a sound with <code>count</code> 0.</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Sound.poly:wavDecode+wavSample}}</code></pre>
<p>A reader is only trustworthy if it is tested on files it did not write itself. <code>core/tests/programs/fixtures/make-fixtures.py</code> builds nine tiny WAV files byte by byte with Python's <code>struct</code> module, with sample values chosen to expose each edge, and the tests check every decoded number:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Fixture</th><th>What it exercises</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>pcm8-mono.wav</code></td><td>unsigned 8-bit: bytes 0, 64, 128, 192, 255 must decode to &minus;1, &minus;0.5, 0, 0.5, 0.992</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>pcm16-stereo.wav</code></td><td>16-bit stereo, with an odd-sized <code>LIST</code> chunk (and its padding byte) before the data</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>pcm24-mono.wav</code>, <code>pcm32-mono.wav</code></td><td>three-byte and four-byte whole numbers, including the extremes</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>float32-stereo.wav</code>, <code>float64-mono.wav</code></td><td>floating point samples (copied to an aligned spot before being read as numbers)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>extensible16.wav</code></td><td>the extensible header with PCM hidden inside</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>truncated.wav</code></td><td>a data chunk that claims 1000 bytes but holds 8</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>not-audio.wav</code>, and a missing file</td><td>both must give an empty sound, not a crash</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>25.6g Changing the sample rate: resampling</h2>
<p>A recording made at 44,100 samples a second, played on a device that wants 48,000, must be re-measured at the new rate. Doing it badly is easy and audible. Repeating each sample, or drawing a straight line between neighbours (linear interpolation), changes the sound: the first adds a staircase of false high frequencies, the second dulls the treble. Doing it <em>right</em> uses the sampling theorem of section 24.6: a wave that contains no frequency above half the sample rate is <strong>completely</strong> described by its samples, so the wave between the samples can be rebuilt exactly, and then measured wherever we like.</p>
<p>The rebuilding is a sum. Each sample contributes one copy of the curve <code>sinc(x) = sin(&pi;x) / (&pi;x)</code>, scaled by the sample's value and centred on the sample. That curve is 1 at its centre and <em>exactly 0 at every other sample position</em>, so at each original sample the sum returns the original value, and between samples it is the smooth wave that passes through them all. To get the height at a new instant, add up the curves of the nearest samples:</p>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/sinc.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @sinc. The weights for one output sample. Each dot is an input sample, 0 being the one nearest the output instant. The dashed blue curve is the ideal sinc; it never ends, so it is multiplied by a smooth window (dotted gold) that fades it to zero. The result (green) says how much of each neighbouring sample goes into the new sample. (The program uses 16 zero crossings on each side; the figure shows 6 so that the shapes are visible.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Resample.poly:resampleWindow+resampleSinc+resampleAt}}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Going down needs one more step.</strong> A new rate of 4,000 samples a second can only hold frequencies below 2,000 Hz. A 3,000 Hz tone in the original does not simply vanish if you keep every other sample: it <em>folds</em> back to 4,000 &minus; 3,000 = 1,000 Hz and plays as a false tone at full strength (<em>aliasing</em>, section 24.5). So when the new rate is lower the sinc curves are stretched by the ratio of the rates, which makes the sum a low-pass filter at the same time. The studio program shows the difference with real numbers:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>3,000 Hz tone at 8,000 Hz down to 4,000 Hz:
  dropping every second sample gives a false 1,000 Hz tone of 1.000
  resample gives 0.000</code></pre>
<p>The same program resamples its stereo mix to 48,000 and to 22,050 Hz: the frame counts become exactly 192,000 and 88,200 (four seconds), and the 440 Hz melody measures 0.380 before and after (see the output in section 25.6e). The tests also check a 440 Hz sine rebuilt at 12,000 Hz against the exact sine at every new instant (largest error under 0.001), a round trip 8,000 &rarr; 12,000 &rarr; 8,000 Hz, and that a constant (a &ldquo;DC&rdquo; level) stays constant. The cost is about 2 &times; 16 / cutoff multiplications per output sample, so a few seconds of stereo take a fraction of a second.</p>

<h2>25.6h Equalizers: biquad filters</h2>
<p>The one-pole filters of section 25.6c remember one past <em>output</em>. A <strong>biquad</strong> remembers <em>two</em> past outputs and two past inputs, and that small extra memory is enough to build every filter on a mixing desk: steeper low-pass and high-pass filters, band-pass and notch filters, the bass and treble knobs, and a &ldquo;bell&rdquo; that lifts or cuts one region. Each output sample is a weighted sum of five numbers:</p>
<pre><code>y[n] = b0&middot;x[n] + b1&middot;x[n&minus;1] + b2&middot;x[n&minus;2] &minus; a1&middot;y[n&minus;1] &minus; a2&middot;y[n&minus;2]</code></pre>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/biquad.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @biquad. A biquad. The boxes marked z<sup>&minus;1</sup> are one-sample delays: the left column delays the input, the right column delays the output, which is fed back. The feedback is what lets a biquad <em>resonate</em> at a chosen frequency, something no filter without it can do.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Biquad.poly:run}}</code></pre>
<p>Only <code>b0, b1, b2, a1, a2</code> decide what the filter does. Nobody chooses them by hand: Robert Bristow-Johnson's <em>Audio EQ Cookbook</em> turns a musician's description into those five numbers, and <code>ideas/audio/Biquad.poly</code> implements it. The description needs a centre or corner frequency in hertz, a gain in decibels where there is a boost or cut, and a <strong>Q</strong> that says how sharp the filter is (a high Q is narrow; 0.707 is the gentlest setting with no bump):</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Filter (<code>Sound</code> method)</th><th>What it does</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>lowPassSteep(cutoff, q)</code>, <code>highPassSteep(cutoff, q)</code></td><td>12 decibels per octave (the volume drops to a quarter for every doubling of frequency), twice as steep as the one-pole filters; a large <code>q</code> adds a resonant bump at the cutoff</td><td>synthesizer filters, rumble and hiss removal</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bandPass(center, q)</code></td><td>keeps a band about <code>center / q</code> hertz wide, gain exactly 1 at the centre</td><td>telephone and &ldquo;wah&rdquo; sounds, isolating an instrument</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>notch(center, q)</code></td><td>removes a narrow band, gain exactly 0 at the centre</td><td>killing a 50 or 60 Hz mains hum, a feedback whistle</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>bell(center, q, gainDb)</code></td><td>boosts or cuts a band by <code>gainDb</code> decibels and leaves the rest alone</td><td>parametric equalizer: lift the vocals, tame a boomy room</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>lowShelf(corner, gainDb)</code>, <code>highShelf(corner, gainDb)</code></td><td>changes everything below (or above) the corner by the same amount, with a smooth step</td><td>the bass and treble knobs</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>apply(filter)</code></td><td>runs any <code>Biquad</code> over every channel; chain several to build an equalizer</td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>A filter's <strong>response curve</strong> says how much of each frequency it lets through, and for a biquad it is exact, not measured. Take the Fourier transform of the five numbers: with &omega; = 2&pi; &middot; hz / rate, the gain is the size of (b0 + b1e<sup>&minus;j&omega;</sup> + b2e<sup>&minus;2j&omega;</sup>) divided by (1 + a1e<sup>&minus;j&omega;</sup> + a2e<sup>&minus;2j&omega;</sup>). <code>Biquad.gain(hz, rate)</code> evaluates that, which is how the response curves in this section are drawn:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio/Biquad.poly:gain}}</code></pre>
<p>Three filters in a row make an equalizer. The program applies +6 dB of bass below 200 Hz, &minus;6 dB around 1,000 Hz and +3 dB of treble above 6,000 Hz, predicts the gain at eight test frequencies by multiplying the three response curves, then actually runs a tone of each frequency through the three filters and measures what comes out (the <em>strength</em> of the tone, section 25.6e). The prediction and the measurement agree to three decimals:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>tone</th><th>50 Hz</th><th>100 Hz</th><th>200 Hz</th><th>500 Hz</th><th>1,000 Hz</th><th>2,000 Hz</th><th>6,000 Hz</th><th>12,000 Hz</th></tr>
  <tr><td>predicted gain</td><td>1.986</td><td>1.896</td><td>1.370</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.502</td><td>0.810</td><td>1.167</td><td>1.397</td></tr>
  <tr><td>measured gain</td><td>1.986</td><td>1.896</td><td>1.370</td><td>0.821</td><td>0.502</td><td>0.810</td><td>1.167</td><td>1.397</td></tr>
</table>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/studio-eq.pgm:3}}" style="width:92%;border:1px solid var(--rule); display:block; margin:auto;">
<figcaption>Figure @eq. The equalizer's response curve, drawn from <code>Biquad.gain</code>: frequency from 20 Hz to 20 kHz left to right on a logarithmic scale (the faint vertical lines are 100 Hz, 1 kHz and 10 kHz), gain in decibels up and down (the grey line is 0 dB, no change; the picture spans &minus;12 to +12). The bass shelf lifts the left, the bell digs the dip at 1 kHz, the treble shelf lifts the right.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Two more everyday jobs, with the same program. A 440 Hz tone with a 0.3-high 50 Hz mains hum added: a <code>notch(50, 10)</code> takes the hum from 0.300 to 0.000 and leaves the tone at 0.487 (the notch's skirt trims the tone by under 3%). And a <strong>telephone</strong>, which passes only about 300 to 3,400 Hz: a steep high-pass at 300 Hz in series with a steep low-pass at 3,400 Hz has a gain of 0.110 at 100 Hz, 0.993 at 1,000 Hz and 0.147 at 8,000 Hz.</p>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/studio-hum.pgm:3}}" style="width:49%;border:1px solid var(--rule);"><img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/studio-phone.pgm:3}}" style="width:49%;border:1px solid var(--rule);margin-left:1%;">
<figcaption>Figure @filters2. Left: the notch at 50 Hz, a very narrow, very deep slot (the picture spans +3 to &minus;40 dB). Right: the telephone, high-pass and low-pass in series, a plateau from about 300 Hz to 3,400 Hz.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="note"><strong>Why <code>double</code> inside a filter.</strong> A filter with a low centre frequency compared with the sample rate (40 Hz at 48,000 Hz, say) has coefficients that differ from each other by tiny amounts, and 32-bit <code>float</code>s lose exactly those amounts, which makes the filter inaccurate or even unstable. So <code>Biquad</code> keeps its coefficients and its memory as 64-bit <code>double</code>s; the samples themselves stay <code>float</code>. A test runs a 40 Hz low-pass at 48,000 Hz and checks both its gain at the cutoff and a measured tone.</div>
<p><strong>How it is tested.</strong> The cookbook formulas are checked in three independent ways (<code>ideas/audio/tests/BiquadTests.poly</code>, 28 checks): (1) <em>exact properties</em> of the designs, which a typo in a formula would break: a low-pass or high-pass has gain exactly <code>q</code> at its cutoff, a band-pass exactly 1 and a notch exactly 0 at the centre, a +6 dB bell exactly 10<sup>6/20</sup> = 1.99526 at the centre, a +6 dB low shelf exactly 1.99526 at 0 Hz, exactly 1 far above the corner and half the decibels (1.41254) at the corner; (2) numbers from a separate Python implementation of the same formulas; (3) <em>run versus gain</em>: tones put through <code>run</code> and measured agree with <code>gain</code>.</p>

<h2>25.6i Live sound: callbacks, and one source for three operating systems</h2>
<p>Everything above builds a whole sound first and plays it afterwards. A game, a synthesizer or an instrument cannot: the sound depends on what you do <em>now</em>. For that, the program does not hand the speakers a finished sound but a <strong>fill function</strong>, and the system calls it whenever the hardware is about to run out of samples:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>func fill(state: i64, buffer: ptr&lt;float&gt;, frames: i64, first: i64) -&gt; i64</code></pre>
<p>It must write <code>frames</code> frames into <code>buffer</code> (interleaved, one float per channel) and return how many it really wrote; returning 0 means &ldquo;the sound is over&rdquo;. <code>first</code> is the number of the first frame asked for, and <code>state</code> is any address the program gave when it started the stream: a place to keep a note to itself, such as where it is in each wave. Because the system calls it from its own thread, a few dozen times a second, it must be quick and must never wait: a late buffer is a click.</p>
<figure>
{{raw:snippets/figs/callback.svg}}
<figcaption>Figure @callback. Three buffers in flight. While the speakers play one, the other two are already queued. When a buffer finishes, the system calls the program, which refills <em>that</em> buffer (with the sound that will play three buffers from now) and queues it again. Each buffer is 2,048 frames, 46 ms at 44,100 Hz, so the program always has about 90 ms to answer, but the sound it makes is heard about 139 ms after it was made (the <em>latency</em>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>On macOS the buffers belong to the system's AudioQueue, and the function the system calls when a buffer has been played is itself written in polyir:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio-output/macos.poly:audioRefill+audioFillAndQueue}}</code></pre>
<p>The program <code>ideas/audio-output/examples/live.poly</code> uses it to make a stereo sound while it plays: the left channel glides from 220 Hz to 440 Hz in a second (an octave), the right channel is a perfect fifth above it (1.5 times the frequency). The fill function keeps the position within each wave in the <code>state</code> block, so the waves continue smoothly from one buffer to the next:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio-output/examples/live.poly:glide}}</code></pre>
<p>The program checks that the machinery really works, on a real audio device, <em>without making any noise</em>: the stream is started with the volume at 0 (unless you ask to listen with <code>POLYIR_PLAY=1</code>), but the hardware still runs and still calls back in real time. It records every sample the fill function produced, and checks that the number of frames is right, that no buffer boundary breaks the wave (the biggest step from one sample to the next stays small), that the pitch is right (counting upward zero crossings gives one per cycle), and that the stream took about as long as the sound lasts:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:snippets/live.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>That is 22 calls for 44,100 frames (21 full buffers of 2,048 and a last one of 1,092), 329 cycles on the left (the average of 220 and 440 is 330) and 494 on the right (1.5 times that is 495), in 1.1 seconds for a 1-second sound: the extra tenth of a second is the three buffers in flight at the start.</p>
<h3>One import line, three operating systems</h3>
<p>Opening the speakers is different on every system, but the program above is not. <code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code> contains the platform-independent half (<code>ideas/audio-output/Stream.poly</code>: the stream, the time limit, the volume, playing a <code>Sound</code>) and imports a second file chosen by the operating system the program is built on. The <code>{os}</code> in the import line becomes <code>macos</code>, <code>linux</code> or <code>windows</code> (the names of std <code>Platform.os()</code>, expanded by the compiler's import phase):</p>
<pre class="long"><code>import "Stream.poly"
import "{os}.poly"</code></pre>
<table>
  <tr><th>System</th><th>File</th><th>How it makes sound</th><th>Status</th></tr>
  <tr><td>macOS</td><td><code>ideas/audio-output/macos.poly</code></td><td>AudioQueue with a callback (above)</td><td><strong>run and tested</strong>: <code>tests/gpu.sh</code> runs <code>live.poly</code> on a real device, muted</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Linux</td><td><code>ideas/audio-output/linux.poly</code></td><td>ALSA: a loop that asks for a buffer and writes it with <code>snd_pcm_writei</code>, which blocks until the card has room (underruns are recovered with <code>snd_pcm_recover</code>); links <code>-lasound</code></td><td><strong>untested</strong>: written from ALSA's documentation, type-checked by the tests and its LLVM IR validated by clang, but never run on a Linux machine</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Windows</td><td><code>ideas/audio-output/windows.poly</code></td><td>waveOut: three prepared headers (WAVEHDR) taken in turn; wait for a header's &ldquo;done&rdquo; flag, refill it, write it again; links <code>winmm</code></td><td><strong>untested</strong>: written from Microsoft's documentation, type-checked and IR-validated, never run on Windows</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Treat the two untested files as a careful starting point, not as a promise. They share everything that is not system-specific with the macOS one (the fill function, the time limit, the volume, the buffer sizes), and that shared half is tested without any sound hardware at all (<code>core/tests/programs/StreamTests.poly</code>: the fill function is asked for consecutive frames, the time limit cuts the last buffer, the volume scales, a finished stream stays finished, and a <code>Sound</code> plays channel by channel). The compiler's <code>--jit</code> still refuses any program that imports the audio runtime, as it does for Metal and Vulkan, because the JIT runs inside the tool, which does not link those libraries.</p>

<h2>25.7 The mathematics, summarized</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Sound idea</th><th>Mathematics</th><th>Linear algebra view</th></tr>
  <tr><td>a recording of <em>N</em> samples</td><td>a list of <em>N</em> numbers <code>x[i]</code>, <code>x[i] = p(i / rate)</code></td><td>a vector in <em>N</em>-dimensional space</td></tr>
  <tr><td>mixing two sounds</td><td><code>x[i] + y[i]</code></td><td>vector addition</td></tr>
  <tr><td>volume</td><td><code>g &middot; x[i]</code></td><td>scaling a vector</td></tr>
  <tr><td>a pure tone</td><td><code>A sin(2&pi; f i / rate + &phi;)</code></td><td>one basis vector (one axis) of the Fourier basis</td></tr>
  <tr><td>finding which tones are present</td><td>the DFT: dot products with sines and cosines (section 24.4)</td><td>the coordinates of the sound in the Fourier basis</td></tr>
  <tr><td>additive synthesis</td><td>a sum of sines with chosen heights</td><td>the inverse transform: a linear combination of basis vectors</td></tr>
  <tr><td>two different tones do not interfere in analysis</td><td>their dot product over a whole number of cycles is 0</td><td>orthogonality</td></tr>
  <tr><td>an equalizer or reverb</td><td>convolution, or multiplying each frequency by a number</td><td>a (circulant) matrix, diagonal in the Fourier basis</td></tr>
  <tr><td>16-bit storage</td><td>rounding each sample to a multiple of 1/32768</td><td>adding small rounding noise: each extra bit buys about 6 dB</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>25.8 Getting sound out of the computer: buffers</h2>
<p>The sound hardware consumes samples at a fixed speed and <em>never waits</em> for the program. If it runs out of numbers you hear a click or a gap (an <em>underrun</em>). The standard remedy is to hand the hardware <strong>buffers</strong>: blocks of samples, queued in advance. The hardware plays one buffer while the program fills the next. A buffer's size trades <strong>latency</strong> against safety:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Buffer size</th><th>Time it holds at 44,100 Hz</th><th>Use</th></tr>
  <tr><td>64 samples</td><td>1.5 ms</td><td>live instruments (very little delay, but little time to compute)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>512 samples</td><td>11.6 ms</td><td>typical interactive audio</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2048 samples</td><td>46 ms</td><td>what the audio runtime uses (three of them in flight)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4096 samples</td><td>93 ms</td><td>playing a prepared recording, when delay does not matter</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A program that makes sound as it goes (a game, a synthesizer) does not queue everything at once: it gives the system a <em>callback</em>, a function the system calls each time a buffer has finished, and the function must refill that buffer quickly, every time, or the sound breaks. That is a real-time deadline. The audio runtime (<code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>) does exactly that; section 25.6i explains the mechanism with a figure and a live example. Playing a finished <code>Sound</code> is the same machinery with a fill function that simply copies the next samples out of the sound:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/audio-output/Stream.poly:soundFill}}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Where does a GPU fit?</strong> Every output sample of a synthesizer is independent of the others, so one launch can compute a second of sound in a moment, and heavy jobs (huge reverbs, long Fourier transforms, many voices) suit the GPU well. But a GPU launch costs a millisecond or more of set-up and waiting, comparable to a small live-audio buffer, so the lowest-latency live work stays on the CPU, while the GPU shines for large, prepared or offline audio. The example uses it exactly that way: the GPU computes the buffers, then the system plays them.</p>

<h2>25.9 The example, and how to listen</h2>
<p>Build and run it from <code>ideas/gpu/examples</code>:</p>
<pre><code>polyir --emit-msl audio.poly &gt; audio.metal         # the GPU kernels as Metal source
polyir audio.poly --out audio                         # build natively (the audio runtime needs it)
./audio                                               # analysis checks, writes audio-chord.wav and audio-melody.wav
POLYIR_PLAY=1 ./audio                                  # the same, and also plays the chord, then the melody</code></pre>
<p>It also works with any player: <code>afplay audio-melody.wav</code> on a Mac. Playback is off by default so that the test suite stays silent. What the program checks, all on real data: the pure tone gives one spike of height 1 at exactly 400 Hz; the chord gives three spikes of the heights put in; the beating pair gives two neighbouring spikes; the square wave's first two harmonics match 4/&pi; and 4/(3&pi;); nine harmonics overshoot a little above 1; and the GPU's melody matches the CPU's sample by sample. (The saved melody file was also checked with a separate program outside polyir: the first note's strongest frequency is 261.63 Hz and the second's is 293.66 Hz, as the scale requires.)</p>
<figure>
<img src="{{pgm:snippets/pics/audio-7-melody.pgm:2}}" style="width:70%;border:1px solid var(--rule); display:block; margin:auto;">
<figcaption>Figure 54. The melody's waveform: eight separate notes of the C major scale, then a held chord at the end, which is visibly bigger and busier because four waves are added.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/audio.poly:71:89}}</code></pre>

<h3>More sound programs</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Program (in <code>ideas/audio-output/examples</code> unless noted)</th><th>What it shows</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>compose.poly</code></td><td>a short piece built from the <code>Sound</code> operations and written as a WAV file</td><td>nothing but the CPU</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>studio.poly</code></td><td>stereo panning, WAV writing and reading, resampling, an equalizer, hum removal and a telephone filter, each checked by measuring (section 25.6e to 23.6h); draws three response curves</td><td>nothing but the CPU</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>live.poly</code></td><td>sound made while it plays: a stereo glide from a fill function, checked on a real audio device at volume 0 (section 25.6i)</td><td>an audio device (exit 10 without)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/filters.poly</code>, <code>convolve.poly</code>, <code>fft.poly</code></td><td>filters, room reverb and the spectrogram, seen or computed on the GPU</td><td>a GPU</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>25.10 Try it yourself</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Experiment</th><th>What to change in <code>audio.poly</code></th></tr>
  <tr><td>Hear a pure tone</td><td>play only <code>chord</code> after setting a single partial of 440 Hz: a plain, flute-like sine</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hear beats</td><td>write a 3-second synth with partials 440 and 443: three throbs a second</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Change the timbre</td><td>give the chord partials at 2&times; and 3&times; each frequency with heights 0.5 and 0.25 (the melody already does this)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Hear clipping</td><td>raise the melody volume (<code>melodySettings[2]</code>) from 0.12 to 0.4: the final chord distorts</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vibrato</td><td>set <code>p[5] = 5</code> and <code>p[6] = 0.5</code> in <code>setSynth</code>: a gentle wobble</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A different scale</td><td>replace the note list with frequencies <code>440 &middot; 2<sup>(m &minus; 69)/12</sup></code> for your own MIDI numbers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Square-wave music</td><td>make the melody with <code>squareWave</code> instead of sines: the classic chiptune sound</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ 23 -->
<h1 id="queens">26. Searching on a GPU: the N-queens puzzle</h1>

<p>A classroom remark worth testing: the <strong>N-queens problem</strong> can be solved on a GPU. It is true, and it makes a wonderful example because it is <em>not</em> a picture problem at all. It shows how an ordinary computer-science search, backtracking, can be cut into thousands of independent pieces and handed to a GPU. The program here is <code>ideas/gpu/examples/queens.poly</code>; it counts every solution for boards up to 15 &times; 15 (and 16 &times; 16 when asked) on the GPU, checks the counts against the well-known answers, and times the GPU against one CPU core.</p>

<h2>26.1 The puzzle</h2>
<p>A chess queen attacks any piece in her row, her column, or along either diagonal. <strong>Place N queens on an N &times; N board so that no two attack each other.</strong> There is exactly one queen in every row and every column, and no two share a diagonal. For a 4 &times; 4 board there are just two ways (mirror images of each other):</p>
<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 175" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="4 queens solutions">
  <g transform="translate(40,15)">
    <g stroke="#586069" fill="#fff"><rect x="0" y="0" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="35" y="0" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="70" y="0" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="105" y="0" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/>
    <rect x="0" y="35" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="35" y="35" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="70" y="35" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="105" y="35" width="35" height="35"/>
    <rect x="0" y="70" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="35" y="70" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="70" y="70" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="105" y="70" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/>
    <rect x="0" y="105" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="35" y="105" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="70" y="105" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="105" y="105" width="35" height="35"/></g>
    <g font-size="26" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0b5cad"><text x="52" y="28">&#9819;</text><text x="122" y="63">&#9819;</text><text x="17" y="98">&#9819;</text><text x="87" y="133">&#9819;</text></g>
    <text x="70" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">solution 1: columns 1, 3, 0, 2</text>
  </g>
  <g transform="translate(240,15)">
    <g stroke="#586069" fill="#fff"><rect x="0" y="0" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="35" y="0" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="70" y="0" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="105" y="0" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/>
    <rect x="0" y="35" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="35" y="35" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="70" y="35" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="105" y="35" width="35" height="35"/>
    <rect x="0" y="70" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="35" y="70" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="70" y="70" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="105" y="70" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/>
    <rect x="0" y="105" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="35" y="105" width="35" height="35"/><rect x="70" y="105" width="35" height="35" fill="#e6ebf1"/><rect x="105" y="105" width="35" height="35"/></g>
    <g font-size="26" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0b5cad"><text x="87" y="28">&#9819;</text><text x="17" y="63">&#9819;</text><text x="122" y="98">&#9819;</text><text x="52" y="133">&#9819;</text></g>
    <text x="70" y="158" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">solution 2: columns 2, 0, 3, 1</text>
  </g>
  <g font-size="11"><text x="430" y="40" font-weight="700">How many solutions?</text>
    <text x="430" y="62" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N =  4        2</text><text x="430" y="78" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N =  8       92</text><text x="430" y="94" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N = 10      724</text><text x="430" y="110" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N = 12   14,200</text><text x="430" y="126" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N = 14  365,596</text><text x="430" y="142" font-family="Menlo, monospace">N = 16  14,772,512</text></g>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 46. The two 4-queens solutions, and how fast the count grows. Each solution is written as the column of the queen in each row.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The numbers explode: from 92 solutions at 8 &times; 8 to over fourteen million at 16 &times; 16, and the number of <em>boards to examine</em> grows even faster, so counting is a serious amount of work. This is the kind of problem a GPU can help with, but only if we can find independent pieces.</p>

<h2>26.2 The search: backtracking</h2>
<p>The natural approach is <strong>backtracking</strong>, a depth-first search. Fill the board one row at a time. In the current row, try each column that is not attacked by the queens already placed; put a queen there; move on to the next row. If a row has no free column, you are stuck: <em>back up</em> to the previous row and try its next column. If you place a queen in the last row, you have found a solution; count it, then back up and keep going until the first row has run out of columns.</p>
<pre><code>search(row):
    if row == N:  count it and return
    for each column c not attacked by the queens in rows 0..row-1:
        place a queen at (row, c)
        search(row + 1)
        remove it                      (this "undo" is the back-tracking)</code></pre>
<p>All the possible partial boards form a <strong>tree</strong>: the root is the empty board, each branch places the next queen, and the leaves at depth N are the solutions (the dead ends are branches that stopped early).</p>

<h2>26.3 A fast "is this square attacked?": three numbers of bits</h2>
<p>To search quickly we need to know, for the next row, which columns are forbidden. A column is forbidden if a queen above uses that column, or lies on a diagonal that reaches it. Keep three <strong>bit lists</strong> (whole numbers whose binary digits mark squares, one bit per column):</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>cols</code>: the columns that already hold a queen;</li>
  <li><code>left</code>: the columns in the next row attacked along a diagonal that runs down-and-left;</li>
  <li><code>right</code>: the columns in the next row attacked along a diagonal that runs down-and-right.</li>
</ul>
<p>Moving down one row slides each diagonal sideways by one square, which in bits is a <strong>shift</strong>: <code>left</code> shifts one way, <code>right</code> the other. The squares still free in the next row are those where all three lists have a 0: <code>free = ~(cols | left | right)</code> (within the N bits). The lowest free square is <code>free &amp; -free</code> (a classic bit trick: it keeps only the lowest 1 bit). Here is the search for the first 4-queens solution, with bit 0 as column 0, written as four binary digits (column 3 on the left):</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Row</th><th>Queen placed at</th><th><code>cols</code></th><th><code>left</code> (for next row)</th><th><code>right</code> (for next row)</th><th>Free squares in next row</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0</td><td>column 1 (<code>0010</code>)</td><td><code>0010</code></td><td><code>(0010 &lt;&lt; 1) = 0100</code></td><td><code>(0010 &gt;&gt; 1) = 0001</code></td><td><code>~(0010|0100|0001) = 1000</code>: column 3 only</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>column 3 (<code>1000</code>)</td><td><code>1010</code></td><td><code>((0100|1000) &lt;&lt; 1) &amp; 1111 = 1000</code></td><td><code>(0001|1000) &gt;&gt; 1 = 0100</code></td><td><code>~(1010|1000|0100) = 0001</code>: column 0 only</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>column 0 (<code>0001</code>)</td><td><code>1011</code></td><td><code>((1000|0001) &lt;&lt; 1) &amp; 1111 = 0010</code></td><td><code>(0100|0001) &gt;&gt; 1 = 0010</code></td><td><code>~(1011|0010|0010) = 0100</code>: column 2 only</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>column 2 (<code>0100</code>)</td><td>the last row is filled: <strong>solution found</strong>: columns 1, 3, 0, 2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
</table>
<p>Three integer operations per row replace a loop that checked every earlier queen. This is the standard fast way to count solutions, and it uses only the operations a GPU kernel has (<code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>~</code>, shifts and integer arithmetic).</p>

<h2>26.4 Where is the parallelism? Independent subtrees</h2>
<p>Look at the search tree: once you have placed queens in the first few rows, <strong>everything below that partial board is a separate problem</strong> that never looks at any other branch. The search for solutions starting with &ldquo;queen at column 3 in row 0, column 1 in row 1&rdquo; can run at the same time as the search below &ldquo;column 0, column 2&rdquo;, with no communication. That is the recipe:</p>
<figure>
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 215" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="search tree split among threads">
  <g stroke="#586069" fill="none"><line x1="350" y1="25" x2="150" y2="75"/><line x1="350" y1="25" x2="250" y2="75"/><line x1="350" y1="25" x2="350" y2="75"/><line x1="350" y1="25" x2="450" y2="75"/><line x1="350" y1="25" x2="550" y2="75"/>
  <line x1="150" y1="75" x2="110" y2="125"/><line x1="150" y1="75" x2="150" y2="125"/><line x1="150" y1="75" x2="190" y2="125"/><line x1="250" y1="75" x2="215" y2="125"/><line x1="250" y1="75" x2="285" y2="125"/><line x1="350" y1="75" x2="320" y2="125"/><line x1="350" y1="75" x2="380" y2="125"/><line x1="450" y1="75" x2="420" y2="125"/><line x1="450" y1="75" x2="480" y2="125"/><line x1="550" y1="75" x2="520" y2="125"/><line x1="550" y1="75" x2="580" y2="125"/></g>
  <circle cx="350" cy="25" r="9" fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"/><g fill="#eef5fc" stroke="#0b5cad"><circle cx="150" cy="75" r="8"/><circle cx="250" cy="75" r="8"/><circle cx="350" cy="75" r="8"/><circle cx="450" cy="75" r="8"/><circle cx="550" cy="75" r="8"/></g>
  <g fill="#9a6700"><circle cx="110" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="150" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="190" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="215" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="285" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="320" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="380" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="420" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="480" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="520" cy="125" r="8"/><circle cx="580" cy="125" r="8"/></g>
  <g stroke="#9a6700" stroke-dasharray="3 3" fill="#fff8e6" opacity=".9"><rect x="92" y="138" width="116" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="200" y="138" width="100" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="305" y="138" width="90" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="405" y="138" width="90" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="505" y="138" width="90" height="50" rx="6"/></g>
  <g font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1b1f24"><text x="150" y="160">thread (prefix 1)</text><text x="150" y="173">searches below</text><text x="250" y="160">thread</text><text x="250" y="173">prefix 2</text><text x="350" y="160">thread</text><text x="350" y="173">prefix 3</text><text x="450" y="160">thread</text><text x="450" y="173">prefix 4</text><text x="550" y="160">thread</text><text x="550" y="173">prefix 5</text></g>
  <text x="30" y="30" font-size="10" fill="#586069">empty board</text><text x="20" y="80" font-size="10" fill="#586069">row 0 placed</text><text x="20" y="130" font-size="10" fill="#586069">row 1 placed: the prefixes</text>
  <text x="350" y="206" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">The CPU lists the first few levels (the &ldquo;prefixes&rdquo;); each GPU thread owns everything below one prefix; the counts are added.</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure 47. Cutting the search tree at a fixed depth turns one big search into many independent ones. (Drawn two rows deep; the program uses 3 or 4.)</figcaption>
</figure>
<ol>
  <li><strong>On the CPU</strong>, list every valid way to place queens in the first few rows (the <em>prefixes</em>); for each, the three bit lists are all a thread needs. For a 14 &times; 14 board, four rows give 9,632 prefixes; for 15 &times; 15, 13,980.</li>
  <li><strong>On the GPU</strong>, launch one thread per prefix. Each runs the backtracking search for everything below its prefix and returns how many solutions it found. (A <em>map</em>, section 5.5.)</li>
  <li><strong>Add the counts</strong>, with the pair-adding passes of section 13.4. (A <em>reduction</em>, so the whole answer comes out of the GPU as one number.)</li>
</ol>
<p>It is the pattern &ldquo;split into independent chunks, map, reduce&rdquo; again, now applied to a search instead of to pixels. <strong>Why thousands of prefixes and not just a few?</strong> A GPU hides memory delays by having far more threads than cores (section 5.4), and the chunks are of very different sizes; many small pieces keep every core busy better than a few big ones.</p>

<h2>26.5 No recursion on a GPU: an explicit stack</h2>
<p>The recursive pseudo-code above relies on the program's <em>call stack</em>, which a GPU thread does not have. The fix is to do by hand what recursion does for us: keep an explicit <strong>stack</strong> with one entry per row (the three bit lists, and the squares not yet tried at that level), and an integer <code>level</code> saying how deep we are. Each pass of one loop does one of two things:</p>
<ul>
  <li>if the current level has a free square left, take the lowest one, and either count a solution (if it was the last row) or <strong>go down</strong>: push the new bit lists as the next level;</li>
  <li>if the level has no squares left, <strong>go back up</strong> one level (<code>level &minus; 1</code>); when <code>level</code> reaches &minus;1, this thread's subtree is finished.</li>
</ul>
<p>Where does each thread keep its stack? A kernel cannot declare a local array, but it can have a <strong><code>writable</code> array</strong> (section 6): one large scratch array with a private slice for every thread (<code>base = t &times; (n + 1) &times; 4</code>). Because no two threads ever touch the same slice, there are no races and no atomics are needed. The thread that is allowed to write is the one that owns the slice:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/queens.poly:19:64}}</code></pre>
<p>Notice that the CPU can run the <em>same function</em> on one prefix after another, which is how the program checks the GPU: it sums <code>queens(t, ...)</code> over all prefixes on the CPU and compares. Listing the prefixes is plain CPU code: read each number <code>0 &hellip; N<sup>depth</sup> &minus; 1</code> as <code>depth</code> base-N digits (one column per row), check them with the same bit lists, and keep the valid ones:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/queens.poly:66:100}}</code></pre>
<p>The host runs the search, then the pair-adding passes on the per-thread counts, and reads back one number:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:ideas/gpu/examples/queens.poly:91:113}}</code></pre>

<h2>26.6 How fast was it?</h2>
<p>These timings are from the program run on this Apple Silicon Mac (wall-clock milliseconds; the GPU time includes launching the search, waiting for it, and the addition passes, but not compiling the shader or listing the prefixes). The CPU column is <em>one</em> CPU core running the same algorithm:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Board N</th><th>Solutions</th><th>CPU, one core (ms)</th><th>GPU (ms)</th><th>Threads (prefixes)</th><th>GPU faster by</th></tr>
  <tr><td>8</td><td>92</td><td>0</td><td>about 5</td><td>140</td><td>(GPU slower: set-up cost)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>10</td><td>724</td><td>0</td><td>about 5</td><td>364</td><td>(GPU slower)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>12</td><td>14,200</td><td>8</td><td>about 7</td><td>4,080</td><td>about the same</td></tr>
  <tr><td>13</td><td>73,712</td><td>about 40</td><td>about 10</td><td>6,404</td><td>about 4&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>14</td><td>365,596</td><td>about 228</td><td>about 17</td><td>9,632</td><td>about 13&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>15</td><td>2,279,184</td><td>about 1,450</td><td>about 33</td><td>13,980</td><td>about 44&times;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>16</td><td>14,772,512</td><td>about 9,640</td><td>about 107</td><td>19,688</td><td>about 90&times;</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The pattern is the important lesson, not the exact numbers (they vary from run to run and machine to machine). <strong>For tiny boards the GPU loses</strong>: launching work costs milliseconds, and the CPU finishes before that. <strong>As the problem grows, the GPU's advantage grows</strong>, because the fixed cost is spread over more and more work, and there are enough independent subtrees to keep every core busy. This is the same &ldquo;launch overhead&rdquo; rule of section 5.4. An honest caveat: a modern CPU has several cores, so a fair rival to the GPU is the <em>multi-threaded</em> CPU program, which would narrow the gap by up to the core count. The GPU still wins at the large end, by a smaller factor.</p>

<h2>26.7 Why not a perfect speed-up? And how to do better</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Obstacle</th><th>Why it happens</th><th>Remedy</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Load imbalance</strong></td><td>Subtrees are very different sizes: a prefix with queens in awkward places dies quickly, another has a huge subtree. The group of threads finishes only when its slowest thread finishes.</td><td>more, smaller prefixes (a deeper cut); or a shared work queue so idle threads take new prefixes</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Divergence</strong></td><td>Threads in a SIMD group (section 5.2) take different branches at every step of a backtracking search, so the lock-step hardware runs both paths in turn.</td><td>sort prefixes so neighbours do similar work; restructure branches into arithmetic</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Memory traffic</strong></td><td>The per-thread stacks live in memory, not registers; every level is a read and a write.</td><td>keep the stack in fast threadgroup memory or registers (polyir cannot yet)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Redundant work</strong></td><td>Solutions come in mirrored pairs: flipping a solution left-to-right gives another.</td><td>search only the left half of the first row and double (taking care of the middle column when N is odd): almost twice as fast, on CPU and GPU alike</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>The algorithm itself</strong></td><td>Backtracking visits many dead ends.</td><td>smarter orderings and pruning, or entirely different methods (such as <em>dancing links</em>); record computations of this puzzle (N up to about 27) have used very large numbers of accelerator cards</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>26.8 What the example teaches</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>GPUs are not only for graphics.</strong> Anything that splits into many independent pieces of work is a candidate.</li>
  <li><strong>Think in trees, then cut the tree.</strong> A search tree's subtrees are independent; choosing the cut depth chooses the number of threads.</li>
  <li><strong>Recursion becomes an explicit stack,</strong> held in a private slice of a writable array.</li>
  <li><strong>Map, then reduce</strong> keeps the whole computation on the GPU and returns one number.</li>
  <li><strong>Irregular work wastes some of the hardware</strong>, but with enough threads it still wins decisively on big enough problems.</li>
  <li>The same shape fits other searches: counting Sudoku solutions, graph colouring, parts of SAT solving, branch-and-bound for the travelling salesman problem, and brute-force parameter searches.</li>
</ul>

<h2>26.9 Try it yourself</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Experiment</th><th>What to change in <code>queens.poly</code></th></tr>
  <tr><td>A smaller cut</td><td>set <code>depth = 2</code> for <code>n &gt;= 11</code>: far fewer, bigger tasks; the GPU slows down as the imbalance grows</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A deeper cut</td><td>set <code>depth = 5</code>: more, smaller tasks; faster until the prefix list itself costs more</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Check by hand</td><td>run <code>N = 6</code> (answer: 4 solutions) and <code>N = 5</code> (10)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Halve the work</td><td>in <code>listPrefixes</code>, keep only prefixes whose first column is in the left half, and double the total (add one more case for the middle column when N is odd)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Show the solutions</td><td>add a second <code>writable</code> array and let each thread record the columns of the first solution it finds</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ 26 -->
<h1 id="classics">27. Classic problems of GPU computing: a workbook</h1>

<p>Every field has a handful of &ldquo;classic problems&rdquo; that everyone in it meets early, because each one teaches an idea that keeps coming back. GPU computing has its own set. This chapter takes <strong>seven</strong> of them, explains each from scratch (you do not need to have heard of any of them), shows the kernel that solves it, and runs it on the GPU against the CPU. It then answers a question that naturally follows: <em>can a problem be split into parts, shared among several workers, even among several computers, and what does that cost?</em></p>
<p>Each problem is explained in the same five steps, so you can find your way around:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The problem</strong>, in plain words, with a tiny worked example you can check by hand.</li>
<li><strong>Where it shows up</strong> in real programs.</li>
<li><strong>Why it is interesting for a GPU</strong>: what makes it easy, hard, or a poor fit.</li>
<li><strong>The kernel.</strong> Read the comment above it first.</li>
<li><strong>What to notice</strong>, including what the program checks and what the timing taught.</li>
</ol>
<p>The programs are <code>ideas/gpu/examples/classics.poly</code> (Metal) and <code>classics-vulkan.poly</code> (Vulkan). They share one file of kernels, <code>shared/classics.poly</code>. Every kernel has the same shape: two input arrays <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>, a small array of settings <code>opts</code>, and one number as the result for each thread. For every problem the program runs the kernel on the GPU, runs the same kernel one thread after another on the CPU, and requires the answers to agree.</p>

<h2>27.1 The seven problems at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Problem</th><th>In one line</th><th>The idea it teaches</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>SAXPY</strong></td><td>compute <em>a</em> &times; <em>x</em> + <em>y</em> for every number in two lists</td><td><strong>map</strong>: one independent step per element; and what &ldquo;memory-bound&rdquo; means</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Prefix sum</strong></td><td>running totals of a list</td><td><strong>scan</strong>: a job that looks sequential, rebuilt so many steps run at once</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Matrix multiply</strong></td><td>combine two tables of numbers into a third</td><td><strong>gather</strong>: heavy arithmetic, and many threads reading the same data</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Game of Life</strong></td><td>cells that live and die by their neighbours</td><td><strong>stencil</strong>: each element looks at its neighbours; repeated steps; double buffering</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>N-body</strong></td><td>objects pulling on each other with gravity</td><td><strong>all pairs</strong>: every element interacts with every other</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Monte Carlo</strong></td><td>estimate &pi; by throwing random darts</td><td><strong>independent random trials</strong>; random numbers on a GPU</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Sorting</strong></td><td>put numbers in order</td><td><strong>depth versus work</strong>: why a parallel algorithm can lose to a sequential one</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Run once on the Apple M5 machine this guide was written on, the programs print this table (CPU and GPU times in milliseconds; &ldquo;launches&rdquo; is how many separate times the GPU was started):</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:snippets/classics-metal.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>Read it with two cautions. First, the GPU times include <em>everything</em> this guide&rsquo;s runtime does for each launch: making buffers and copying the data in (section 5.6), so a program that kept its data on the GPU between steps would do better than these numbers. Second, the times come from one machine and vary a little from run to run (the Game of Life row, for example, ranged from about 50 to 85 milliseconds over several runs). What matters is the <em>pattern</em>: four problems where the GPU wins clearly (matrix multiply, N-body, Monte Carlo and Life), one where it wins modestly (SAXPY), and two where it loses (prefix sum and sorting). Each section below explains why. (The Vulkan program prints the same table with different times, because Vulkan on a Mac goes through a translation layer; see section 5.8.)</p>

<h2>27.2 SAXPY: the &ldquo;hello world&rdquo; of GPU computing</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>You have two lists of numbers, <em>x</em> and <em>y</em>, and a single number <em>a</em>. For every position, compute <em>a</em> &times; <em>x</em> + <em>y</em>. For example, with <em>a</em> = 2, <em>x</em> = [1, 2, 3] and <em>y</em> = [10, 20, 30], the answer is [2&times;1+10, 2&times;2+20, 2&times;3+30] = [12, 24, 36].</p>
<p>The odd name is historical: <strong>S</strong>ingle-precision <strong>A</strong> times <strong>X</strong> <strong>P</strong>lus <strong>Y</strong>. It comes from BLAS (the Basic Linear Algebra Subprograms), the standard library of vector and matrix routines that scientific software has relied on for decades.</p>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>It is a building block inside bigger calculations: adjusting a position by a scaled velocity in a simulation, correcting an estimate in an iterative solver, blending one signal into another. By itself it is trivial, which is exactly why it is the first thing people try on a new GPU.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>Each answer depends on one element of <em>x</em>, one of <em>y</em> and the number <em>a</em>, and nothing else. No answer needs another, so every thread can work alone, and this is the pattern called a <strong>map</strong>. It also teaches a second lesson: the arithmetic is one multiply and one add, but each element needs two numbers read and one written. The GPU spends nearly all its time waiting for memory, not calculating. A job like that is <strong>memory-bound</strong> (section 5.3), and its speed is set by how fast memory moves, not by how many cores you have.</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:saxpy}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>The program does 4,194,304 of them and requires the GPU&rsquo;s answers to equal the CPU&rsquo;s <em>exactly</em> (the test numbers are chosen so that no rounding can differ). In the table the GPU won, but not by much (about 5 against 14 milliseconds), even though 4 million independent calculations is a perfect shape. The reason is the memory-bound lesson: this runtime copies 48 megabytes into buffers for the launch, and moving data costs as much as the arithmetic.</p>

<h2>27.3 Prefix sum (scan): when a chain of steps can still be parallel</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>A <strong>prefix sum</strong> (also called a <em>scan</em>) replaces every number in a list by the total of all the numbers up to and including it. For [3, 1, 7, 0, 4] the answer is [3, 4, 11, 11, 15]: 3; then 3+1; then 3+1+7; then 3+1+7+0; then 3+1+7+0+4. It is a running total, like the balance column in a bank statement.</p>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>More often than you would expect. Whenever many workers must each produce a different amount of output into one shared array, a prefix sum tells every worker <em>where its output starts</em>: filtering a list (which items survive, and where each goes), packing results, and the counting step inside a fast parallel sort (section 27.8). It is one of the most important building blocks in parallel programming.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>On a CPU it is one loop: <code>total = total + x[i]; out[i] = total</code>. But that loop is a chain: step <em>i</em> needs the answer of step <em>i</em> &minus; 1, so it looks impossible to run in parallel. The surprise is that it can be, with a cleverer method. The <strong>doubling method</strong> works in rounds. In the first round every element adds in its neighbour 1 place to the left; in the second, the element 2 places to the left; then 4, then 8, and so on. Each round every element is computed at once. Here is a list of eight 1s:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>start        1  1  1  1  1  1  1  1
add left 1   1  2  2  2  2  2  2  2     each element now holds the sum of up to 2 numbers
add left 2   1  2  3  4  4  4  4  4     ... up to 4 numbers
add left 4   1  2  3  4  5  6  7  8     ... up to 8 numbers: the finished running total</code></pre>
<p>After <em>k</em> rounds each element holds the sum of the last 2<sup>k</sup> numbers ending at itself, so log<sub>2</sub> <em>n</em> rounds are enough: 16 rounds for 65,536 numbers, 20 for a million. Each round is one launch, and a round needs the finished result of the previous round (so the launches cannot be merged).</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:scanStep}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>The program runs 16 rounds and requires an exact match with the CPU&rsquo;s one-pass loop. The timing table shows an <em>honest loss</em>: the GPU needs 8 milliseconds, the CPU under 1. Two reasons. The GPU does <strong>more total work</strong>: about <em>n</em> &times; log<sub>2</sub> <em>n</em> additions (a million) against <em>n</em> (65,536) for the CPU. And it needs 16 separate launches, each with its fixed launch cost (section 5.6). A parallel algorithm is not automatically a faster one. Smarter versions (a <em>work-efficient</em> scan) do much less extra work, and a scan is worth running on a GPU when the list is very long or when the data is already there; for a modest list on its own, the CPU is the right tool.</p>

<h2>27.4 Matrix multiplication: the workhorse</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>A <strong>matrix</strong> is a table of numbers in rows and columns. To multiply two of them, A and B, each entry of the answer C is a <em>dot product</em>: take a row of A and a column of B, multiply them entry by entry, and add. For</p>
<pre class="long"><code>A = [ 1 2 ]     B = [ 5 6 ]     C = [ 1&times;5+2&times;7   1&times;6+2&times;8 ]   =   [ 19  22 ]
    [ 3 4 ]         [ 7 8 ]         [ 3&times;5+4&times;7   3&times;6+4&times;8 ]       [ 43  50 ]</code></pre>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>Everywhere. The transformations of chapter 12 that rotate and move 3D shapes are matrix multiplications. A neural network is mostly a long series of them: this is why the machine-learning boom and the GPU boom happened together. Scientific simulation, image processing and search ranking all use them.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>Multiplying two <em>n</em> &times; <em>n</em> matrices takes <em>n</em><sup>3</sup> multiply-adds (for <em>n</em> = 256, that is 16.7 million), and every one of the <em>n</em><sup>2</sup> answers is independent of the others. That is lots of heavy, regular, independent work, the best case for a GPU. It also adds a new feature: each thread reads a whole row and a whole column (a <strong>gather</strong>), and neighbouring threads read the same row or column over and over. Good implementations exploit that by loading small blocks (<em>tiles</em>) of the matrices into the fast shared memory of a workgroup (section 6.7) so each number is fetched from slow memory once instead of many times. The kernel here is the plain version, without tiling.</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:matmul}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>Thread <code>i</code> makes output number <code>i</code>, and the two lines <code>row = i / n</code> and <code>col = i % n</code> turn that number back into a row and a column (section 23.3). The program checks two things: a small matrix times the <em>identity matrix</em> (all zeros except 1s down the diagonal) must come back unchanged, which is the matrix version of multiplying by 1; and a 256 &times; 256 product must equal the CPU&rsquo;s exactly. This is a clear win: about 37 milliseconds on the CPU against 2 on the GPU, from a kernel with no tricks.</p>

<h2>27.5 Conway&rsquo;s Game of Life: a stencil that repeats</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>The <strong>Game of Life</strong> was invented by the mathematician John Conway in 1970. It is not a game with players. You have a grid of cells, each either <em>alive</em> or <em>dead</em>, and a rule that turns the whole grid into the next <em>generation</em>:</p>
<ul>
<li>A live cell with 2 or 3 live neighbours (of its 8 surrounding cells) <strong>stays alive</strong>; otherwise it dies (of loneliness or crowding).</li>
<li>A dead cell with <strong>exactly 3</strong> live neighbours <strong>comes alive</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>From these two rules, surprisingly rich behaviour appears. A row of three live cells flips between a horizontal bar and a vertical bar forever (a &ldquo;blinker&rdquo;); and a pattern of five cells called a <em>glider</em> crawls across the grid, moving one cell diagonally every four generations:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>blinker:   . . .      . # .         glider:   . # .      . . .      . . .
           # # #  -&gt;   . # .                   . . #  -&gt;  # . #  -&gt;  . . #  ...
           . . .      . # .                   # # #      . # #      # . #</code></pre>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>Life itself is a toy, but it is the standard example of a <strong>cellular automaton</strong>: a grid where every cell follows the same simple rule using only its neighbours. The same shape appears in real simulations of heat flow, fluids and weather, and in every image filter that reads the pixels around a pixel (the blur of chapter 24 is one). Such a calculation is called a <strong>stencil</strong>.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>Within one generation, each cell&rsquo;s new state depends only on the <em>previous</em> generation of its neighbours, so every cell can be computed at the same moment. Between generations, though, there is a strict order: generation 2 needs all of generation 1 finished. So Life is many fully parallel steps in a sequence: one launch per generation, and the launches act as barriers.</p>
<p>One rule of stencils is easy to get wrong: <strong>you cannot update the board in place.</strong> If cell 5 overwrote itself before cell 6 looked at it, cell 6 would see a mixture of old and new states. The kernel therefore reads from one array and writes to a different one, and the host swaps the roles for the next generation. This is called <strong>double buffering</strong> (or ping-pong), and the program does it by feeding each launch&rsquo;s output in as the next launch&rsquo;s input.</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:lifeStep}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>The board wraps round at its edges (the cell left of column 0 is the last column), done with the remainder operator. The program checks a property you can see: a glider on a small board must reappear four generations later one cell down and one cell to the right. Then it runs a 256 &times; 256 random board for 200 generations on both GPU and CPU and requires identical boards. The GPU wins (roughly 50&ndash;85 ms against 250 ms), but a lot of its time is the 200 separate launches, which is why the margin is smaller than for matrix multiplication.</p>

<h2>27.6 N-body gravity: every element meets every other</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>You have <em>n</em> objects (stars, say) at various positions, each with a mass. Gravity makes every object pull on every other one, harder when the other is heavier or closer. To move the objects forward in time, you first need the total pull on each object: the sum, over all the other objects, of that object&rsquo;s pull. The pull of object <em>j</em> on object <em>i</em> points from <em>i</em> towards <em>j</em>, has strength proportional to <em>mass<sub>j</sub></em> divided by the squared distance, and is added up over every <em>j</em>.</p>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>Astronomers use it to simulate galaxies and star clusters. The same pattern, every particle interacting with every other, is used for molecules (atoms pushing and pulling each other) and in some physics-based graphics. It is one of the oldest uses of supercomputers.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>The answers for different objects are independent, and each answer is a loop over all <em>n</em> objects: <em>n</em> &times; <em>n</em> pair interactions in all (for 2,048 bodies, 4.2 million). It is heavy, regular arithmetic with the same steps for every thread: very good for a GPU. The program uses two threads per body, one for the x part of the pull and one for the y part. The simple <strong>all-pairs</strong> method gets expensive for very large <em>n</em>, so astronomers use smarter methods that group faraway stars together; those are much harder to run on a GPU, because they are irregular and branch a lot (these are the &ldquo;poor fit&rdquo; cases of section 5.10).</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:accel}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>A small number (0.01) is added to each squared distance so that a body does not pull on <em>itself</em> with an infinite force (its distance to itself is zero). The program compares with the CPU (allowing a tiny rounding difference, because the GPU may round differently) and checks <strong>Newton&rsquo;s third law</strong>: every pull has an equal and opposite pull back, so the total force (mass times acceleration, added over all bodies) must be nearly zero. A mistake in the sign or the indexing would break that check immediately. The timing is a clear win: about 22 milliseconds on the CPU against 2 on the GPU.</p>

<h2>27.7 Monte Carlo: estimating &pi; with random darts</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Take a square one unit wide, and inside it a quarter of a circle of radius 1, with its centre at one corner. The square has area 1; the quarter circle has area &pi;/4 (about 0.785). Now throw darts at random so they land anywhere in the square with equal chance. The fraction that land <em>inside</em> the quarter circle should be about &pi;/4. So: <strong>count the hits, divide by the number of throws, multiply by 4, and you have an estimate of &pi;.</strong> With 4 darts of which 3 land inside, the estimate is 4 &times; 3/4 = 3.0. With millions of darts it gets much closer to 3.14159&hellip;</p>
<h3>Where it shows up</h3>
<p>The method is called <strong>Monte Carlo</strong> (after the casino): answer a question by running many random experiments and counting. Banks use it to price financial contracts, physicists to follow particles through material, and computer graphics uses it to work out how light bounces around a scene. It is the method of choice when an exact formula is out of reach.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>Each dart is completely independent of the others, so this is one of the best shapes there is: millions of experiments, no communication, the same steps each. Two things are worth noticing.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Accuracy grows slowly.</strong> The error shrinks like 1/&radic;<em>N</em>: 100 times more darts gives only 10 times more accuracy. So Monte Carlo needs <em>huge</em> numbers of trials, which suits a GPU.</li>
<li><strong>Random numbers on a GPU.</strong> Thousands of threads each need their own stream of random numbers, and they must not all produce the same stream. A <em>pseudo-random generator</em> computes each next number from the last by a fixed formula, so the numbers look random but are completely determined by where you start. The kernel starts each thread from a different seed (its thread number). The formula used is a <em>linear congruential generator</em>: <code>next = (1103515245 &times; state + 12345) mod 2<sup>31</sup></code>. Because it uses only whole-number arithmetic, the GPU and the CPU produce exactly the same sequence, which lets the program check them <em>exactly</em> with no tolerance.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:hits}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>Each of 65,536 threads throws 256 darts, 16.7 million in all, and the program prints its estimate (about 3.141) and requires it to be within 0.01 of &pi;. It also requires every thread&rsquo;s count to equal the CPU&rsquo;s. Timing: about 160 milliseconds on the CPU against about 22 on the GPU.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>A real bug this example found.</strong> The first Vulkan version of this program gave different counts from the CPU, thread by thread, while the Metal version agreed. The cause was in the GLSL that polyir wrote: GLSL reads a number such as <code>2654435761</code> as a <em>32-bit</em> value unless it carries a 64-bit marker, and that number does not fit in 32 bits, so the shader used a wrong constant. The compiler now writes such constants with the marker (<code>2654435761l</code>), and a test checks it. Two things to take from this: the CPU-versus-GPU comparison is what caught it, and a program that &ldquo;looks right&rdquo; on one GPU interface can still be wrong on another. (Making the Vulkan runtime request 64-bit integer support from the GPU is part of the same fix.)</div>

<h2>27.8 Sorting: when a parallel algorithm loses</h2>
<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Put a list of numbers in increasing order: [5, 2, 9, 1] becomes [1, 2, 5, 9]. Sorting is among the most common tasks in programming: databases, search results, scheduling, and many other algorithms use it as a step.</p>
<h3>Why it is interesting for a GPU</h3>
<p>Fast CPU sorts (quicksort, merge sort) decide what to do next from the data they see, and move numbers around in a data-dependent order: hard to turn into thousands of simultaneous steps. Parallel sorts use a different plan: <em>fixed</em> patterns of compare-and-swap, where two neighbouring numbers are looked at and swapped if they are out of order, so each step is the same for every pair.</p>
<p>The simplest is the <strong>odd-even transposition sort</strong>. It works in <em>phases</em>. In an even phase, compare the pairs (0,1), (2,3), (4,5)&hellip; and put the smaller number on the left; in an odd phase, compare (1,2), (3,4), (5,6)&hellip;. Repeat for <em>n</em> phases and the list is sorted. Sorting [4, 3, 2, 1]:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>start               4  3  2  1
phase 0: (0,1)(2,3) 3  4  1  2
phase 1: (1,2)      3  1  4  2
phase 0: (0,1)(2,3) 1  3  2  4
phase 1: (1,2)      1  2  3  4      sorted after 4 phases (n = 4)</code></pre>
<p>All the pairs in one phase are independent, so a phase is one launch with one thread per element: each thread works out whether it is the left or the right of its pair and returns the minimum or the maximum.</p>
<h3>The kernel</h3>
<pre class="long"><code>{{def:ideas/gpu/examples/shared/classics.poly:oddEvenStep}}</code></pre>
<h3>What to notice</h3>
<p>The program sorts 2,048 numbers by 2,048 launches and requires the exact same order as a plain CPU sort. And here the GPU <strong>loses badly</strong>: about 410 milliseconds against 2. This is the most important lesson of the chapter. The sort needs <em>n</em> phases, each a separate launch with its fixed cost (about 0.2 milliseconds here), so the launch overhead alone is most of the time, and the method does about <em>n</em><sup>2</sup>/2 comparisons where a good CPU sort does about <em>n</em> log <em>n</em>. The count of dependent rounds (the <em>depth</em>, here <em>n</em>) matters as much as the number of independent pieces in each round.</p>
<p>Real GPU sorts avoid this. <strong>Bitonic sort</strong> needs only about log<sub>2</sub> <em>n</em> &times; (log<sub>2</sub> <em>n</em> + 1) / 2 rounds (210 for a million numbers, instead of a million), and <strong>radix sort</strong> is built from the prefix sums of section 27.3. Those are what libraries use. Odd-even sort is here because you can understand it in one minute, and because its failure is instructive.</p>

<h2>27.9 Splitting a problem into parts: partitioning, and what grid computing is</h2>
<p>Two terms are worth defining before the question, because they sound similar and mean different things.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Partitioning</strong></td><td>Dividing a problem&rsquo;s data (or work) into pieces, so different workers can handle different pieces at the same time. Sometimes called <em>decomposition</em>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Grid computing</strong> (or <em>distributed computing</em>)</td><td>Using many separate computers, connected by a network, as one big machine: a cluster in a university, or thousands of volunteers&rsquo; home computers over the internet (as in projects such as Folding@home, which simulates how proteins fold). A job server hands out pieces of a big problem, and the machines send answers back.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>The &ldquo;grid&rdquo; of a GPU launch</strong></td><td>A different use of the same word. GPU programmers call all the threads of one launch the <em>grid</em>. It has nothing to do with a network of computers.</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Does partitioning work on a GPU? Yes, and it is what a GPU launch already is</h3>
<p>The whole idea of this guide is partitioning: the data is split into elements, each element is given to a thread, and the threads are grouped into workgroups (section 5.2). So a GPU job is a partitioned job. You can also partition <em>one level up</em>, into several launches. There are good reasons: the data may be too big to fit in the GPU&rsquo;s memory at once; you may have more than one GPU and want to share the work; or you may want to overlap copying one piece with computing another.</p>
<p>The program shows two partitions working, and each checks that the parts add up to the whole <em>exactly</em>:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{lines:snippets/classics-metal.txt:11:13}}</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><strong>Monte Carlo, in 4 launches.</strong> The 65,536 threads are divided into four launches of 16,384, and each launch is told where its thread numbers start. Every thread&rsquo;s count equals what it was in the single big launch. This works perfectly because the pieces need <em>nothing</em> from each other: no communication at all.</li>
<li><strong>Game of Life, in 2 parts.</strong> The board is cut into a top half and a bottom half, each run as its own launch. Now the pieces <em>do</em> need something: a cell on the cut needs the row of cells on the other side. So each part is given a copy of its neighbour&rsquo;s boundary row (a &ldquo;ghost&rdquo; or <em>halo</em> row) above and below, and these rows must be swapped again <strong>every generation</strong>, because the boundary changes each time. After 20 generations the two halves stitched together equal the whole board. (The program also fails with exit code 13 if the ghost row is wrong; I checked that.)</li>
</ul>
<p>The comparison between these two is the key to the whole subject. Partitioning always costs <strong>communication</strong>, the information that must pass between the parts. Monte Carlo needs none. Life needs a boundary exchange, and the amount of boundary (one row per cut) is small next to the amount of work inside each part (all the rows), so bigger parts waste less: the work grows with the <em>area</em> of a part, the communication with its <em>edge</em>.</p>
<h3>Does grid computing work with GPUs? Yes, for the right jobs</h3>
<p>Yes. Each computer in a grid can have a GPU and use it for its piece. Folding@home, for example, released a GPU version of its client years ago (a 2007 research paper already mentions it), and supercomputers are built from nodes that each contain several GPUs; research on such machines studies how neighbouring GPUs, in the same node or in different nodes, swap boundary data. The jobs that suit this are those with a high ratio of <em>computing</em> to <em>communication</em>: small inputs, small outputs, a lot of arithmetic in between.</p>
<h3>Why it does not always work</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Obstacle</th><th>Why</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>A GPU is one device, not a network.</strong></td><td>A GPU cannot send a message to another computer. The CPU (and the operating system) does the network work, hands the GPU a piece, and carries the result away. Grid computing is therefore organised by CPUs; the GPU only speeds up each piece.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>The network is far slower than the GPU.</strong></td><td>A GPU moves data to and from its memory at many gigabytes a second (the M5&rsquo;s memory moves 153 GB/s, section 5.7); a home internet connection moves a small fraction of that. If a piece takes longer to send than to compute, the grid is slower than one machine. This is why SAXPY, which does one multiply per number moved, is a poor grid job even though it is perfect on a single GPU.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Tightly coupled problems.</strong></td><td>If every part needs data from every other part at every step (N-body: every body is pulled by every other body), the parts must exchange everything constantly. That needs very fast links between them, as in a supercomputer, and not a spread of machines on the internet.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Trusting the answers.</strong></td><td>Volunteer machines can fail or return wrong results, and floating-point arithmetic may round differently on different GPUs and drivers. A platform called BOINC (used by many volunteer projects) therefore runs each job on two unrelated computers and compares the answers, running a third copy if they differ; because exact matches are often impossible, projects can supply a checking function that accepts values within a tolerance. This costs extra work.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Memory limits.</strong></td><td>Each piece must fit in the GPU&rsquo;s memory, which is usually smaller than the computer&rsquo;s. Big problems need parts small enough to fit, and then more of them.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Pieces must be big enough.</strong></td><td>Sending a piece, starting a launch and returning an answer all have fixed costs (section 5.6). A piece that finishes in a millisecond is not worth sending across a network.</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Several GPUs at once: the Vulkan multi-device example</h3>
<p>The Vulkan runtime can open a separate <em>context</em> (its own device, queue and memory) on every GPU the computer has, and can start a launch <em>without waiting</em> for it, so that several GPUs run at the same time. <code>ideas/gpu/examples/multigpu-vulkan.poly</code> uses both to split the two jobs above across workers. A &ldquo;worker&rdquo; is one context. Each worker has its own memory, which makes the point of the whole section concrete: <strong>the workers cannot see each other&rsquo;s data</strong>. For Monte Carlo that does not matter. For Life, each worker owns a strip of rows, and every generation the CPU copies each strip&rsquo;s edge rows into its neighbours&rsquo; inputs, because the CPU is the only thing that can reach all the memories. One run of it here:</p>
<pre class="long"><code>{{file:snippets/multigpu-vulkan.txt}}</code></pre>
<p>Read the second line carefully. This laptop has <strong>one</strong> GPU, so the program fell back to making <em>two separate logical devices on it</em>. Vulkan allows that, and each behaves like a separate GPU with its own memory, so the splitting code, the separate memories and the boundary exchange are all genuinely exercised. But the hardware is shared, so there is no speed-up: the Monte Carlo job took 146 milliseconds on one worker and 138 on two, within the run-to-run variation. On a machine with two or more GPUs the same program makes one worker for each GPU (up to four). <strong>I have not been able to run that case</strong>, so the claim that it would then run faster is what the model predicts, not something I measured here. What the run does show is that every part matched the whole exactly, that the program also works with four workers (checked once), and that a wrong boundary row is caught (the program exits with 13).</p>
<p>Starting every worker&rsquo;s launch before waiting for any of them is what lets the GPUs overlap (the &ldquo;B&rdquo; timeline of Figure @cpugpu in section 5.6). Waiting after each launch, as the single-GPU runtime calls do, would run the workers one after another and give no benefit however many GPUs there were.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>What this guide&rsquo;s runtimes can do.</strong> The Metal runtime opens only the system&rsquo;s default device. The Vulkan runtime can open any of the computer&rsquo;s GPUs (<code>vulkanGpuCount</code>, <code>vulkanStartOn</code>) and can submit without waiting (<code>vulkanDispatchBuffersAsync</code>, then <code>vulkanFinish</code>). Neither does anything across a network: spreading a job over several <em>computers</em> would be organised by CPUs and the operating system, as the table above explains.</div>
<table>
  <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Splits into parts?</th><th>Suits a grid of computers?</th></tr>
  <tr><td>SAXPY</td><td>yes, trivially, no communication</td><td>poor: almost no arithmetic per number sent</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prefix sum</td><td>yes: scan each part, then add each part&rsquo;s starting total to the next (a small amount of communication)</td><td>poor: little arithmetic per number</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matrix multiply</td><td>yes: give each worker some rows of A</td><td>fair for big matrices: every worker needs all of B, but the arithmetic grows faster (<em>n</em><sup>3</sup>) than the data (<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Game of Life</td><td>yes, with boundary rows swapped every generation</td><td>poor over a slow network; good between GPUs joined by fast links</td></tr>
  <tr><td>N-body (all pairs)</td><td>yes, but every part needs every body&rsquo;s position</td><td>poor: all-to-all communication</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monte Carlo</td><td>yes, with no communication</td><td><strong>excellent</strong>: tiny input, tiny output, independent, lots of arithmetic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sorting</td><td>yes: sort each part, then merge (the merge is the hard part)</td><td>fair: needs the parts&rsquo; data brought together</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The question to ask of any problem is the same as in section 5.10, one level up: <strong>are there many independent pieces, and does each piece do enough work to repay the cost of moving it?</strong></p>

<h2>27.10 What the seven problems taught, together</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Pattern</th><th>GPU rounds (depth)</th><th>Result here</th><th>The lesson</th></tr>
  <tr><td>SAXPY</td><td>map</td><td>1</td><td>GPU wins modestly</td><td>memory-bound: moving data is the cost</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prefix sum</td><td>scan</td><td>log<sub>2</sub> <em>n</em></td><td>GPU loses</td><td>extra work and many launches can cancel the gain</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matrix multiply</td><td>gather</td><td>1</td><td>GPU wins clearly</td><td>lots of independent, regular arithmetic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Game of Life</td><td>stencil</td><td>one per generation</td><td>GPU wins</td><td>double buffering; launches act as barriers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>N-body</td><td>all pairs</td><td>1</td><td>GPU wins clearly</td><td>heavy arithmetic per thread suits a GPU</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monte Carlo</td><td>independent trials</td><td>1</td><td>GPU wins clearly</td><td>independent work; random numbers need a seed per thread</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sorting</td><td>compare-and-swap</td><td><em>n</em></td><td>GPU loses badly</td><td>depth matters: choose a parallel algorithm with few rounds</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Exercises, with answers</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1. How many rounds does the doubling prefix sum need for 1,000,000 numbers?</td><td>20, because 2<sup>20</sup> = 1,048,576 is the first power of 2 that reaches a million.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2. How many multiply-adds are in a 100 &times; 100 matrix multiplication?</td><td>100<sup>3</sup> = 1,000,000: each of the 10,000 entries is a dot product of 100 pairs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3. Why must the Life kernel not write its answer into the same array it reads?</td><td>A cell updated early would be seen, in its new state, by the cells that are computed after it, so they would use a mixture of old and new generations. Reading from one array and writing to another avoids that.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4. Using 100 times more darts in the &pi; estimate: how much more accurate?</td><td>About 10 times (the error shrinks like 1/&radic;<em>N</em>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5. Why does sorting with <em>n</em> launches lose, even though each phase is fully parallel?</td><td>It needs <em>n</em> phases one after another; each launch has a fixed cost of about 0.2 ms here, and the method does far more comparisons than a CPU sort.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>6. You cut a 1,000 &times; 1,000 Life board into 4 horizontal strips. How many boundary rows must each strip receive per generation?</td><td>Two (one from the strip above and one from the strip below), 1,000 numbers each, against 250 rows of its own: so a small cost compared with the work.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7. Which of the seven would you give to thousands of volunteers&rsquo; computers over the internet?</td><td>Monte Carlo: tiny input, tiny output, no communication between the pieces, and a lot of arithmetic each.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><strong>How it was checked.</strong> Each problem runs on the GPU and is compared with the same kernel on the CPU: exact equality for SAXPY, prefix sum, matrix multiply, Game of Life, Monte Carlo and sorting (the test data are small whole numbers, or the arithmetic is all whole-number, so no rounding can differ), and a small tolerance for N-body. Property checks held as well: a glider moves one cell diagonally in 4 generations, a matrix times the identity is unchanged, the forces add up to nearly zero, the estimate of &pi; is within 0.01, and the two partitioned runs equal their whole. The programs pass on Metal, on Vulkan with GLSL shaders and on Vulkan with the SPIR-V written directly by polyir. Two deliberate breakages were caught (dropping the &ldquo;add the first element&rdquo; case in the prefix sum exits with code 13, and a wrong ghost row in the Life partition exits with 13); my first attempt at the prefix-sum check did <em>not</em> catch that breakage, because the test data started with a 0, so the data now start at 1. The statements about volunteer computing come from the BOINC and Folding@home literature (as quoted in search results), and those about halo exchange between GPUs from papers on multi-GPU simulations; I did not run anything across several GPUs or computers.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ 14 -->
<h1 id="native">28. Why GPU programs are native-only</h1>

<p>Talking to Metal or Vulkan means calling operating-system libraries (<code>libobjc</code>, the Metal framework, the
Vulkan loader). A program that does that must be built as a real executable with <code>clang</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>polyir app.poly --out app</code> works. The command-line tool notices which host runtime the program declares
      (<code>MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice</code> means Metal, <code>vkCreateInstance</code> means Vulkan) and adds the link
      flags itself: <code>-framework Metal -framework Foundation -lobjc</code>, or <code>-lvulkan</code> with the loader's
      folder (found with <code>brew --prefix</code>) and a run-time search path.</li>
  <li><code>polyir app.poly --jit</code> is refused at once with: <em>this program uses the GPU host runtime (Metal or Vulkan),
      which only works in a native build</em>. The JIT runs inside the compiler tool, which does not load those libraries.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <em>shader text</em> is different: <code>--emit-msl</code> and <code>--emit-glsl</code> never touch a GPU, so they work on
any machine and for any target.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ 11 -->
<h1 id="workflow">29. Workflow, tests and tools</h1>

<h2>Everything needed (on a Mac)</h2>
<pre><code>brew install glslang spirv-tools vulkan-loader molten-vk   # Vulkan: GLSL compiler, SPIR-V validator and tools, loader, Vulkan-on-Metal
# the polyir tool itself:
cd lang/cli &amp;&amp; ../../../nina/build/nina up . build        # produces lang/cli/build/out/polyir-lang</code></pre>

<h2>The whole GPU workflow</h2>
<pre><code># 1. write a program with kernel funcs and a host main (ideas/gpu/examples/smooth.poly)
# 2a. Metal
polyir --emit-msl smooth.poly &gt; smooth.metal
polyir smooth.poly --out smooth &amp;&amp; ./smooth

# 2b. Vulkan
polyir --emit-glsl smooth-vulkan.poly | awk '/^\/\/ ==== /{f=$3} f{print &gt; f}'   # splits into smooth.comp
glslangValidator -V smooth.comp -o smooth.spv
polyir smooth-vulkan.poly --out smooth-vulkan &amp;&amp; ./smooth-vulkan</code></pre>

<h2>The test scripts</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Command (from <code>polyir/</code>)</th><th>What it checks</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cd tests &amp;&amp; ../../../nina/build/nina up . build &amp;&amp; ./build/out/polyir-tests</code></td><td>about 100 numeric checks of the compiler, diagnostics, kernel rules and the exact MSL/GLSL text, <em>and</em> every check written in polyir itself: each <code>core/tests/programs/*Tests.poly</code> file is compiled and run through the JIT and every function named <code>checkSomething()</code> in it must measure 0 off (192 checks: the Wave, Sound, stereo, WAV, resampling, biquad, stream, logical-operator, prelude, GPU-function, float-remainder and container tests). The Nina-side checks also cover where errors are reported (<code>src/DiagnosticChecks.nina</code>, using the programs in <code>core/tests/programs/fixtures/diagnostics/</code>). Prints <em>all checks passed</em></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/golden.sh diff tests/golden/base</code></td><td>the LLVM IR of all 33 example programs is byte-for-byte unchanged (a safety net for refactors; recapture with <code>golden.sh capture</code> after a change you meant to make)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/perf.sh check</code></td><td>compiling all examples is not slower than the recorded baseline</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/vulkan.sh</code></td><td>every kernel of every example becomes SPIR-V twice: through GLSL and glslang, and written directly by <code>--emit-spirv</code> and checked with <code>spirv-val</code> (208 kernels each way)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/nina-kernels.sh</code></td><td>the same five computations run through Nina's own <code>@Kernel("gpu")</code> path and through polyir, on the real GPU; every number must agree to a thousandth (section 9). Skipped without Apple Silicon or this repository's own <code>nina</code> build</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/gpu.sh</code></td><td>builds and runs the GPU examples on real hardware (Metal and Vulkan) and the native sound examples (<code>compose</code>, <code>studio</code>, and <code>live</code>, which streams to a real audio device at volume 0), and checks that <code>--jit</code> refuses them; each Vulkan example runs twice, once with glslang's modules and once with the directly written SPIR-V. Exit code 10 (no GPU or no audio device) is reported as a skip</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr>
  <tr><td>exit 10</td><td>no GPU, no audio device, or the Vulkan loader cannot find MoltenVK (install <code>molten-vk</code> and <code>vulkan-loader</code>)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>exit 11</td><td>the <code>.metal</code> or <code>.spv</code> file is not in the current folder; run the program from the folder that holds it</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>kernel check failed: ... is not a math function</code></td><td>the kernel calls something other than a math function or an earlier kernel; declare math functions with <code>extern func</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td>glslang: cannot convert from int64_t to int</td><td>an older build without the explicit conversions; rebuild the tool</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>expected IDENT (found ':')</code></td><td>a variable or parameter is named like a keyword (<code>index</code>, <code>kernel</code>, <code>uniform</code>, ...)</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ 12 -->
<h1 id="limits">30. Limits, design decisions and what is next</h1>

<h2>Design decisions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>GPU output comes from the HIR, never from LLVM IR.</strong> The HIR keeps <code>if</code>/loops structured, which both GPU formats require.</li>
  <li><strong>Existing behaviour was protected while the compiler was restructured.</strong> The refactor to the HIR kept the generated LLVM IR byte-identical on all 32 examples (the golden test), and the throughput check ensured the compiler did not get slower.</li>
  <li><strong>Host runtimes are written in polyir itself</strong> rather than C++: the language is expressive enough, and it exercises the compiler on real FFI code.</li>
  <li><strong>Containers are references.</strong> A <code>mutarray</code>, <code>sparse</code> or <code>mutmap</code> value is a pointer to a header made with <code>calloc</code> when the container is created (24, 72 and 40 bytes; every field is 8 bytes, at fixed offsets written down in <code>src/Containers.nina</code>). A <code>push</code> or a write updates the header in place, so every name for the container sees it, including through a function the container was passed to. The old design copied the header, which lost a callee's pushes and could leave the caller pointing at freed memory. Consequences: writes to a <code>sparse</code> or <code>mutmap</code> work on any name (a <code>let</code>, a parameter, a struct field), the compiler no longer needs to re-bind a variable after a <code>push</code>, a global cannot be a container (it would be a null pointer; the compiler says so), and the IR of the six examples that use these containers changed (the other 27 are byte-for-byte the same).</li>
  <li><strong>Float <code>%</code> is exact on every output.</strong> Found by the cross-check with Nina's own GPU path (see "A second opinion" in section 9); fixed separately for Metal, GLSL and SPIR-V, and for the CPU.</li>
  <li><strong>Errors name their file.</strong> The import step leaves a marker for each file in the list of declarations, and a diagnostic reads its function's file from it, so no phase of the compiler had to change its signature to carry file names (section 4).</li>
  <li><strong>Nina's own <code>@Kernel("gpu")</code> is kept, as a second opinion.</strong> Retiring it was part of the plan and was investigated (see "What is next"); the blockers are real, so it stays as an independent implementation that the cross-check compares polyir with.</li>
  <li><strong>Kernels also run on the CPU.</strong> That makes every GPU result checkable against a trusted reference.</li>
  <li><strong>Shader text is target-independent; running it is native-only.</strong></li>
  <li><strong><code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> short-circuit, and <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds tighter than <code>||</code>, as in C.</strong> Both were wrong at first: the operators were lowered to a bitwise <code>and</code>/<code>or</code> of <em>both</em> sides, with equal precedence. The bug showed itself the first time a guard mattered: the WAV reader, written as <code>if (size &lt; 12 || readWord(bytes, 0) != ...)</code>, crashed on a missing file because the right side read a null pointer. The compiler now lowers an <code>if</code> or <code>for</code> condition, and a <code>&amp;&amp;</code>/<code>||</code> stored by <code>let</code>, <code>var</code>, assignment or <code>return</code>, to jumps (a left side that decides the answer jumps straight past the right side; a stored one merges a &ldquo;true&rdquo; and a &ldquo;false&rdquo; block with a phi). Inside a larger expression (an argument, an operand of <code>==</code>) both sides are still evaluated; put it in a <code>let</code>. Fourteen checks in <code>core/tests/programs/LogicTests.poly</code> cover it (null guards, call counts, loop conditions, precedence). The GPU emitters were never affected: MSL and GLSL already short-circuit natively.</li>
  <li><strong>Libraries are tested by programs written in the language itself.</strong> <code>core/tests/programs/*Tests.poly</code> are ordinary polyir files whose <code>check</code> functions return how far a measurement is from its expected value; the Nina test driver finds them by name and runs them through the JIT. A test is a function, written in the language it tests.</li>
  <li><strong>The Math library is part of the language, not a special case in the compiler.</strong> The compiler does not know what <code>fract</code> means: it holds the text of <code>core/stdlib/Math.poly</code>, adds the part a program calls for when (and only when) a name is unknown, and checks again. The library is therefore ordinary polyir that is tested, imported and used on the GPU like any other.</li>
  <li><strong>The operating system is chosen by an import line.</strong> <code>{os}</code> in an import path becomes <code>macos</code>, <code>linux</code> or <code>windows</code>, so one source file can use the right audio output on each system without conditional compilation in the language.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Known limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Kernel shape: one returned result per element. A line or a two-dimensional grid of threads (no three-dimensional grids); a fixed workgroup of 256 threads; workgroup memory is one array of one element type per parameter; atomic updates on 32-bit integers and (as a compare-and-swap loop) on floats, but none on workgroup memory and none on 64-bit values; no subgroup (SIMD-group) operations; a kernel with workgroup memory cannot be called by another kernel and takes no element inputs.</li>
  <li>The original <code>metalDispatch</code> and <code>vulkanDispatch</code> bind at most four buffers; the <code>...Buffers</code>, <code>...2D</code> and <code>...Groups</code> calls take any number, up to what the GPU API allows.</li>
  <li>The Metal host runtime works on Apple Silicon only. The Vulkan runtime uses queue family 0 and host-visible memory (no staging copies).</li>
  <li>The audio runtime (<code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>) has been run only on macOS (AudioQueue, with live callbacks). The Linux (ALSA) and Windows (waveOut) back ends are written and type-checked but <strong>untested</strong> on real machines. Output is 32-bit float samples with any number of channels; there is no input (recording) and no choice of output device.</li>
  <li><code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> evaluate both sides when nested inside a larger expression (see the design decisions); an <code>if</code>/<code>for</code> condition or a stored value short-circuits.</li>
  <li>Push constants: uniform values must be 4-byte scalars laid out in declaration order; vector uniforms need std430 alignment that the host does not compute for you.</li>
  <li>On Vulkan a kernel that reads a <code>uniform</code> array can only be called from a kernel that has a <code>uniform</code> array of the <em>same name</em> (GLSL has no pointers, so the called function reads the array by name).</li>
  <li><code>index</code>, <code>writable</code> and <code>workgroup</code> are reserved words.</li>
  <li>The direct SPIR-V writer (<code>--emit-spirv</code>): push-constant values must be 32-bit numbers; 8- and 16-bit integers are computed as 32-bit; SPIR-V 1.0 with <code>Uniform</code> and <code>BufferBlock</code> storage buffers; no optimization; the hex text needs <code>xxd</code> to become a binary file (the Vulkan runtime reads the text directly).</li>
  <li>A global cannot be a <code>mutarray</code>, <code>sparse</code> or <code>mutmap</code> (a global is set up before any code runs and a container is allocated when it is created); make the container in a function and pass it to the functions that use it. The containers themselves are simple, not fast: a <code>mutarray</code> grows by one element per <code>push</code>, a <code>mutmap</code> has the number of slots it was made with (inserting more keys is undefined) and a <code>sparse</code> scans every entry to read one.</li>
  <li>An error inside the code the compiler generates for a generic <code>struct Box&lt;T&gt;</code> does not name a file (the instances belong to no file); every other error does. The unused-<code>var</code> lint is syntactic and checks the file being compiled, not the libraries it imports.</li>
  <li>On a GPU, float <code>%</code> is exact while the quotient times the divisor stays below 2<sup>24</sup>; beyond that a float cannot hold the whole part and the GPU's answer can differ from the CPU's. The other math functions (<code>sinf</code>, <code>expf</code>, ...) keep the GPU's fast-math results, which can differ from the CPU's in the last digits (the conformance tests compare floats with a small tolerance for that reason).</li>
</ul>

<h2>What is next</h2>
<ol>
  <li>More kernel shapes still missing: several returned results, three-dimensional grids, workgroup atomics and subgroup operations, a choice of workgroup size.</li>
  <li><strong>Nina's <code>@Kernel("gpu")</code>.</strong> <em>Investigated, not retired.</em> Rewiring the decorator to polyir's GPU path would let the older C++ Metal generator and bridge go, but three things stand in the way. (1) <em>A bootstrap cycle:</em> the polyir compiler is written in Nina and built by <code>ninac</code>, while the decorator is C++ inside <code>ninac</code>; the library that holds the compiler exports C++ names, not a C interface a decorator could call. (2) <em>A feature gap:</em> Nina kernels use <code>Double</code>, <code>Vec4</code>, <code>Mat4</code>, <code>Stencil4</code> and <code>reduce</code>, which polyir kernels do not have (GPUs have no <code>double</code>, and polyir has no matrix type: <code>compare.poly</code> shows the replacement, a 16-float <code>uniform</code> array). (3) <em>A closed runtime:</em> the <code>Gpu</code> resource is a fixed set of C++ functions for five launch shapes, on Metal only and for the C++ target only. What exists instead is the cross-check of section 9, which keeps the two honest. A path to retiring it: (a) export a C entry point from the compiler library and load it at run time (so ninac does not depend on polyir); (b) print Nina's kernel representation as polyir source, mapping <code>Double</code> to <code>float</code>, <code>Vec4</code> to <code>vec&lt;float, 4&gt;</code>, <code>Mat4</code> to a 16-float uniform and <code>reduce</code> to the workgroup block sums; (c) give the <code>Gpu</code> resource a generic any-number-of-buffers launch like <code>metalDispatchBuffers</code>; (d) get Vulkan for free from the SPIR-V output; (e) run both generators over the four Nina kernel examples and retire the C++ one when they agree. A separate, older fault was seen on the way: Nina's own <code>examples/stencil-kernels</code> (and the standard library's <code>Grid</code>) does not compile with the current <code>nina</code> build (<code>use of undeclared identifier 'Cell'</code>, a forward-declaration problem in C++ code generation for <code>DepthWalk&lt;T&gt;</code>), so no stencil kernel is in the cross-check.</li>
    <li><strong>Waveforms and audio as part of polyir itself.</strong> <em>Done so far:</em> the Wave library (<code>ideas/audio/Wave.poly</code>: oscillators, <code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code>, <code>mix</code>, <code>fade</code>, <code>noteFrequency</code>); the Sound buffer type with any number of channels (<code>ideas/audio/Sound.poly</code>: <code>tone</code>, <code>add</code>, <code>pan</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>normalize</code>, <code>noise</code>, <code>strength</code>, WAV writing and reading, <code>resample</code>); filters (one-pole and steeper low/high-pass, band-pass, notch, bell and shelves as biquads in <code>ideas/audio/Biquad.poly</code>, echo, reverb, convolution); GPU convolution and the GPU fast Fourier transform (<code>ideas/audio/Convolve.poly</code>, <code>ideas/audio/Fft.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/SoundGpu.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/FftGpu.poly</code>); and callback-driven audio output with one source per operating system (<code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>). <em>Still to do:</em> run and fix the Linux and Windows back ends on real machines; and recording (input). These libraries are under review in <code>ideas/</code> and are not part of the standard library; their general helpers (<code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code>, <code>mix</code>) are, as the built-in Math library (section 25.6a).</li>
</ol>

