<h1 id="map">31. Source map</h1>

<p>Everything lives under <code>polyir/</code>. File names are Capitalized to match their module (a repository rule).</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Path</th><th>Role</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Polyir.intent</code></td><td>The grammar: tokens and parse rules (<code>kernel</code>, <code>uniform</code>, <code>index</code> are defined here).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Pipeline.nina</code></td><td>The pipeline. Start reading here. Entry points for the CPU and both GPU outputs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Imports.nina</code>, <code>Generics.nina</code>, <code>Coroutines.nina</code></td><td>Phases 2, 4 and 3.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Origins.nina</code></td><td>Which file each function and global came from (the markers the import step leaves), so errors say <code>file:line:col</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Containers.nina</code>, <code>src/hir/lower/LowerNew.nina</code>, <code>LowerAssoc.nina</code>, <code>LowerBuiltin.nina</code></td><td>The container headers on the heap (layouts and the field helpers), creating a container, reading and writing <code>sparse</code> and <code>mutmap</code>, <code>push</code> and <code>len</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Prelude.nina</code>, <code>Prelude.intent</code>, <code>core/stdlib/sync-prelude.py</code></td><td>The Math, GPU and Text libraries built into the language: which names, what each needs, the retry in <code>Pipeline.analyze</code>; the embedded copies of <code>Math.poly</code>, <code>Gpu.poly</code> and <code>Text.poly</code> and the script that refreshes them.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/HirNodes.nina</code>, <code>HirTypes.nina</code></td><td>The HIR data types.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/check/*</code></td><td>Phase 5: parse tree → HIR, and the 5b diagnostics (<code>CheckDiagnose.nina</code>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/Declare.nina</code>, <code>Types.nina</code>, <code>Compiler.nina</code></td><td>Phase 6 and shared lookup helpers.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/lower/*</code></td><td>Phase 7: HIR → LLVM IR.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/KernelRoles.nina</code></td><td>What each kernel parameter means.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/KernelVerify.nina</code></td><td>The kernel rules.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/KernelMsl.nina</code></td><td>HIR → Metal source.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/KernelGlsl.nina</code></td><td>HIR → Vulkan GLSL.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/KernelMath.nina</code></td><td>The shared table of math function names (C, MSL, GLSL).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/gpu/Kernels.nina</code></td><td>Finds the kernels, verifies them, and calls the emitters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>src/hir/spirv/*</code></td><td>The direct SPIR-V writer: <code>SpirvModule</code> (ids, memoized types and constants, IEEE float bits, hex output), <code>SpirvFn</code> (blocks and structured control flow), <code>SpirvTypes</code>, <code>SpirvExpr</code>, <code>SpirvStmt</code>, <code>SpirvAtomic</code> (atomics, barrier), <code>SpirvRemainder</code> (the exact float remainder), <code>SpirvKernel</code> (buffers, push constants, functions) and <code>SpirvMain</code> (the entry point).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/Metal.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/Vulkan.poly</code></td><td>The host runtimes (importable libraries written in polyir).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>cli/src/Polyircli.intent</code>, <code>Backend.nina</code></td><td>The command-line tool: flags, native build, link flags, the native-only rule.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>examples/*.poly</code></td><td>Sample programs for the language; checked against golden IR.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/*.poly</code></td><td>GPU programs that run on hardware: <code>scale</code>, <code>smooth</code> (Metal and Vulkan), <code>conformance</code> (all five kernels, both back ends), <code>applications</code> (geometry and math, plus the CPU glue), <code>raytrace</code> (a camera, a sphere, a floor and shadows).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/camera.poly</code>, <code>raycaster.poly</code></td><td>Camera poses and an orbit camera (ten pictures) and a Doom-style 2.5D view, both drawn on the GPU.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/shared/scene.poly</code>, <code>picture.poly</code></td><td>The ray tracer's kernels, camera builders (<code>buildCamera</code>, <code>poseCamera</code>) and the picture-file writer.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/texture.poly</code>, <code>shared/texture.poly</code></td><td>Textures by hand: nearest, bilinear, wrap modes, mipmaps, a textured floor, blur and edge detection.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/triangles.poly</code></td><td>A triangle rasterizer on the GPU: barycentric weights, depth, flat and smooth shading, a polygon fan, a cube and spheres.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/camera.poly</code>, <code>camera-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/camera.poly</code></td><td>The camera from scratch (chapter 14): one kernel that turns every corner of a toy room into a picture position in four plain steps, three pictures (camera moves, zoom, dolly zoom), a worked example and seven common-sense checks; checked against the CPU on Metal and Vulkan.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/handed-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/handed.poly</code></td><td>Left- versus right-handed z (section 14.9): one camera kernel told the room&rsquo;s handedness, tables of z against depth, the same room in both systems giving one picture, and a letter F mirrored when the systems are mixed up; checked against the CPU on Vulkan (GLSL and direct SPIR-V).</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/multigpu-vulkan.poly</code></td><td>One job split across several Vulkan contexts (one per GPU, or separate logical devices on a single GPU), with launches started together and boundary rows carried between the separate memories by the CPU; checked against the whole job.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/classics.poly</code>, <code>classics-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/classics.poly</code></td><td>The workbook of chapter 27: SAXPY, prefix sum, matrix multiply, Game of Life, N-body, Monte Carlo pi and a parallel sort, plus two partitioning demonstrations; checked against the CPU on Metal and Vulkan, with a timing table.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/handshake.poly</code>, <code>handshake-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/handshake.poly</code></td><td>Times each step of a CPU-to-GPU launch (find the GPU, compile, make buffers, a tiny and a big launch, read back) in Metal and in Vulkan with one shared kernel.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/pixels.poly</code>, <code>pixels-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/pixels.poly</code></td><td>Images as signals (Metal and Vulkan hosts, shared kernels and checks): channel, quantize, decimate, boxAverage, enlargeNearest and enlargeBilinear kernels, a table of pixel numbers, and checks that the GPU equals the CPU and that averaging keeps the mean.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/colour.poly</code>, <code>colour-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/colour.poly</code></td><td>Colour as a signal on the GPU: luma and chroma views, hue rotation, saturation scaling, chroma-only and luma-only blur, luma sharpening, plus a CPU vectorscope. The kernels are shared; one host program runs them through Metal and one through Vulkan. Each checks GPU against CPU and three properties of the maths.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/ntsc.poly</code></td><td>An old NTSC television on the GPU: RGB to YIQ, the colour subcarrier, then a 4-tap decoder with tint and colour knobs; saves five pictures and checks GPU against CPU and a round trip.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/dsp.poly</code></td><td>Signals: a 1D and 2D discrete Fourier transform on the GPU, aliasing, filter responses.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/reduce.poly</code>, <code>reduce-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/reduce.poly</code></td><td>Multi-pass tree reductions (sum and maximum) on Metal and Vulkan.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/flatten.poly</code>, <code>shared/matrix.poly</code></td><td>The whole 3D-to-2D chain (model, view, projection, divide, viewport) on the GPU, checked against the plain rule; 4 &times; 4 matrix helpers.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/lighting.poly</code>, <code>shared/lighting.poly</code></td><td>Colour output, ambient/diffuse/specular lighting, shadows, gamma, normal visualization, bumps and a mirror.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/scatter.poly</code>, <code>scatter-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/scatter.poly</code></td><td><code>writable</code> arrays: scatter, atomic and racy histograms, a splatted galaxy.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/shapes.poly</code>, <code>shapes-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/shapes.poly</code></td><td>Kernel shapes: nine buffers in one launch, grid kernels (a plasma and a vignette), and the float, minimum and maximum atomics.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/reduce1.poly</code>, <code>reduce1-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/reduce1.poly</code></td><td>Workgroup memory and barriers: block sums and maxima in three launches, and the whole sum in one launch with an atomic float add; a timing table against the pair-adding tree.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/zbuffer.poly</code>, <code>zbuffer-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/zbuffer.poly</code></td><td>A z-buffer by scatter: one thread per triangle claiming pixels with <code>atomicMin</code> on a packed (depth, triangle) key, then a pass per pixel.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>stdlib/Gpu.poly</code></td><td>The CPU side of the GPU functions (atomics, <code>groupSize</code>, <code>localIndex</code>, <code>groupIndex</code>, <code>barrier</code>), built into the language like the Wave library.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>stdlib/Math.poly</code></td><td>The Math library: <code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code> and <code>mix</code> on floats, built into the language (section 25.6a); runs on the CPU and in GPU kernels.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/queens.poly</code>, <code>queens-vulkan.poly</code>, <code>shared/queens.poly</code></td><td>N-queens: counting solutions on the GPU with per-thread stacks and a GPU reduction, with a CPU timing table.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio/Sound.poly</code>, <code>ideas/audio/Biquad.poly</code>, <code>ideas/audio/Resample.poly</code></td><td>The Sound buffer type (channels and frames, tone, mix, pan, scale, normalize, noise, strength, WAV writing and reading, resampling, one-pole filters, echo, reverb, convolution), the biquad filters (low/high-pass, band-pass, notch, bell, shelves, exact response) and the windowed-sinc resampler.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code>, <code>ideas/audio-output/Stream.poly</code>, <code>macos.poly</code>, <code>linux.poly</code>, <code>windows.poly</code></td><td>Audio output: the facade, the platform-independent stream (fill function, time limit, volume, playing a Sound), and one back end per operating system chosen by <code>import "audio/{os}.poly"</code> (macOS run and tested; Linux and Windows untested).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio-output/examples/compose.poly</code>, <code>studio.poly</code>, <code>live.poly</code></td><td>A composed piece; the stereo/WAV/resampling/equalizer tour with three response curves; and a live stream checked on a real audio device at volume 0.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio/Convolve.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/SoundGpu.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/examples/convolve.poly</code>, <code>convolve-vulkan.poly</code></td><td>Convolution as a GPU kernel (one thread per output sample), a helper that runs it on a <code>Sound</code>, and a reverb example with a CPU-versus-GPU timing table.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio/Fft.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/FftGpu.poly</code>, <code>ideas/gpu/examples/fft.poly</code></td><td>The fast Fourier transform as GPU kernels (Stockham stages, batches, windowed framing, spectrogram pixels, complex multiply), its host driver, and an example that checks it, times it and draws a spectrogram.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/filters.poly</code></td><td>Filters seen through the GPU's Fourier transform: two tones through a low-pass and a high-pass, filter responses measured with a click, an echo and a reverb.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/audio/Wave.poly</code></td><td>The Wave library (under review in <code>ideas/</code>): oscillators (<code>sine</code>, <code>square</code>, <code>sawtooth</code>, <code>triangle</code>, <code>noise</code>) and helpers (<code>fade</code>, <code>noteFrequency</code>), with <code>sign</code>, <code>fract</code>, <code>clamp</code> and <code>mix</code> from the built-in <code>core/stdlib/Math.poly</code>; imported, runs on the CPU and in GPU kernels.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/audio.poly</code>, <code>shared/audio.poly</code>, <code>shared/spectrum.poly</code>, <code>shared/plot.poly</code>, <code>ideas/audio-output/Audio.poly</code></td><td>Sound: synthesis kernels (sine sums, sign of a sine, harmonic series, a melody), the GPU Fourier transform, waveform plots, and the AudioQueue playback runtime (WAV writing now lives in the Sound library).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ideas/gpu/examples/shared/kernels.poly</code></td><td>The kernel set the two conformance programs share, and the float comparison <code>close</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>tests/</code></td><td>Unit tests (<code>src/Main.nina</code>), the driver for tests written in polyir (<code>src/FileTests.nina</code>, <code>polyir/*Tests.poly</code>, <code>polyir/Check.poly</code>), WAV fixtures (<code>fixtures/</code>, made by <code>make-fixtures.py</code>), golden IR, performance baseline, the diagnostics fixtures (<code>fixtures/diagnostics/</code>, checked by <code>src/DiagnosticChecks.nina</code>), <code>gpu.sh</code>, <code>vulkan.sh</code>, and the cross-check with Nina's own GPU path (<code>nina-kernels/</code>, <code>nina-kernels.sh</code>).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>docs/</code></td><td>This guide: <code>guide.html.in</code> (source), <code>build.py</code> (makes the PDF and numbers the figures in page order), <code>snippets/</code> (compiler-generated listings, program output and pictures; <code>regenerate.sh</code> rebuilds them from the real programs).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Rebuilding this PDF</h2>
<pre><code>core/docs/language/snippets/regenerate.sh   # rebuilds the program output, pictures and diagrams from the real programs
python3 core/docs/language/build.py          # needs Google Chrome; writes core/docs/language/polyir-guide.pdf</code></pre>
<p>Code listings use <code>&#123;&#123;def:file:name&#125;&#125;</code> (one function, found by name) so they stay true when files change; refresh
the Metal and GLSL listings in <code>docs/snippets/</code> with <code>polyir --emit-msl</code> / <code>--emit-glsl</code> when the emitters change. Figures are numbered
automatically in page order: a caption written <code>Figure @name.</code> becomes the next number and every mention is updated.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ 14 -->
<h1 id="glossary">32. Glossary</h1>
<table>
  <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  <tr><td>AST / parse tree</td><td>The program as a tree of syntax pieces, straight from the grammar.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Arithmetic intensity</td><td>How much computation is done per byte moved from memory; high intensity favours the GPU.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Buffer</td><td>A block of memory the GPU can read or write.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Divergence</td><td>Threads of one SIMD group taking different branches; the group runs both paths, so it is slower.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bit depth</td><td>How many different values one stored number can have: 8 bits gives 256 levels. Rounding to those levels is quantization.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Channel</td><td>One of the numbers stored at each pixel: red, green, blue (and sometimes alpha). Each channel alone is a grey picture.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pixel / sample</td><td>One measurement of light at one position of a picture; a point in the data, though a screen draws it as a small square.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Resolution</td><td>The number of pixels in a picture (width by height); finer detail than two pixels per repeat cannot be kept.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Composite signal</td><td>Brightness and colour carried together in one wave per scan line, as in analogue NTSC television (sections 24.12 and 24.13).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Colour burst</td><td>A few cycles of the pure colour carrier sent after each line&rsquo;s sync pulse; the receiver locks to it so it can measure every hue against a known phase.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Interlacing</td><td>Drawing a picture as two fields, the odd lines then the even lines, so the screen refreshes twice as often as whole pictures are sent.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Luma / chroma</td><td>The brightness part of a colour signal (Y) and the colour part (here I and Q); a black-and-white set uses luma only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vectorscope</td><td>A plot of every pixel at its colour-plane position (I across, Q up): angle is hue, distance from the centre is saturation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cellular automaton</td><td>A grid of cells that all follow one simple rule using only their neighbours; the Game of Life is the best-known (section 27.5).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Double buffering</td><td>Reading the previous step from one array and writing the next step to another, then swapping them, so no element sees a half-updated neighbour.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grid computing</td><td>Using many networked computers as one machine for a big job. Not to be confused with the &ldquo;grid&rdquo; of threads in one GPU launch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Halo (ghost) cells</td><td>Copies of the boundary rows of a neighbouring part, kept so a part can compute its own edge; they must be refreshed whenever the neighbour changes (section 27.9).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monte Carlo method</td><td>Answering a question by running many random experiments and counting (section 27.7).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Partitioning</td><td>Dividing a problem&rsquo;s data or work into parts that different workers handle at the same time.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prefix sum (scan)</td><td>Replacing each number in a list by the total of all numbers up to it; a running total (section 27.3).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SAXPY</td><td>Compute <em>a</em> &times; <em>x</em> + <em>y</em> for every element of two lists; the &ldquo;hello world&rdquo; of GPU computing (section 27.2).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stencil</td><td>A calculation in which each element is computed from its neighbours, such as a blur or one step of the Game of Life.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Depth cue</td><td>A clue in a flat picture from which the brain works out distance: size, converging lines, overlap, shading, haze, motion and the difference between the two eyes (section 14.2).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Retina</td><td>The light-sensitive sheet at the back of the eye; it records a flat picture, and the brain adds the depth (section 14.3).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ray</td><td>A straight line from the eye in one direction; each pixel records the light arriving along one ray, and says nothing about the distance (sections 14.3 and 14.4).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stereo vision</td><td>Judging distance from the small difference between the pictures of two eyes a few centimetres apart; useful for things within a few metres (section 14.3).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Depth (of a point)</td><td>How far in front of the camera a point is, measured along the way the camera looks; farther points are drawn smaller, and nearer ones hide farther ones (section 14.8).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dolly zoom</td><td>Moving the camera away while narrowing the lens so a subject keeps its size and the background appears to compress; the &ldquo;Vertigo effect&rdquo; (section 14.12).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Field of view</td><td>How many degrees of the world, from the top of the picture to the bottom, the camera sees; a narrow one magnifies (section 14.12).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Parallax</td><td>Near things shift more than far things when the camera moves sideways; a main clue to depth (section 14.13).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pan, tilt, roll</td><td>Turning the camera left or right, up or down, or leaning it over: yaw, pitch and roll (section 14.13).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Picture plane</td><td>The flat rectangle in front of the virtual camera&rsquo;s eye through which each point is seen; where the line from the eye to a point crosses it is where the point appears (section 14.6).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pinhole camera</td><td>A dark box with a tiny hole: each point of the scene sends one ray through the hole to one spot of the picture. The virtual camera works the same way (section 14.6).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Command buffer</td><td>A recorded list of GPU commands (use this pipeline, bind these buffers, run this many threads) that is submitted to a queue and run later (section 5.6).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Driver</td><td>The software from the GPU&rsquo;s maker (or Apple) that translates kernels into the GPU&rsquo;s own instructions and carries out commands.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pipeline</td><td>A kernel translated for one particular GPU and ready to run; built once, used for many launches.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Queue</td><td>Where submitted command buffers wait, in order, for the GPU to take them.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Validation layer</td><td>An optional Vulkan component that checks your calls for mistakes while you develop; Vulkan drivers themselves check very little.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Descriptor set</td><td>Vulkan's table that says which buffer sits at which binding for a dispatch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dispatch</td><td>Starting a kernel for a given number of elements.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Element</td><td>One item of the data a kernel processes; the unit of work of one thread.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>FFI / <code>extern func</code></td><td>Calling a function written in another language (here, C interfaces of the OS).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GLSL</td><td>OpenGL Shading Language; the text from which SPIR-V is made.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grid</td><td>All the threads of one kernel launch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Host</td><td>The CPU-side program that drives the GPU.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>HIR</td><td>High-level intermediate representation: the type-checked program all back ends read.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matrix</td><td>A grid of numbers that transforms vectors; its columns are where the axes go.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Homogeneous coordinates</td><td>Writing a point as (x, y, z, 1) so that a 4&times;4 matrix can move it as well as turn it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>z-axis</td><td>The room&rsquo;s third coordinate direction, at right angles to x and y. In a right-handed system with x right and y up it points toward the viewer, so a larger z is nearer; in a left-handed one it points away (section 14.9).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Handedness</td><td>Whether z points toward you (right-handed) or away (left-handed) when x is right and y is up.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Dot product</td><td>Multiply matching slots and add; equals |a||b|cos(angle), so it measures alignment.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cross product</td><td>A vector perpendicular to two others, whose length is the area they span.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Yaw, pitch, roll</td><td>Turning left/right, up/down, and sideways-tilt (shaking, nodding, ear-to-shoulder).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Parallax</td><td>Near things shifting more than far things when the camera moves.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>View matrix</td><td>The inverse of the camera's placement: it turns world coordinates into what the camera sees.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Orbit camera</td><td>A camera that circles a target at a distance, steered by yaw and pitch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ray casting (2.5D)</td><td>Drawing a flat map as a 3D-looking view by shooting one ray per screen column.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normal</td><td>A unit vector pointing straight out of a surface; the dot product with the light direction gives diffuse brightness.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reduction</td><td>Combining many values into one (a sum, a maximum).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Kernel</td><td>The small program a GPU runs once per element.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>LLVM IR</td><td>The typed, low-level instruction language LLVM compiles to machine code.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Metal / MSL</td><td>Apple's GPU API and its shading language.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monomorphization</td><td>Generating one concrete copy of generic code for each type it is used with.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>MoltenVK</td><td>A library that implements Vulkan on top of Metal.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normalize</td><td>Divide a vector by its length so the length becomes 1.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Phi node</td><td>An LLVM instruction that picks a value depending on which block jumped here; polyir labels with parameters express it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Push constants</td><td>A few bytes sent with a Vulkan dispatch, used here for uniform values and the element count.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SPIR-V</td><td>The binary shader format Vulkan loads.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>SIMD group</td><td>A set of about 32 threads that execute the same instruction in lock step (a &ldquo;warp&rdquo; on NVIDIA).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stencil</td><td>A computation where each output depends on neighbouring inputs.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Storage buffer</td><td>Vulkan's name for a general read/write array in GPU memory.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Threadgroup</td><td>A team of threads (for example 256) that can share fast memory and synchronise; a grid is made of threadgroups.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ray tracing</td><td>Drawing a picture by following a ray from the eye through each pixel to find what it hits and how it is lit.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Texture / texel</td><td>An image used as a sticker (or lookup table) on a surface; a texel is one square of it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sampling, filter</td><td>Asking a texture for its colour at a fractional address (u, v); nearest and bilinear are the two basic ways to answer.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mipmap</td><td>A set of half-size, quarter-size, ... copies of a texture so far-away surfaces read an already-averaged copy.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Aliasing</td><td>Flickering or jagged patterns from sampling a detailed image too coarsely.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Convolution</td><td>Sliding a small grid of weights over an image and summing the weighted neighbours (blur, edges).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vertex</td><td>A corner point of a shape, carrying its position, normal and texture address.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Mesh</td><td>A surface made of many triangles that share corners (a vertex list plus an index list).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rasterization</td><td>Finding which pixels a flat triangle covers and filling them (as opposed to ray tracing).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Barycentric coordinates</td><td>Three weights adding to 1 that say how much of each triangle corner a point is made of; used to blend any corner data.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Depth buffer</td><td>A per-pixel memory of the nearest distance drawn so far, so the nearest shape wins whatever the drawing order.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Projection</td><td>Flattening 3D points onto the 2D screen: orthographic drops depth, perspective divides by it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Frustum</td><td>The camera's visible volume, a pyramid with its tip cut off.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Signal / sample</td><td>A list of numbers measured at evenly spaced positions (time, or x, y, z in a picture or volume); one number is a sample (a pixel, a voxel).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Spectrum / DFT</td><td>Which wave speeds a signal contains and how strongly; the discrete Fourier transform computes it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Nyquist limit</td><td>A signal sampled S times per unit can only hold waves slower than S/2; faster ones alias to slower ones.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Basis, determinant, eigenvector</td><td>Linear algebra words: perpendicular axes everything is built from; the factor by which a matrix scales area; a direction a matrix only stretches.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Voxel</td><td>A pixel of a 3D grid (a volume pixel).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scatter, atomic</td><td>Writing a value to a computed position; an atomic operation (such as <code>atomicAdd</code>) updates a memory slot as one indivisible step, so simultaneous threads never lose an update.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Normal vector</td><td>A unit-length arrow perpendicular to a surface at a point; the answer to &ldquo;which way is this surface facing?&rdquo;</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gamma / sRGB</td><td>The squashing curve (about brightness<sup>1/2.2</sup>) used to store brightness in files and screens; do lighting maths on linear light and encode last.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Diffuse, specular, ambient</td><td>The three classic lighting terms: light scattered by a rough surface (N&middot;L), the shiny glint, and a little light from everywhere.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Clip space, NDC, w</td><td>After the projection matrix the fourth number w holds the distance in front of the camera; dividing by it (the perspective divide) gives normalized device coordinates between &minus;1 and 1.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Backtracking</td><td>A depth-first search that extends a partial answer step by step and backs up when stuck; its subtrees are independent, so it parallelizes.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sine, sign</td><td>Sine is the smooth wave (height of a point circling steadily); the sign function returns &minus;1, 0 or +1 for a negative, zero or positive number. The sign of a sine is a square wave.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Frequency, amplitude, phase</td><td>How many cycles per second (pitch), how tall the wave is (loudness), and where in its cycle it starts.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Harmonic, timbre</td><td>A harmonic is a whole-number multiple of a base frequency; the mix of harmonics gives an instrument its timbre (tone colour).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Beats</td><td>The slow swelling and fading heard when two nearly equal frequencies are added; its rate is the difference of the two frequencies.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Envelope</td><td>A slow curve multiplied onto a sound to shape its volume over time (attack, decay, sustain, release).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Audio buffer</td><td>A block of samples handed to the sound hardware in advance; its size sets latency against safety from gaps.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Filter, low-pass, high-pass</td><td>A rule making each output sample from nearby input samples; a low-pass keeps slow waves and softens fast ones, a high-pass the reverse.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Impulse, frequency response</td><td>A single click, which contains every frequency equally; the spectrum of a filter's answer to a click is its response, the scale factor it applies to each frequency.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>FFT, butterfly, twiddle factor</td><td>The fast Fourier transform: log<sub>2</sub> N stages of simple two-input operations (butterflies using rotations called twiddle factors) that give the same result as the plain transform in about N log N steps.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Spectrogram</td><td>A picture of a sound's frequencies over time: FFTs of short overlapping windowed slices, drawn as brightness (time across, frequency up).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Channel, frame, interleaved</td><td>A channel is one stream of samples (left, right, ...); a frame is one sample for every channel at one instant; interleaved means the frames are stored one after another: left, right, left, right, ...</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Pan law</td><td>The rule that sets the left and right volumes for a sound placed between the speakers; the constant-power law (left = cos &theta;, right = sin &theta;) keeps the total loudness steady.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Resampling</td><td>Changing a sound's sample rate by rebuilding the wave between the samples (with sinc curves) and measuring it at the new instants; going to a lower rate must also low-pass filter or it aliases.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sinc, window</td><td>sinc(x) = sin(&pi;x)/(&pi;x), the curve that is 1 at 0 and 0 at every other whole number, used to rebuild a wave from its samples; a window fades it smoothly to zero so a finite sum can be used.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Biquad, Q</td><td>A filter whose output is a weighted sum of the two previous inputs, the current one and the two previous outputs (five numbers); Q says how narrow or resonant it is.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Equalizer, shelf, bell, notch</td><td>Filters that change the volume of regions of the spectrum: a shelf lifts or cuts everything below or above a corner (bass and treble knobs), a bell one band, a notch removes a narrow band.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Decibel (dB)</td><td>A logarithmic loudness unit: +6 dB doubles the amplitude, &minus;6 dB halves it, +20 dB is ten times.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Callback, fill function, latency</td><td>A function the system calls when it needs something; here, when the sound hardware needs more samples. The delay between making a sample and hearing it is the latency.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Built-in (prelude)</td><td>Library code the compiler adds on its own when a program calls it without defining or importing it; here the Math, GPU and Text functions.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Workgroup, workgroup memory, barrier</td><td>A team of threads (here 256) that share a small fast memory and can wait for each other at a barrier; the grid is made of workgroups. (Metal says threadgroup.)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Grid (of threads)</td><td>A kernel launched over width &times; height threads, one per cell of a picture; a kernel with two <code>index</code> parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Compare-and-swap</td><td>An atomic instruction that writes a new value into a memory slot only if the slot still holds what the thread last read; retried in a loop it builds any atomic update, such as adding a float.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gather and scatter</td><td>Gather: each thread reads from many places and writes one result (a thread per pixel). Scatter: each thread writes to many places (a thread per triangle), so conflicting writes must be settled, for example with <code>atomicMin</code>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Short-circuit</td><td>Evaluating the right side of <code>&amp;&amp;</code> or <code>||</code> only when the left side has not already decided the answer.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Thread</td><td>One running copy of a kernel; thread <em>n</em> handles element <em>n</em>.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Unified memory</td><td>CPU and GPU share one memory pool (Apple Silicon), so a buffer needs no copying between them.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Uniform</td><td>A value (or read-only array) shared by all threads of a dispatch.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Vulkan</td><td>The cross-vendor GPU API.</td></tr>
</table>

</body>
</html>
